/* ============================================================
   THESMALLBOOK, 💀 GRAVEYARD DEEP CONTENT (auto-generated)
   Every grave's full story, in two acts: HOW IT STARTED (origin)
   and THE FALL. Built from the 308 hand-authored case-study pages.
   window.GRAVE_DEEP[id] = { origin, fall }
   ============================================================ */
window.GRAVE_DEEP = {"thomas-cook":{"origin":"Thomas Cook invented the package holiday in 1841, churches chartering trains became a global empire of planes, hotels and high-street stores.","fall":"But it entered the internet age dragging 560 physical stores and £1.7B of debt from misfired mergers, while Booking.com and budget airlines unbundled its entire reason to exist. When a final rescue deal collapsed in September 2019, the 178-year-old firm ceased trading at 2 AM; 600,000 tourists were abroad mid-holiday, triggering the largest peacetime repatriation in British history."},"daewoo-empire":{"origin":"Kim Woo-choong built Daewoo from a $10,000 textile trader into Korea's second-largest conglomerate, cars, ships, electronics, 320,000 employees, on one motto: 'The world is wide, and there is much to do.' His strategy was pure expansion: borrow, build, enter every market, never retreat.","fall":"When the 1997 Asian crisis hit, other chaebols cut back; Kim ACCELERATED, adding 14 new companies. Two years later Daewoo drowned under $80B of debt with $30B of hidden accounting fraud. Kim fled abroad for six years; the empire was dissolved."},"commodore-64":{"origin":"The Commodore 64 remains the best-selling single computer model in history, 12.5M+ units. Commodore OWNED the home-computer boom, even owned its own chip factory.","fall":"Then management turned every advantage into ash: they price-warred their own products into zero margin, fired the engineers behind their hits, shipped the revolutionary Amiga with almost no marketing budget, and burned cash on failed clones instead. While they coasted on the aging 64, the PC standard ate the future. Bankruptcy came in 1994, the market leader of 1984 didn't survive one platform cycle."},"palm-webos":{"origin":"Palm put a computer in your pocket before Apple did, the Pilot owned the 90s, the Treo pioneered the smartphone.","fall":"But years of ownership musical chairs (3Com, spin-offs, split OS companies) left it frozen while the iPhone landed. Palm's CEO famously scoffed: 'PC guys are not going to just figure this out.' The comeback attempt, 2009's webOS, genuinely ahead of its time with card multitasking, arrived underfunded, on weak hardware, exclusive to a struggling carrier. HP bought Palm for $1.2B, killed webOS within 49 days of the TouchPad launch."},"skype-fall":{"origin":"Skype WAS video calling, a verb, 300M users, bought by Microsoft for $8.5B in 2011.","fall":"Then a decade of neglect: endless confusing redesigns, sluggish apps, missed mobile-first behavior. When COVID handed video calling the greatest demand shock in history, the world chose Zoom, simpler, faster, just worked, while Skype's own parent pushed Teams instead. Microsoft officially shut Skype down in May 2025: the biggest brand in the category's history didn't even get to attend its market's biggest moment."},"tupperware-party":{"origin":"Tupperware didn't sell containers; it sold the Tupperware Party, a 1950s social-selling revolution that empowered a generation of women entrepreneurs.","fall":"But the model WAS the moat, and the moat expired: parties died with changing social lives, the company barely entered retail or e-commerce until 2022 (Target, decades too late), and cheaper competitors filled Amazon. Sales fell for years; debt piled; in September 2024 the icon filed for bankruptcy, still famous, still in every kitchen, still unable to sell you anything the way you actually buy."},"red-lobster-shrimp":{"origin":"In 1968, 34-year-old Georgia restaurateur Bill Darden opened the first Red Lobster in Lakeland, Florida, fresh, affordable seafood for inland America, a novelty at the time. General Mills bought the young chain in 1970 and scaled it into America's biggest casual-seafood empire: 700+ restaurants at peak.","fall":"Red Lobster's occasional '$20 Endless Shrimp' was a beloved traffic gimmick. In 2023 its owner, Thai Union, which happened to be a giant SHRIMP SUPPLIER, made it permanent. Customers treated it as a professional sport; the chain lost $11M in one quarter on the deal while its supplier-owner sold it all the shrimp. It was the final absurdity atop years of private-equity damage: a sale-leaseback had sold all the restaurants' real estate, saddling every location with rent forever. Bankruptcy filed May 2024."},"bed-bath-beyond":{"origin":"In 1971, Warren Eisenberg and Leonard Feinstein opened Bed 'n Bath in Springfield, New Jersey, a specialty shop for a category department stores treated as filler. They rode the suburban housing boom to 300+ superstores, and by the 2000s every American wedding registry ran through Bed Bath & Beyond.","fall":"The coupon-famous retailer faced Amazon like everyone else, but chose a spectacular way to die: between 2004 and 2021 it spent $11.8 BILLION buying back its own stock, more than its entire final market value many times over, while stores aged and e-commerce stayed an afterthought. A final act of chaos, replacing beloved brands with unready private labels mid-supply-crisis, emptied shelves and drove away loyalists. It filed for bankruptcy in 2023, having spent the money that could have funded ten reinventions on polishing a sinking share price."},"vice-media":{"origin":"Vice was the future of media, edgy documentaries, a generation's trust, a $5.7B valuation with Disney and Fox money.","fall":"But the valuation was priced on a fantasy: that cool travels wherever Facebook's algorithm points. Vice (like BuzzFeed News beside it) built its economics on platform reach it didn't control; when algorithms pivoted to video, then away from news entirely, the traffic, and the ad model, evaporated. Expensive bureaus, celebrity executives and swagger remained. Bankruptcy in 2023, sold for ~$350M; by 2024 Vice.com stopped publishing altogether."},"23andme-dna":{"origin":"23andMe made DNA testing a holiday gift, 15M people spat in tubes.","fall":"But a spit kit is a one-time purchase: once you know your ancestry, you're done, and the company never found a repeatable second product. Drug-discovery bets burned cash slowly; a 2023 data breach exposing millions of profiles burned trust quickly. The stock fell from $6B to penny-stock; the entire board resigned in 2024; bankruptcy followed in 2025, with the most intimate database ever assembled listed as an asset for sale, exactly what privacy critics had warned all along."},"lordstown-truck":{"origin":"In 2017, Tesla-star Steve Burns bought General Motors' freshly closed Lordstown, Ohio plant for $20 million and promised to refill it with electric pickup trucks. The Endurance launched through a 2020 SPAC merger that briefly valued the dream in the billions, before a single truck had shipped.","fall":"Lordstown bought a shuttered GM plant to build electric trucks and rode the 2020 SPAC mania to a $1.6B valuation, waving '100,000 pre-orders' at investors. A short-seller report found the truth: the 'orders' were non-binding letters of intent, some from companies with no fleets, one from an office with two employees. The CEO resigned, the SEC circled, a prototype burned in testing, and the company produced only a token handful of trucks before 2023 bankruptcy. The factory ended up sold... to Foxconn."},"clubhouse-fade":{"origin":"In lockdown 2021, Clubhouse was THE app: invite-only audio rooms with Elon and Zuckerberg dropping in, 10M weekly users, a $4B valuation from a16z.","fall":"But the magic was borrowed from the pandemic: when offices and bars reopened, hours-long audio hangouts collapsed, while Twitter Spaces, Spotify and Discord cloned the feature in months. Clubhouse had scarcity theater but no retention engine, no recordings, no discovery, no reason to return. By 2023 it laid off half its staff and pivoted; the world barely noticed."},"frank-javice":{"origin":"Charlie Javice, Forbes 30 Under 30, built Frank, a startup simplifying student financial aid, and sold it to JPMorgan for $175M on the claim of 4.25 million users.","fall":"When the bank emailed a test batch, delivery rates were catastrophic: prosecutors showed Javice had paid a data scientist $18,000 to fabricate a synthetic list of ~4 million fake students after an employee refused. Real users: under 300,000. JPMorgan sued; in 2025 she was convicted of fraud and sentenced to over 7 years."},"ozy-media":{"origin":"In 2013, TV host Carlos Watson and Samir Rao launched Ozy, a digital magazine about 'the New and the Next', backed by Goldman Sachs and Laurene Powell Jobs. It collected Emmy awards, threw festivals with world leaders, and claimed tens of millions of readers.","fall":"Ozy Media raised $83M as a buzzy digital-media darling, boasting 50M monthly readers and hit shows. The empire's numbers were heavily inflated, traffic bought and exaggerated, awards self-invented ('we were told we'd have won'). It unraveled with one surreal scene: on a due-diligence call with Goldman Sachs about a $40M investment, Ozy's co-founder COO impersonated a YouTube executive, with a voice-altering app, to vouch for Ozy's fake YouTube numbers. Goldman called YouTube; the New York Times called everyone. Ozy collapsed within days; the founders were later convicted and charged."},"cambridge-analytica":{"origin":"A cheerful personality quiz, 'thisisyourdigitallife', was taken by 270,000 Facebook users. Through Facebook's friends-API, it quietly harvested data on up to 87 MILLION people, which Cambridge Analytica turned into psychographic voter-targeting products sold to political campaigns.","fall":"When whistleblower Christopher Wylie went public in 2018, the blast radius was historic: CA shut down within months, Facebook took a $5B FTC fine and a permanent trust wound, and 'data privacy' went from nerd topic to dinner-table fear."},"gomechanic-india":{"origin":"In 2016, five friends in Gurgaon led by Amit Bhasin founded GoMechanic to organise India's chaotic car-repair market: transparent prices and an app stitched to a network of partner garages. Sequoia-backed, it grew to 100+ cities through the pandemic on asset-light economics, on paper, at least.","fall":"GoMechanic, Sequoia-backed, on the road to unicorn status, was India's largest car-servicing startup. During due diligence for a new round, investors found the engine was painted on: revenues inflated with fake garages and bogus receivables. Founder Amit Bhasin posted a public confession, 'we got carried away... grave errors in judgment', 70% of the 1,000+ staff were fired the same week, and the company sold in a distress deal for a fraction of its funding. The confession's most honest line: they chased 'growth at all costs.'"},"bharatpe-grover":{"origin":"Ashneer Grover co-built BharatPe into a $2.8B fintech and became a household name as Shark Tank India's bluntest judge.","fall":"Then a leaked audio surfaced of him abusing a bank employee over IPO shares; his defiance ('I'm not apologizing') turned a PR problem into a board war. A governance review alleged family-run expense fraud, luxury trips and personal bills through fake vendors; his wife (head of controls) was implicated; he resigned minutes before the board could fire him, then live-tweeted the war. Criminal cases followed. The company survived; his throne didn't."},"koo-app":{"origin":"Koo, the yellow bird, was India's answer to Twitter: government ministers onboarded during a 2021 standoff with Twitter, 60M+ downloads, Tiger Global money at a $270M valuation.","fall":"But its growth spiked only when Twitter had political trouble; between controversies, users drifted back, celebrities, cricketers and news lived where the audience already was. Multi-language features were genuinely good; the network effect never came. When funding winter hit and a sale to Dailyhunt collapsed, Koo shut down in July 2024 with a farewell note: 'the little yellow bird says goodbye.'"},"tinyowl-food":{"origin":"In 2014, five IIT Bombay graduates launched TinyOwl in Mumbai, real menus, ordered food in a few taps, and became one of India's first hyperlocal stars, raising $30M+ and hiring hundreds. Then came the viral 2015 layoffs, managers escorting engineers out photographed mid-negotiation, and the startup became the era's cautionary image.","fall":"TinyOwl was Zomato-before-Zomato-delivered: a slick food-ordering app that raised $27M and expanded to 11 cities in months. Unit economics were an afterthought, deep discounts bought orders that lost money each time, and city launches multiplied the bleeding. The layoffs became legend for the wrong reason: at the Pune office, terminated employees demanding dues locked co-founder Gaurav Choudhary in the office for two days. After more rounds of cuts, the remains were merged into Roadrunnr and vanished. Swiggy, founded a year later, obsessed with delivery economics, took the market."},"wamu-2008":{"origin":"WaMu wanted to be the Walmart of banking, home loans for everyone, especially the everyone other banks refused. Its option-ARM mortgages let borrowers pay LESS than the interest owed, with the difference quietly added to their debt; internal risk officers who objected were sidelined while sales staff won trips to Hawaii.","fall":"When housing turned, depositors pulled $16.7B in nine days, and regulators seized the 119-year-old bank overnight, selling it to JPMorgan for $1.9B, less than one percent of its peak value."},"general-magic":{"origin":"General Magic, a legendary Apple spin-off whose team included future iPod father Tony Fadell and Android creator Andy Rubin, built a handheld touchscreen communicator with apps, e-commerce, and downloadable software... in 1994. The press called it the most important company in Silicon Valley.","fall":"But the infrastructure its magic needed didn't exist: no mobile internet, no cheap touchscreens, batteries from the stone age, and partners (Sony, Motorola, AT&T) each pulling the vision apart. It sold almost nothing, faded through the 90s, and closed in 2002, five years before its alumni built the exact same dream at Apple, on time."},"pebble-watch":{"origin":"Pebble invented the modern smartwatch market, its 2012 Kickstarter raised $10.3M (the platform's record), and its 2015 campaign raised $20.3M more. Fans adored it.","fall":"Then Apple Watch and Android Wear arrived with billion-dollar budgets, and Pebble responded by expanding: more models, more inventory, more headcount, financed by debt against holiday sales that missed. It reportedly turned down a ~$740M acquisition offer from Citizen in 2015; eighteen months later it sold its software scraps to Fitbit for ~$23M and shut down, refunding backers pennies."},"micromax-fall":{"origin":"By 2014 Micromax was India's largest phone brand, even passing Samsung, by nailing one playbook: cheap feature-rich 3G phones through offline dealers.","fall":"Then September 2016 happened: Reliance Jio launched free 4G data for everyone. Overnight, 4G went from luxury to mandatory, and Micromax's entire pipeline and inventory was 3G. Chinese brands (Xiaomi, Oppo, Vivo) flooded in with 4G phones, online-first sales, and faster refresh cycles. Micromax, caught mid-stride with dealer shelves full of obsolete stock, fell from #1 to under 1% market share within three years."},"hike-messenger":{"origin":"Hike was India's unicorn messenger, $1.4B valuation, backed by SoftBank and Tencent, founded by Kavin Bharti Mittal with telecom royalty behind it. It threw everything at beating WhatsApp: stickers in Indian languages, hidden chats, news, payments, games, a 'super app' before the term was cool.","fall":"But WhatsApp was already every Indian's default, free, and pre-installed in the family group. Network effects don't care about features: your messenger is where your people are. Hike pivoted repeatedly, shrank, and in January 2021 shut the messenger entirely."},"archegos-hwang":{"origin":"Bill Hwang quietly built one of history's great fortunes, $20B, using total return swaps so no single bank could see his full position. Five banks each lent him billions against the SAME concentrated bets on a handful of stocks, each thinking they were his main lender.","fall":"When ViacomCBS dipped, margin calls hit all banks at once, they raced to dump identical positions, and Archegos lost $20B in two days, possibly the fastest personal wealth destruction ever. Credit Suisse alone ate $5.5B."},"svb-bankrun":{"origin":"In 1983, two bankers founded Silicon Valley Bank on a simple insight big banks ignored: startups needed a bank that spoke venture. Deposit your funding round, borrow against your promise, for forty years SVB became the financial plumbing of Silicon Valley, holding deposits from nearly half of all US venture-backed companies.","fall":"SVB banked half of America's startups. Flooded with deposits, it parked billions in long-term bonds paying ~1.6%, safe, unless interest rates rose. Rates rose faster than any time in decades. The bonds sank; SVB announced a loss and a capital raise on the same day its risk was trending on Twitter. Its depositors, the most online, group-chat-connected customers on Earth, pulled $42B in ONE day. Regulators seized the 40-year-old bank before Friday lunch."},"credit-suisse-end":{"origin":"Credit Suisse survived two world wars but not its own culture. Archegos ($5.5B), Greensill ($10B frozen), spying scandals, a money-laundering conviction, revolving-door CEOs, each hit was 'manageable,' so nothing fundamentally changed. By 2023 clients were quietly pulling billions per week.","fall":"When one major shareholder said 'absolutely not' to more capital on live TV, the run went terminal. The Swiss government forced a weekend fire-sale to rival UBS for scraps."},"melvin-gamestop":{"origin":"In 2014, star trader Gabe Plotkin, one of Steve Cohen's best lieutenants, launched Melvin Capital with nearly a billion dollars, named for his late grandfather. It became one of Wall Street's hottest funds, and in late 2020 it bet billions that dying mall retailer GameStop would keep falling.","fall":"Melvin Capital was Wall Street royalty, until Reddit noticed its massive short position in GameStop, visible in public filings, exceeded 100% of the stock's float. Millions of retail traders on r/WallStreetBets bought in unison, partly for profit, partly for vengeance. The stock went from $17 to $483; Melvin lost 53% in a month, needed a $2.75B emergency rescue, and never recovered. The fund shut down in 2022."},"three-arrows-cap":{"origin":"In 2012, two young Credit Suisse traders, Su Zhu and Kyle Davies, founded Three Arrows Capital in a Singapore café. Their macro calls made them crypto's most celebrated fund: $10 billion under management at peak, a Bali villa, and a podcast philosophy that crypto had entered a 'supercycle' where it only ever went up.","fall":"3AC's founders were crypto's smartest bulls, $10B at peak, borrowing from every lender in the industry, often uncollateralized, to buy more crypto. Their thesis: a 'supercycle' where prices basically don't crash again. They piled into Luna; Luna went to zero. Margin calls cascaded, 3AC ghosted its lenders, and its bankruptcy dominoed through Celsius, Voyager, and BlockFi. The founders went untraceable while liquidators chased yachts."},"celsius-freeze":{"origin":"In 2017, serial telecom founder Alex Mashinsky launched Celsius Network with a seductive pitch: 'unbank yourself', deposit your crypto, earn 8%+ interest, no bank involved. By 2021 it held over $11 billion in customer deposits and Mashinsky was podcasting weekly as crypto's friendly uncle.","fall":"Alex Mashinsky's pitch was seductive: banks rob you, so give Celsius your crypto and earn 18% yield. 'Unbank yourself.' Weekly AMAs, endless confidence. The yield came from increasingly desperate bets with customer funds, and when markets turned, Celsius froze all withdrawals, revealing a $1.2B hole. 1.7 million users lost access to $4.7B. Mashinsky sold his own tokens before the freeze and was later convicted of fraud."},"kerviel-socgen":{"origin":"Jérôme Kerviel was a junior trader who'd worked in the bank's back office, so he knew exactly which controls checked what, and when. He built €50B in unauthorized positions (more than the bank's entire market value), hiding them with fake offsetting trades timed to vanish before checks ran. Compliance flagged anomalies 74 times; each time his explanations were accepted.","fall":"When the bank finally found the truth, unwinding the position in a falling market cost €4.9B."},"borders-amazon":{"origin":"In 1971, brothers Tom and Louis Borders opened a used-book shop in Ann Arbor, Michigan, then wrote the inventory software that made modern bookselling possible. By the 1990s Borders was a retail colossus: cathedral-sized stores, cafés, and the industry's admiration. In 2001 it handed its online business to a young Seattle bookstore called Amazon to run.","fall":"Borders was America's second-largest bookstore chain, and in 2001 it made retail history's most self-destructive deal: it handed its ENTIRE online business to Amazon, reasoning e-commerce was a distraction from stores. For seven years, every Borders.com customer was actually an Amazon customer being trained. Borders doubled down on CD/DVD sections just as music went digital, and expanded stores as traffic moved online. It liquidated in 2011. Barnes & Noble, which built its own site, survived."},"circuit-city":{"origin":"In 1949, Samuel Wurtzel opened Wards, Richmond, Virginia's first retail TV shop. His son Alan built it into Circuit City, the category-killer that reinvented big-box electronics, and, in 1997, spun off a little used-car website called CarMax, before ordering its best-commissioned sales staff fired to cut costs.","fall":"Circuit City was literally a case study in the book 'Good to Great.' Then in 2007, management had an idea: fire the 3,400 HIGHEST-PAID sales associates, meaning the most experienced, best-performing people, and replace them with cheaper hires. Customers walked into stores where nobody could answer questions, while Best Buy's staff could. Sales collapsed, morale imploded, and within 18 months of the layoffs, Circuit City filed for bankruptcy and liquidated completely."},"jcpenney-johnson":{"origin":"Ron Johnson had built Apple's stores, retail's golden boy. Hired to reinvent JCPenney, he abolished coupons and fake markdowns overnight for 'fair and square' everyday pricing, rebuilt stores as boutiques, and refused to test any of it ('we didn't test at Apple').","fall":"But JCPenney's customers LOVED the coupon hunt, the fake discount was the fun. Sales fell 25% in one year, a $4.3B evaporation. Johnson was out in 17 months; the coupons came back."},"quaker-snapple":{"origin":"Fresh off making Gatorade huge, Quaker Oats paid $1.7B for Snapple, $1B more than analysts said it was worth, confident it could repeat the trick.","fall":"But Snapple wasn't Gatorade: its magic was quirky distribution through delis and gas stations, weird flavors, and offbeat ads. Quaker forced it into supermarket logistics, killed the beloved spokesperson, and 'professionalized' the brand into blandness. 27 months later they sold Snapple for $300M, a $1.4B loss."},"daimler-chrysler":{"origin":"In 1998, Daimler-Benz, maker of Mercedes, the engineering aristocracy of cars, bought Chrysler for $36 billion in the largest industrial merger ever attempted. Its chief Jürgen Schrempp called it a 'merger of equals'; Stuttgart's engineers were promised America's mass market, and Auburn Hills German discipline.","fall":"In 1998 Mercedes-maker Daimler merged with Chrysler in a $36B deal sold as a 'merger of equals', the future of global carmaking. In reality German precision-engineering culture and Detroit's scrappy cost-cutting culture despised each other. Executives fought over everything from expense accounts to engineering philosophy; talent fled; the promised synergies never appeared. Daimler's CEO later admitted 'merger of equals' had always been a polite fiction. In 2007 Chrysler was offloaded for roughly $7B."},"hp-autonomy":{"origin":"In 2011, HP's brand-new CEO Léo Apotheker bet $11.1 billion on Autonomy, the celebrated British software firm of professor-turned-tycoon Mike Lynch, to turn a hardware giant into an enterprise-software company overnight. It was HP's biggest deal since Compaq, and it closed in record time.","fall":"Desperate to transform into a software company, HP paid $11.1B for British software firm Autonomy, a 64% premium. HP's own CFO opposed the deal; other bidders had walked away over the numbers; short-sellers had publicly questioned Autonomy's accounting for years. HP bought anyway, on a compressed due-diligence timeline. Thirteen months later HP wrote off $8.8B, alleging accounting improprieties inflated the price, and spent the next decade in courtrooms."},"sony-betamax":{"origin":"In 1975, Sony launched Betamax, co-founder Masaru Ibuka's personal mission to let people record television. For a year Sony owned the home-video category it had invented. Then JVC shipped VHS with twice the recording time and, unlike proud Sony, licensed it to every electronics maker on Earth.","fall":"Betamax launched first and had better picture quality. But Sony kept it proprietary and pricey while JVC licensed VHS to everyone, flooding stores with cheap machines. VHS tapes recorded 2-4 hours (a whole movie or a football game); early Betamax managed one. Movie studios and rental shops followed the bigger installed base, which attracted more buyers, which attracted more movies. Sony's superior technology became a niche curiosity, and in 1988 Sony surrendered, and started making VHS players."},"ibm-msdos":{"origin":"In 1980, IBM, the undisputed emperor of computing, arrived at tiny Microsoft for an operating system for its new personal computer. Microsoft bought a $75,000 clone called 86-DOS, renamed it PC DOS, and, in a clause nobody in Armonk thought mattered, kept the right to sell it to anyone else.","fall":"Racing to launch the IBM PC, IBM licensed an operating system from a tiny company called Microsoft, and let Bill Gates keep the right to sell that same DOS to other manufacturers. IBM believed the money was in hardware; software was an accessory. Clone makers copied the hardware, licensed the same DOS, and undercut IBM. The 'IBM-compatible' market exploded, with Microsoft collecting a toll on every machine. IBM eventually sold off its own PC business; Microsoft became one of the most valuable companies on Earth."},"nortel-collapse":{"origin":"At its peak, Nortel was worth $250B and made up more than a THIRD of Canada's entire main stock index. It acquired companies frantically at dot-com prices, booked aggressive revenue, and optimized every quarter for the stock price.","fall":"When the bubble burst, revenue halved; then accounting scandals forced restatements; then years of firefighting left nothing invested in the next generation of products. The 114-year-old giant filed for bankruptcy in 2009 and was sold off in pieces."},"pan-am-fall":{"origin":"Pan Am WAS international air travel, it launched the 747, glamorized flying, even took reservations for moon flights.","fall":"But it bet everything on jumbo jets right before the 1973 oil crisis quadrupled fuel prices, and it had no domestic US network to feed its international routes when deregulation unleashed competition. It sold its iconic skyscraper, then its Pacific routes, then its Atlantic routes, eating itself to pay for one more year, until the Lockerbie tragedy and Gulf War fuel prices finished it in 1991."},"katerra-collapse":{"origin":"Katerra raised almost $3B (mostly SoftBank) to make buildings the way Toyota makes cars: factories, standard parts, vertical integration. It bought dozens of construction firms, built massive factories, and promised revolution.","fall":"But construction isn't cars: every site, code, climate, and client is different. Katerra took on huge fixed costs before proving its process actually saved money on real projects, absorbed acquisitions faster than it could integrate them, and bled cash on every 'revolutionary' building. Bankrupt in 2021."},"hyperloop-one":{"origin":"In 2014, investors rushed to turn Elon Musk's 2013 hyperloop whitepaper into a real company. Hyperloop One raised $450 million, built a full test track in the Nevada desert, hit 310 km/h in 2017, rebranded as Virgin Hyperloop, and announced feasibility studies for Dubai, India and the American Midwest.","fall":"Born from Elon Musk's white paper, Hyperloop One raised $450M+ to shoot passenger pods through vacuum tubes at 700 mph. It built a Nevada test track, signed glossy MoUs with governments, and rode a decade of hype. Total passengers ever carried: two employees, once, at 100 mph in 2020. The physics were possible; the economics, building hundreds of miles of near-perfect vacuum tube through earthquakes, land rights, and thermal expansion, never closed. It quietly shut down in 2023, selling the tubes for scrap."},"bird-scooters":{"origin":"In 2017, Travis VanderZanden, a former Uber and Lyft executive, founded Bird in Santa Monica and dropped dockless electric scooters on the streets overnight. Eighteen months later it was the fastest company in history to a $1 billion valuation, ordering hundreds of thousands of scooters from factories in China.","fall":"Bird became the fastest startup ever to a $1B valuation by carpet-bombing cities with rental scooters. Growth was spectacular; the math was not: each scooter cost hundreds of dollars, lasted a few MONTHS on the street (vandalism, weather, rivers), and needed armies of paid chargers. Bird lost money on every ride while competitors flooded the same sidewalks. It went public via SPAC at $2.5B, revealed it had even overcounted revenue for two years, became a penny stock, and filed for bankruptcy in 2023."},"convoy-trucking":{"origin":"Convoy, backed by Bezos, Gates, and top VCs at a $3.8B valuation, digitized freight matching between shippers and truckers. The tech worked.","fall":"But trucking is a brutal, cyclical, wafer-thin-margin commodity market, and Convoy's software convenience never translated into pricing power. When the 2022-23 freight recession crushed rates, Convoy was still structurally unprofitable and mid-fundraise. Money markets slammed shut, and one of logistics' best-funded startups shut down in days, catching even employees off guard."},"olive-ai":{"origin":"Founded in 2012 by former Air Force officer Sean Lane, Olive AI promised to automate the miserable back-offices of American hospitals with AI 'workers'. By 2021 it had raised $852 million at a $4 billion valuation, bought over a dozen startups, and become the poster child of healthcare's AI gold rush.","fall":"Olive AI hit a $4B valuation selling hospitals an 'AI workforce' to automate their brutal admin work. Investigations later found much of the 'AI' was basic screen-scraping automation, sold faster than it could be made to work, customers reported projects that never delivered the promised savings. Olive kept raising and rebranding while quietly losing hospitals, then began mass layoffs, sold off business lines, and shut down completely in 2023."},"irl-app":{"origin":"In 2017, Abraham Shafi launched IRL, 'in real life', a social app to help Gen Z find things to do with actual friends. SoftBank backed it at a $1.17 billion valuation in 2021; it claimed 20 million users and chart-topping growth rivaling BeReal.","fall":"Social app IRL ('In Real Life') raised $170M+, SoftBank led a round valuing it at $1.17B, on the strength of 20 million claimed monthly users chatting about events. After employees raised doubts and the board investigated, the finding was surreal: roughly 95% of IRL's 'users' were bots or automated accounts. The real user base of the billion-dollar social network was a rounding error. The company shut down in 2023; the SEC later charged the founder with fraud."},"color-labs":{"origin":"In 2011, celebrated serial founder Bill Nguyen raised $41 million for Color, a photo-sharing app, before writing a line of code. Launch day was chaos: a beautiful, baffling interface where everyone nearby appeared and no friends existed. It was Silicon Valley's most-funded mystery app.","fall":"In 2011, Color raised $41M from elite VCs BEFORE launching, for a photo app nobody had used. The idea: photos shared automatically with everyone physically near you. Launch day was a massacre: confusing interface, empty feeds (the app only worked if strangers around you also had it), and instant privacy discomfort. Reviews were brutal, usage cratered, and no pivot stuck. Within about 18 months the team was gone and the app was history, a permanent Silicon Valley cautionary tale."},"argo-ai":{"origin":"In 2017, ex-Google self-driving chief Bryan Salesky and ex-Uber ATG executive Peter Rander founded Argo AI in Pittsburgh. Ford and Volkswagen poured billions into it, valuing the robotaxi firm at $7.5 billion, with pilot services planned for Miami, Austin and Berlin.","fall":"Ford and Volkswagen poured $3.6B into Argo AI to win the self-driving race, full autonomy was 'a couple of years away,' as the whole industry kept saying, every year, for a decade. Argo's tech was genuinely respected; the problem was the last 1% of driving reality (weird intersections, rain, humans) devouring years and billions. In 2022 Ford concluded profitable robotaxis were 'a long way off,' both giants stopped funding, and Argo, 2,000 employees, closed almost overnight."},"essential-phone":{"origin":"In 2017, Andy Rubin, the engineer who created Android, launched Essential, promising a bezel-less 'anti-iPhone' from a secret Palo Alto lab. The Essential Phone shipped to warm reviews on a near-billion-dollar valuation, and the industry's most feared founder was back.","fall":"Andy Rubin CREATED Android, so when he launched Essential with $330M and a $1.2B valuation before shipping anything, the industry expected magic. The titanium-framed phone was beautiful but arrived months late, at iPhone prices, with a mediocre camera, sold mainly through one US carrier. Roughly 150,000 units sold, a catastrophic number against the marketing. Price cuts flopped, the sequel died in development, scandal engulfed Rubin, and Essential shut down in 2020."},"nirav-modi-pnb":{"origin":"Nirav Modi built a global luxury jewellery brand, flagship boutiques in New York and Hong Kong, celebrities in his front rows, from a Mumbai diamond family business. His firm and his uncle Mehul Choksi's Gitanjali empire were quietly financed, for six years, by fraudulent bank guarantees no auditor had caught.","fall":"Celebrity jeweller Nirav Modi, stores in Mumbai, London, New York; diamonds on Hollywood red carpets, ran a seven-year fraud through a single Punjab National Bank branch. Bank insiders issued him unauthorized 'Letters of Undertaking' via the SWIFT system WITHOUT recording them in the bank's own core software, the two systems weren't linked. New fake guarantees paid off old ones, a rolling ₹14,000 crore snowball, until one clerk retired and a replacement asked for collateral. Modi fled India days before it went public; extradition battles continue."},"bre-x-gold":{"origin":"Tiny Canadian miner Bre-X announced a monster gold find in the Borneo jungle, estimates swelled from 2 million ounces to 70 million, the 'richest deposit ever.' The stock rocketed from pennies to a $6B valuation; pension funds piled in. The truth: a geologist had been shaving gold from his wedding ring and buying river gold to sprinkle into ('salt') the core samples.","fall":"When an independent partner drilled its OWN holes: nothing. The geologist fell from a helicopter days before questioning; the stock hit zero within weeks."},"luckin-coffee":{"origin":"In 2017, Chinese fuel-billionaire Lu Zhengyao launched Luckin Coffee in Beijing to beat Starbucks at its own game: app-only ordering, aggressive coupons, a new store roughly every day. Within 18 months it had 2,000+ stores and history's fastest IPO, eighteen months after its first cup was sold.","fall":"Luckin opened coffee shops faster than any company in history, 4,500 stores in three years, and IPO'd on Nasdaq at a $4B+ valuation on a story of app-driven, discount-fueled hypergrowth. An anonymous 89-page short-seller report claimed the sales were staged; Luckin denied everything. Months later its own board investigation confirmed it: employees, directed from the top, had fabricated $310M in sales through fake orders. The stock collapsed 95%, Nasdaq delisted it, and executives were purged."},"vw-dieselgate":{"origin":"In 2006, Volkswagen set out to conquer America with 'clean diesel', a TDI engine that couldn't legally meet US emissions rules. So its engineers wrote software that recognised the emissions test and cheated it. Within a decade, eleven million cars carried the defeat device.","fall":"Volkswagen marketed 'clean diesel' as its weapon to become the world's #1 carmaker. The engineering truth: they couldn't hit US emissions limits at the promised cost and performance. Instead of admitting it, engineers installed software in 11 MILLION cars that detected laboratory testing and switched to clean mode, then emitted up to 40x the legal limit on real roads. Independent researchers with $70,000 of road tests exposed a conspiracy that cost VW $35B+, criminal convictions, and its reputation."},"wells-fargo-fake":{"origin":"After the 2008 crisis, Wells Fargo styled itself banking's honest survivor, too dull to fail, too decent to cheat, built on cross-selling ordinary Americans 'eight is great' products each. Its chief John Stumpf was the most admired banker in America; its branch culture had quotas for everything.","fall":"Wells Fargo's sacred metric was cross-selling: 'eight is great', eight products per customer. Branch staff faced daily quotas that customers' actual needs couldn't possibly fill, under threat of firing. So employees opened 3.5 MILLION unauthorized accounts and credit cards in real customers' names, forging signatures, moving money, generating fees. Thousands of low-level employees were fired over the years for it, while the incentive system that made it inevitable kept running until it all exploded in 2016."},"boeing-mcdonnell":{"origin":"In 1997, Boeing, the engineering-led giant behind every iconic jet of the jet age, merged with its smaller, struggling rival McDonnell Douglas for $13 billion. The deal was pitched as blending strengths. Within two years, McDonnell's cost-first executives were running the combined company.","fall":"For 80 years Boeing was an engineers' church, 'if it's not Boeing, I'm not going.' Then the 1997 McDonnell Douglas merger put finance culture in charge: headquarters moved away from the factories, stock buybacks outranked new aircraft, and outsourcing chased quarterly margins. The 737 MAX was the endpoint, an old airframe stretched one more time to avoid a costly new design, with software (MCAS) papering over the physics and training costs minimized as a selling point. Two crashes, 346 deaths, a 20-month grounding."},"musk-twitter-fire":{"origin":"In October 2022, Elon Musk completed his $44 billion takeover of Twitter, the platform politicians, journalists and half the planet used as a public square, vowing to restore free speech and shipping speed. Within the week, roughly half the company, including most of its trust and safety teams, was gone.","fall":"Elon Musk offered $44B for Twitter with minimal due diligence, '$54.20 a share,' a number containing a weed joke, then spent months trying to cancel the deal before courts forced it through. He fired ~80% of staff in weeks, demanded 'hardcore' loyalty pledges, made verification a subscription overnight (spawning fake 'verified' corporate accounts that tanked real stocks), and dared advertisers to leave. They did: ad revenue halved. Within a year, Fidelity marked the company at ~30% of the purchase price."},"zuck-metaverse":{"origin":"In October 2021, Mark Zuckerberg renamed Facebook, a trillion-dollar, three-billion-user company, to Meta, betting its future on the metaverse: a virtual world you'd inhabit through Quest headsets. Reality Labs, the division building it, was licensed to lose $10 billion a year, indefinitely.","fall":"In 2021 Mark Zuckerberg renamed Facebook to 'Meta' and redirected the company toward the metaverse, legless avatars, virtual offices, a future he declared inevitable. Reality Labs burned $10-15B per YEAR while flagship world Horizon Worlds struggled to keep even 200,000 monthly users, reports said many were Meta employees, reluctantly. The stock fell 76% before Zuckerberg partially retreated to a 'year of efficiency' (and then to AI). The metaverse didn't die, but $46B+ bought the lesson that you can't schedule a paradigm."},"howard-hughes-spruce":{"origin":"Howard Hughes promised the US government a fleet of giant flying boats to dodge U-boats in WWII. Wartime aluminum restrictions meant building it from wood, an engineering nightmare Hughes attacked with obsessive perfectionism, redesigning endlessly. The war ENDED with zero aircraft delivered. Hauled before a Senate committee for wasting millions, Hughes defiantly flew the colossal H-4 Hercules exactly once, 26 seconds, 70 feet up.","fall":"Then he kept it in a climate-controlled hangar, maintained flight-ready by a full crew, for 29 years. It never flew again."},"napoleon-egypt":{"origin":"Napoleon invaded Egypt to cut Britain's route to India, dazzling battles, scholars discovering the Rosetta Stone, glory dispatches home.","fall":"Then Admiral Nelson found the French fleet anchored at Aboukir Bay and annihilated it, stranding 35,000 soldiers with no way home. Plague, desert sieges, and a failed march into Syria followed. Napoleon's response: he slipped away on a fast ship, leaving his army behind (it surrendered years later), and spun the catastrophe into a hero's return that carried him to power."},"mao-sparrows":{"origin":"In 1958, Mao Zedong's China declared war on its own wildlife: the Four Pests Campaign marked sparrows for extermination because they ate grain. Hundreds of millions of citizens banged pots, waved flags and fired shotguns until the birds dropped dead from exhaustion, the opening campaign of the Great Leap Forward.","fall":"In 1958 China declared war on four pests, rats, flies, mosquitoes, and sparrows, accused of eating grain. Hundreds of millions of people banged pots so sparrows couldn't land until they fell dead from exhaustion; nests were destroyed nationwide. Sparrows, it turned out, mostly ate INSECTS. With their predator erased, locust populations exploded and devoured the harvests, deepening (alongside disastrous farm policies) the Great Famine. In 1960 sparrows were quietly removed from the list, replaced by bed bugs."},"ottoman-printing":{"origin":"Within decades of Gutenberg, presses covered Europe, multiplying books, literacy, science, and dissent. The Ottoman Empire, then the superpower, restricted printing in Arabic script for nearly 250 years, protecting the prestige (and income) of its calligrapher guilds and religious authorities' control over texts. Europe compounded knowledge at print speed; the empire copied by hand.","fall":"By the time printing spread freely, the scientific and industrial gap had become the story of the next two centuries."},"french-panama":{"origin":"In 1880, Ferdinand de Lesseps, the national hero who built the Suez Canal, launched the Panama Canal Company with lottery-style bonds. Half of France's middle class invested; a second Suez between the oceans seemed certain. De Lesseps was 75, and had never once seen Panama.","fall":"Ferdinand de Lesseps had built the Suez Canal, so France believed him when he said Panama would be the same: a sea-level canal, no locks, done in years. It wasn't the same: mountains instead of flat sand, a river that flooded 40 feet, and mosquitoes carrying yellow fever and malaria that killed workers by the thousands. De Lesseps, a diplomat not an engineer, rejected the lock-canal designs experts begged for, Suez proved he was right, didn't it? After 22,000 deaths and 800,000 ruined French investors, the company collapsed in history's biggest financial scandal to date. The Americans later finished it, WITH locks."},"imperial-armada":{"origin":"In 1588, King Philip II of Spain assembled the greatest fleet ever launched, 130 ships, 30,000 men, the 'Invincible Armada', to invade England, depose Elizabeth and end her pirates. The campaign was considered a formality of divine approval; Europe's Catholic powers had already begun celebrating.","fall":"Philip II of Spain sent 130 ships to conquer England, calling it the Enterprise of God, so confident in divine favor that inconvenient details went unfixed: the fleet's commander begged to be replaced (he got seasick and knew nothing of naval war), the plan required a rendezvous with an army that had no deep-water port to board from, and English ships were faster and out-gunned them at range. After fireships scattered the fleet, storms around Scotland and Ireland wrecked the rest. Philip's response: 'I sent my ships to fight the English, not the elements.'"},"xerox-alto-repeat":{"origin":"In the 1980s, Kodak's Rochester research labs co-invented the OLED, the organic light-emitting display technology that now lights billions of phones, TVs and laptops. Kodak patented it properly, built prototype displays, and for a while owned the future of every screen on Earth.","fall":"Everyone knows Kodak invented the digital camera and shelved it. Fewer know they did it TWICE: Kodak researchers co-invented OLED display technology in the 1980s, the screens now on billions of phones. Kodak licensed and sold off its OLED business in 2009 for a fraction of its future worth, to raise cash while dying from... digital photography. The same organization that couldn't imagine cameras without film couldn't imagine screens as a business either."},"mike-tyson-repeat":{"origin":"From Brooklyn's Brownsville, in and out of juvenile detention, Mike Tyson was taken in by legendary trainer Cus D'Amato, who built him into the youngest heavyweight champion in history at 20. The money followed: $400 million through promoter Don King, mansions, tigers, gold.","fall":"Mike Tyson earned over $400M, and filed for bankruptcy $23M in debt. The autopsy: an entourage of dozens living on his dime, two Bengal tigers ($70k each plus a trainer), mansions he forgot he owned, $2M in one jewelry visit, and promoters (he sued Don King for $100M) treating his fortune as their float. Nobody in the room was paid to say no; everyone was paid to say yes. Tyson later rebuilt modestly, and said losing it all was the beginning of actually owning his life."},"50cent-bankrupt":{"origin":"Curtis '50 Cent' Jackson grew up dealing crack in South Jamaica, Queens, survived nine bullets at point-blank range, and turned 'Get Rich or Die Tryin'' into the biggest debut of the decade. In 2007 he took equity in Vitamin Water instead of an endorsement fee, and cashed a reported $100 million when Coca-Cola bought it.","fall":"Curtis '50 Cent' Jackson made a fortune, famously ~$100M+ from Vitamin Water equity alone. By 2015 he was in bankruptcy court: lawsuits (including a $7M privacy judgment), a mansion costing $70k+ a MONTH to run, failed ventures, and spending engineered for Instagram (he later admitted the piles of cash in photos were partly props). The twist: 50 ran the bankruptcy strategically, restructured, paid creditors, and emerged joking about it, a rare case of using the crash as a tool."},"johnny-manziel":{"origin":"Johnny 'Football' Manziel won the Heisman as a freshman, improvisational genius, drafted in the first round by Cleveland in 2014.","fall":"Then the work stopped mattering to him: he later admitted he barely studied the playbook, partied through preparation weeks (once reportedly wearing a disguise in Vegas the weekend before a game), and relied on the talent that had always been enough. In college, it was. Against professionals who study 60 hours a week, it wasn't even close. Out of the NFL by 2016, at age 23."},"blockbuster-redbox":{"origin":"In 1985, David Cook opened the first Blockbuster in Dallas, 8,000 tapes, computerised inventory, spotless uniforms, and built it into the world's biggest video chain: 9,000 stores and 84 million members at peak. Its executives turned down buying a tiny DVD-by-mail startup called Netflix for $50 million in 2000.","fall":"Everyone remembers Blockbuster; Movie Gallery was the #2 chain that made the same mistake HARDER. In 2005, a year after Netflix was obviously working, it paid $850M to acquire Hollywood Video, doubling its bet on physical stores with heavy debt. Streaming and Redbox's $1 kiosks ate the market from both ends while Movie Gallery serviced acquisition loans. Bankruptcy in 2007, a brief exit, bankruptcy again in 2010, total liquidation, 4,700 stores gone."},"toshiba-westinghouse":{"origin":"In 2006, Toshiba's president Atsutoshi Nishida outbid everyone, paying $5.4 billion, nearly triple the nearest offer, for Westinghouse, the legendary American nuclear builder. The bet: a coming renaissance of reactors across China and America that Toshiba would design, build and fuel for a generation.","fall":"Toshiba paid $5.4B for nuclear-plant builder Westinghouse in 2006, triple what rivals bid, betting on a global 'nuclear renaissance.' Fukushima (2011) froze the industry; US construction projects ran billions over budget under fixed-price contracts Westinghouse had guaranteed. Toshiba, meanwhile, was caught inflating profits for years (a $1.2B accounting scandal born of impossible internal 'Challenge' targets). The 2017 reckoning: $6.3B writedown, Westinghouse bankrupt, and Toshiba forced to sell its crown-jewel memory-chip business to survive, then got delisted after 74 years."},"greensill-supply":{"origin":"Lex Greensill turned supply-chain finance, the world's most boring business, into a $7B fintech with a private jet fleet and David Cameron as advisor. The engine: packaging invoices into funds Credit Suisse sold as 'safe as cash.' Hidden inside: massive concentrated exposure to one client, steel magnate Sanjeev Gupta, including lending against 'future receivables' from customers that didn't exist yet.","fall":"When its insurer refused to renew ONE policy, the whole tower lost its 'safe' label overnight and collapsed in a week."},"wirecard-shadow":{"origin":"EM.TV, a Munich media firm, became the German dot-com era's darling, up 30,000% after IPO, buying the rights to the Muppets and half of Formula 1's holding company. Retail investors treated it as a national lottery ticket.","fall":"But the acquisitions were funded at bubble prices with shares and debt, revenue projections were fantasy, and management (two brothers, one later convicted) misstated results. When numbers were corrected, the stock fell 98%. A generation of German savers learned the market could do that."},"ackman-herbalife":{"origin":"In December 2012, hedge-fund star Bill Ackman stood on a Manhattan stage and announced a $1 billion short against Herbalife, the supplement company he called a pyramid scheme. He'd spent years investigating it, promised to donate his personal profits, and the stock began to fall on his word alone.","fall":"Bill Ackman publicly declared Herbalife a pyramid scheme and bet $1B on its stock going to ZERO, with a 3-hour presentation, a documentary, and total moral certainty. Rival billionaire Carl Icahn, who Ackman had feuded with for years, took the other side ON LIVE TV and bought 26% of the company, squeezing the short. Regulators fined Herbalife $200M but didn't kill it. Ackman held for FIVE YEARS as losses mounted, unable to walk away from a bet that had become his identity. He finally exited in 2018, near total loss on the position."},"paytm-ipo":{"origin":"In 2010, Vijay Shekhar Sharma launched Paytm as a phone-recharge site inside his startup One97. India's 2016 demonetisation made the wallet a national habit overnight; the wallet became a payments bank, and in November 2021 Paytm floated India's biggest IPO ever at the time, ₹18,300 crore.","fall":"Paytm raised ₹18,300 crore in India's biggest-ever IPO (2021), priced at ₹2,150 per share on the story of India's fintech revolution. The prospectus itself showed years of losses with no stated path to profit, brutal competition from free UPI payments, and a business pivoting every few quarters. The stock fell 27% on DAY ONE, the worst big-IPO debut in Indian history, and kept falling, down ~75% within a year. Retail investors who bought the brand's fame, not the filings, carried the loss."},"zilingo-unravel":{"origin":"Zilingo made 27-year-old Ankiti Bose the celebrated face of Southeast Asian startups, a fashion supply-chain platform valued at $970M, one round from unicorn status.","fall":"Then a routine fundraise due-diligence found the basics weren't there: financial statements hadn't been properly filed for years, revenue recognition was questioned, and the board suspended, then fired, the CEO amid contested harassment-complaint claims and counterclaims of a smear campaign. Funding froze, creditors seized assets, and the company was liquidated. Nobody was ever convicted, the company died of chaos, not verdicts."},"housing-rahul":{"origin":"Rahul Yadav, 26, built Housing.com into India's hottest startup, $100M+ from SoftBank, genuine product brilliance.","fall":"Then the implosion, in public: a leaked resignation letter calling investors 'intellectually incapable,' Twitter wars with rival CEOs, allotting his entire personal equity to employees in a grand gesture, then un-resigning. The board, exhausted, fired him within months for behavior 'not befitting a CEO.' The company he built merged away its independence; the era's most talented product founder became its cautionary tale."},"stayzilla-shut":{"origin":"Stayzilla was India's homestay pioneer, Airbnb before Airbnb mattered in India, live in 900 cities with 55,000 stays. Its playbook was the era's: discounts and cashbacks to buy growth, expansion everywhere at once, unit economics postponed.","fall":"When the 2016 funding winter arrived, there was no profitable core to retreat to, 900 shallow markets instead of 10 deep ones. The founder honestly admitted the model 'was ahead of its market' and shut it down, then faced a criminal complaint from a vendor over dues, a founder's worst month, in public."},"dunzo-cash":{"origin":"Dunzo was Bangalore's beloved verb, 'just Dunzo it.' Google invested; Reliance put in $200M at a $775M valuation.","fall":"Then quick-commerce arrived and Dunzo chose to fight Zomato-Blinkit, Zepto, and Swiggy Instamart simultaneously, dark stores, ads everywhere, deep discounts, a capital war against opponents with 10x the ammunition. When the next round didn't come: unpaid salaries for months, vendor lawsuits, cofounders exiting, and by 2024-25 the app effectively went dark. Being loved, it turned out, was not a moat."},"gitanjali-choksi":{"origin":"Mehul Choksi built Gitanjali Gems into a 4,000-store jewelry empire with celebrity endorsements, respectability itself. With nephew Nirav Modi, he ran the same PNB letters-of-undertaking carousel: fake bank guarantees rolling over for years, funding a listed company whose glitter was borrowed.","fall":"When it broke, Choksi had already taken citizenship in Antigua under its investment program. Lakhs of small shareholders in his listed companies were wiped out; franchisees who'd paid for stores were stranded with worthless stock."},"ranbaxy-data":{"origin":"Born in 1937 as a medicine distributor and reborn in 1961, Ranbaxy became India's first pharmaceutical multinational, the Singh brothers' empire of affordable generics, shipping from FDA-approved plants to half the world, its red-and-white logo trusted from Delhi pharmacies to American drugstores.","fall":"Ranbaxy was India's pharmaceutical pride, first to crack the US generics market. Inside, a whistleblower (Dinesh Thakur) discovered the unthinkable: test data for drug approvals fabricated across markets, stability tests backdated, results invented for drugs already being swallowed by patients worldwide. His seven-year fight ended with Ranbaxy pleading GUILTY to seven US federal criminal counts and a $500M fine. The company that proved Indian pharma could compete globally also proved what happens when 'jugaad' meets regulated science."},"speak-asia":{"origin":"Speak Asia ran full-page newspaper ads and prime-time TV spots: pay ₹11,000, fill two online surveys a week, earn ₹52,000 a year. 'Market research,' they called it. 24 lakh Indians joined, many borrowing to pay entry fees, whole families signing up. The truth: there were almost no real corporate clients wanting surveys; payouts came from new members' fees, the oldest trick wearing a laptop.","fall":"When payments froze in 2011, ₹2,200 crore was gone and the promoters were untraceable abroad."},"sahara-repeat":{"origin":"In 1978, Subrata Roy founded Sahara India Pariwar in Gorakhpur with a few thousand rupees as a village chit fund, finance built entirely on trust. It swelled into an empire of 30 million depositors: news channels, an IPL team, hotels, and Aamby Valley, a city with its own airport.","fall":"Amit Bhardwaj toured India as a crypto visionary, books, seminars, Bollywood connections, selling 'cloud mining contracts': give me your Bitcoin, get 10% MONTHLY returns for 18 months. At Bitcoin's 2017 peak mania, tens of thousands handed over coins. There was little real mining; payouts were recycled deposits, later paid in his own worthless token when Bitcoin ran short. Investigators estimated the Bitcoin collected could be worth up to ₹1 lakh crore at later prices. Bhardwaj died in 2022 with cases still crawling; most victims never saw a satoshi."},"deepwater-bp":{"origin":"In 2009, BP, Europe's biggest oil company, freshly rebranded 'Beyond Petroleum', leased the Deepwater Horizon rig to drill the Macondo well a mile beneath the Gulf of Mexico. The project was running 43 days late and $58 million over budget when the rig began its final job.","fall":"BP's Macondo well was over budget and 43 days late, every extra day cost $1M. So decisions tilted: fewer centralizers than the design called for, a cement test skipped, ambiguous pressure-test results explained away, warnings from rig workers overridden. Each shortcut saved hours. Together they removed every barrier between the crew and the blowout that killed 11 people and poured 4 million barrels of oil into the Gulf for 87 straight days on live camera. Total cost to BP: over $65 billion, 65,000 times the daily overrun they were racing to avoid."},"rana-plaza":{"origin":"On April 23, 2013, cracks appeared in the walls of Rana Plaza, a Dhaka building holding five garment factories. A bank and shops on lower floors evacuated and stayed out.","fall":"But the factories had deadlines for Western fast-fashion brands, so managers ordered 3,000+ workers back in the next morning, some threatened with docked pay. The building collapsed at 8:57 AM, killing 1,134 people. The owner had illegally added three floors and heavy generators to a structure built for shops, on swampy ground, with commercial-grade permits."},"maple-syrup-heist":{"origin":"Quebec's Federation of Maple Syrup Producers controls the world supply from fortress-like warehouses, the 'OPEC of maple syrup', stockpiling barrels to keep prices steady. Its great global reserve in Saint-Louis-de-Bonsecours held $30 million of Canada's liquid gold, guarded by one employee and a padlock.","fall":"Canada's syrup cartel kept a strategic reserve, thousands of barrels in a rented warehouse, audited once a year by counting barrels. Thieves rented space in the SAME warehouse, and for a year quietly siphoned syrup from barrels, refilling many with water, trucking $18.7M of liquid gold to black-market buyers. The heist, the largest agricultural theft in history, was discovered only when an inspector climbed a stack and a 'full' barrel nearly tipped over because it was empty."},"lehman-2008":{"origin":"In 1850, brothers Henry, Emanuel and Mayer Lehman founded a cotton brokerage in Montgomery, Alabama. Over 158 years their firm survived the railroads, the Crash of 1929 and two world wars to become Wall Street's fourth-largest investment bank, and by 2006, its hungriest packager of subprime mortgages.","fall":"Lehman survived the Civil War, the Great Depression, and two world wars, then died in a weekend. The 158-year-old bank leveraged itself 31:1 into subprime mortgages, used accounting tricks (Repo 105) to hide debt at quarter-ends, and CEO Dick Fuld, 'the Gorilla', turned down rescue offers he considered beneath Lehman's dignity. On September 15, 2008, it filed the largest bankruptcy in history and took the global economy's confidence down with it."},"barings-leeson":{"origin":"Founded in 1762, Barings was Britain's oldest merchant bank, it financed the Napoleonic Wars and the Louisiana Purchase, and counted the Queen among its clients. In 1992 it dispatched 25-year-old Nick Leeson to Singapore to run a new futures desk, back office and all.","fall":"Barings, Britain's oldest merchant bank, financier of the Louisiana Purchase, banker to the Queen, let 28-year-old Nick Leeson run BOTH trading and back-office settlement in Singapore. He hid losses in error account 88888, doubled down repeatedly to trade his way out, and forged documents to cover margin calls. The Kobe earthquake shattered his positions: £827M in losses, twice the bank's capital. Barings sold to ING for £1."},"blackberry-crackberry":{"origin":"In 1984, 23-year-old engineering student Mike Lazaridis founded Research In Motion in a Waterloo strip mall. Fifteen years later RIM unleashed the BlackBerry, real email in your pocket, security no rival could touch, and by 2009 it owned half the American smartphone market. Presidents refused to surrender theirs.","fall":"John DeLorean, GM's youngest-ever division head, celebrity executive, left to build his stainless-steel gullwinged dream car. Everything was maximal: a factory in Northern Ireland (for government subsidies) with an untrained workforce, constant redesigns, a 1981 recession launch at nearly double the promised price. Quality was dire, sales a fraction of projections. Desperate for cash, DeLorean was arrested in an FBI cocaine-trafficking sting (later acquitted, entrapment). The company died; the car became famous three years later, in Back to the Future."},"napster-burn":{"origin":"Napster hit 80 million users faster than any product in history, teenagers Shawn Fanning and Sean Parker had built the future of music distribution.","fall":"But every download was copyright infringement; instead of converting to a licensed model early (labels initially would have talked), Napster fought Metallica, Dr. Dre, and the RIAA in court while its users' behavior remained illegal. Shut down by injunction in 2001. Two years later Apple launched iTunes and monetized the exact demand Napster proved."},"segway-hype":{"origin":"Inventor Dean Kamen spent a decade and $100 million building 'IT', a self-balancing machine code-named Ginger, so secret that leak-driven hype claimed it would rebuild cities. Launched in December 2001 with Jeff Bezos and John Doerr on stage, the Segway was declared bigger than the internet.","fall":"Pre-launch, Segway ('Project Ginger') was hyped by Steve Jobs ('as big as the PC') and Jeff Bezos; inventor Dean Kamen predicted 10,000 units a WEEK and said cities would be redesigned around it. The reveal: a $5,000 scooter you couldn't ride on sidewalks (banned in many cities), roads (too slow), or anywhere dignity mattered. It sold ~30,000 units in six years, 1% of projections, becoming shorthand for hype-vs-reality. Mall cops and tour groups kept it on life support."},"google-glass":{"origin":"Google launched Glass with skydivers livestreaming into its I/O keynote, the most spectacular product demo ever.","fall":"Then reality: $1,500 for a beta product, battery dying in hours, and a face-mounted camera that made everyone around the wearer feel recorded. Bars banned it; 'Glasshole' entered the dictionary; wearers reported strangers' open hostility. The consumer product retreated in 2015, the lesson being social, not technical."},"atari-et":{"origin":"In 1982, Atari was the fastest-growing company in American history, $2 billion a year in cartridges, sold out nationwide. When E.T. became the year's biggest movie, Atari paid $21 million for the game rights and gave one programmer five weeks to ship it by Christmas.","fall":"Atari paid $21M for E.T. movie rights in July 1982 and demanded the game ship for Christmas, giving one programmer FIVE WEEKS (normal: 6+ months). They manufactured 4 million cartridges for a game that was barely playable; an estimated 3.5M came back or never sold. Atari secretly dumped truckloads into a New Mexico landfill (excavated in 2014, the legend was true). The debacle helped trigger the 1983 crash that nearly killed the entire video game industry."},"netscape-ie":{"origin":"Netscape owned 90% of the browser market. Facing Microsoft's bundled-free IE, it made the fatal engineering call: throw away the entire working codebase and rewrite from scratch. The rewrite took THREE YEARS, an eternity during which Netscape shipped almost nothing while IE improved relentlessly with Windows' distribution behind it.","fall":"By the time anything emerged, the war was over. Joel Spolsky's verdict became doctrine: 'the single worst strategic mistake any software company can make.'"},"swissair-grounding":{"origin":"For seventy years Swissair was Switzerland's other flag, the 'flying bank', so profitable it bought stakes in other airlines, so proud it served caviar above the Alps. In the 1990s its Hunter Strategy went shopping for Europe's struggling carriers, piling debt onto a balance sheet built on quality.","fall":"Swissair was so financially solid it was called 'the Flying Bank', a symbol of Swiss reliability. Consultants sold it the 'Hunter Strategy': buy stakes in half of Europe's weakest airlines (Sabena, LOT, LTU, Air Littoral...) to build an alliance by acquisition. Each stake bled cash; each rescue demanded more. In October 2001, out of cash, the fleet was grounded overnight, planes full of passengers, stranded worldwide. Switzerland treated it as a national humiliation; executives faced (unsuccessful) criminal trials."},"kingfisher-vijay":{"origin":"In 2005, liquor and Formula-1 baron Vijay Mallya, the self-styled 'King of Good Times', launched Kingfisher Airlines, painting Indian skies in his beer's colours: red livery, in-flight entertainment, glamour no rival matched. That same year he bought the country's biggest budget airline, Air Deccan.","fall":"Ken Olsen built DEC into the world's #2 computer company, the minicomputer king. In 1977 he declared there was 'no reason for any individual to have a computer in his home.' DEC dismissed PCs as toys for a decade, launched three incompatible half-hearted PCs at once in 1983 (all flopped), and kept perfecting minicomputers as the world moved to desktops. The proud engineering giant sold itself to PC-maker Compaq in 1998, bought by the very category it had laughed at."},"polaroid-instant":{"origin":"Polaroid didn't miss digital, it helped invent it, with quality digital prototypes in the 1980s and deep sensor patents.","fall":"But the business model was razor-and-blades: cameras cheap, film at fat margins, and digital had no film. Executives couldn't approve a future without the blade revenue, so digital projects were starved while film cash flowed. When digital arrived anyway (without them), the collapse was total: bankruptcy in 2001, the beloved brand license-sold to strangers."},"vine-shutdown":{"origin":"Vine's 6-second looping videos created modern short-form culture and its first stars.","fall":"But Twitter (which bought it pre-launch for $30M) never built creator monetization, top Viners asked for $1.2M collectively to stay; Twitter declined, and the stars left for YouTube and Instagram, taking audiences with them. Shut down in 2017 as 'unprofitable.' A year later, TikTok scaled the exact format with creator funds into a $300B+ phenomenon."},"friendster-lag":{"origin":"Friendster invented mainstream social networking a year before MySpace and three before Facebook, 3M users in months.","fall":"Then it drowned in its own success: pages took 20-40 SECONDS to load for two years while the board debated features and turned down Google's $30M acquisition offer (in pre-IPO stock that would be worth billions). Users defected en masse to faster MySpace. The postmortem was engineering triage: they kept scaling features while the site's basic experience burned."},"digg-v4":{"origin":"In 2004, Kevin Rose built Digg as a rebellion against media gatekeepers: users submitted links, the crowd voted, and the front page belonged to everyone. By 2008 it rivalled Facebook for buzz, 200 million page views a year, and Rose was on magazine covers as the face of Web 2.0.","fall":"Digg was the internet's front page, 30M+ users voting stories up. Version 4 changed everything at once: killed the bury button, broke comments, auto-promoted publishers' feeds over users' picks, the community's power handed to brands overnight, plus launch-day crashes. Users revolted by mass-migrating to Reddit within WEEKS (Reddit's traffic graph shows the cliff). The $175M-valued pioneer sold for $500k in 2012."},"compaq-hp":{"origin":"In 2001, Hewlett-Packard, Silicon Valley's original garage company, beloved for 'the HP Way' of trust and engineering pride, announced a $25 billion acquisition of Compaq, the great PC maker. CEO Carly Fiorina promised scale for the commodity age; the founding families publicly revolted. It was the most contested merger in tech history.","fall":"When Xerox finally commercialized its revolutionary PARC technology as the Star workstation, it priced the future at $16,500 per unit (≈$50k today), needing whole networked office installations of $100k+. The same year, IBM's crude beige PC launched at $1,565. The Star was a decade ahead in capability and a decade behind in understanding who buys computers and how. Two years later Apple's Lisa repeated the pricing mistake; the Mac finally got it right, with Xerox's own ideas."},"wecrashed-others":{"origin":"In 1936, the Hindenburg, the Zeppelin Company's 245-metre flagship, the largest flying object ever built, began crossing the Atlantic in half a ship's time: dining room, writing lounge, a grand piano, cabins above the clouds. It was engineered for non-flammable helium; an American export ban meant hydrogen.","fall":"The Hindenburg was the largest flying machine ever built, a floating luxury hotel crossing the Atlantic in half a ship's time. It was designed for inert helium; a US export ban left Germany filling it with flammable HYDROGEN, accepted as routine ('we've flown hydrogen for years'). Landing at Lakehurst, static or a spark ignited it: 34 seconds from flame to ground, on newsreel, with radio's most famous broadcast ('Oh, the humanity!'). Public confidence in airships died that evening, permanently."},"vasa-ship":{"origin":"In 1625, King Gustavus Adolphus of Sweden commissioned the Vasa, the most heavily armed warship the Baltic had ever seen, 64 bronze cannons on two gun decks, gilded sculptures crowning the stern. Mid-build, the King demanded a second gun deck. The master shipwright, with the King as client, could not refuse.","fall":"King Gustavus Adolphus demanded his great warship carry a second gun deck, mid-construction, against the shipwright's design. Nobody dared refuse the king at war. The stability test (30 men running side to side) was stopped after three runs because the ship rolled alarmingly, and NOBODY TOLD THE KING. The Vasa sailed 1,300 meters on her maiden voyage, caught one gust, heeled, flooded through the open gun ports, and sank in full view of the celebrating crowd. She lay in the harbor mud for 333 years."},"napoleon-continental":{"origin":"In 1806, Napoleon, master of Europe after Austerlitz, issued the Berlin Decrees: the Continental Blockade, sealing every port from Hamburg to Naples against British trade. England's island economy would be strangled into peace; France would now rule by commerce what it had conquered by arms.","fall":"Tower Records was music retail's cathedral, $1B in revenue, stores that were pilgrimage sites. In the late 90s it borrowed ~$110M to expand internationally at maximum speed... directly into Napster (1999), iTunes (2003), and the CD's collapse. The debt that funded the new stores demanded CD-era cash flows exactly when those flows halved. Bankruptcy in 2004, liquidation in 2006. The documentary title captured it: All Things Must Pass."},"ac-gilbert":{"origin":"A.C. Gilbert made Erector Sets and chemistry kits, the century's iconic educational toys, a company with a 58-year reputation for quality.","fall":"When 1960s kids turned to TV-advertised toys, new management panicked: slashed quality to cut prices, chased fads (spy kits, slot cars) far from its identity, flooded discount channels that cheapened the brand, and churned through strategies yearly. Each pivot alienated loyalists without winning the fad market. Dead by 1967, a Harvard case study in panic-driven identity loss."},"orkut-brazil":{"origin":"In 2004, Google engineer Orkut Büyükkökten built a small social network as a side project, invite-only, clean, human. Brazil adopted it as a national living room: within four years the vast majority of Brazilians online were on Orkut, and 'scraps', public testimonials between friends, became part of the country's courtship.","fall":"Orkut launched WEEKS before Facebook in 2004, with Google's infinite resources behind it. It became the #1 site in Brazil and India, then Google's attention wandered: spam overwhelmed communities, performance lagged, redesigns arrived years late, and the A-team moved to other projects (eventually Google+). Facebook methodically localized, shipped weekly, and took both strongholds. Google closed Orkut in 2014, having held the winning position in two of the world's biggest markets and simply... not pressed it."},"stockton-fisher":{"origin":"In 2007, Henrik Fisker, the designer of the BMW Z8 and Aston Martin DB9, founded Fisker Automotive to build the Karma, the world's first true luxury plug-in hybrid: gorgeous, Californian, range-extending. Al Gore's investment firm put money in; Leonardo DiCaprio took delivery of the first.","fall":"Henrik Fisker designed the gorgeous Karma hybrid, and outsourced its soul: battery packs from troubled A123 Systems that recalled and bankrupted mid-production, assembly in Finland, software full of gremlins. Karmas caught fire in garages; Consumer Reports' test car DIED on the test track; and 338 brand-new Karmas drowned in a New Jersey port during Hurricane Sandy, the perfect metaphor. Fisker burned $1.4B without ever controlling its own supply chain."},"colonial-parkinson":{"origin":"RadioShack survived a century by riding waves, CB radios, PCs (the TRS-80 was once the world's best-selling computer!), phones.","fall":"But it never chose an identity: was it the tinkerer's parts store? A phone kiosk? A toy shop? By the 2000s it was 7,000 tiny stores selling everything badly, renamed 'The Shack' in a cringe rebrand, its knowledgeable staff cut for cost. The maker movement, its natural rebirth, happened without it, on Amazon and Adafruit. Bankruptcy in 2015, again in 2017."},"gowalla-foursquare":{"origin":"In 2009, Josh Williams launched Gowalla from Austin, the beautiful check-in app, with illustrated passports stamped as you travelled the world. Foursquare shipped weeks later: uglier, point-obsessed, game-obsessed. The 'check-in wars' of 2010 became location technology's land rush, and Gowalla was the critics' favourite.","fall":"Color raised a legendary $41M BEFORE LAUNCH (Sequoia gave more than they'd given Google pre-launch) for 'implicit proximity-based social photo sharing.' Launch day: users opened it, saw empty feeds (it needed OTHER nearby users to function, a cold-start paradox), couldn't understand what it was for, and left one-star reviews. The team pivoted twice, returned some money, and shut down. The name became the era's shorthand for funding-round-as-product."},"boo-com":{"origin":"In 1998, Swedish literary it-couple Ernst Malmsten and Kajsa Leander, who had sold their bookstore website at the top, raised $135 million for boo.com: a global online fashion store launching in eighteen countries at once, headquartered in chic Carnaby Street, London, with a 3-D virtual model called Miss Boo.","fall":"Boo.com launched global fashion e-commerce in 1999 with 3D product spins, a virtual assistant ('Miss Boo'), and offices in six countries pre-revenue. The site took EIGHT MINUTES to load on the era's dial-up (it required Flash and broadband almost nobody had), didn't work on Macs at launch, and processing returns across 18 countries ate everything. Champagne culture, Concorde flights, $135M gone in 18 months, the dot-com crash's poster child."},"iridium-sat":{"origin":"In the 1990s, Motorola bet $6 billion on the most ambitious communications project in history: 66 satellites in low orbit, so a call from the Gobi, the Pacific or the pole would connect to anywhere on Earth. Iridium's first calls rang in September 1998, and its brick phones cost $3,000, at $3 a minute.","fall":"Motorola's Iridium launched 66 satellites for a global phone, engineering's Everest, conceived in 1987 when cellular was patchy. By its 1998 launch, GSM roaming covered the profitable world. The Iridium handset: $3,000, brick-sized, $7/minute, and needing line-of-sight to sky, it FAILED INDOORS AND IN CARS, where businesspeople live. It won 20,000 subscribers against a 500,000 break-even. Bankruptcy in nine months; the $5B constellation sold for $25M."},"premier-smokeless":{"origin":"In 1988, RJ Reynolds, the tobacco giant behind Camel and Salem, unveiled Premier, a $325 million 'smokeless' cigarette that heated tobacco instead of burning it: the industry's boldest answer to the health panic, and the most expensive new-product launch in its history.","fall":"RJ Reynolds spent $325M developing Premier, a 'smokeless' cigarette that heated instead of burned. Focus groups had warned everyone: smokers said it tasted 'like charcoal' and hissed when lit; it required instructions to smoke; and health groups attacked it anyway. The company's own CEO admitted it 'tasted like sh*t.' It launched anyway (sunk costs demanded it), died in four months, and became a Harvard case in ignoring your own research."},"hubble-mirror":{"origin":"Astronomer Lyman Spitzer proposed a telescope above the atmosphere in 1946 and fought for it for thirty years; Congress funded it in 1977. NASA and ESA built the mirror to a tolerance of a millionth of an inch with contractor Perkin-Elmer, and, pressed by budgets and schedules, skipped the independent test that would have caught the flaw.","fall":"Hubble launched as humanity's greatest eye, and returned blurry images. The primary mirror was ground 2.2 MICRONS wrong (1/50th a hair) because the testing device itself was misassembled, and two cheaper tests that FLAGGED the error were dismissed, since the fancy instrument 'couldn't be wrong.' The fix (corrective optics installed by spacewalk in 1993) cost hundreds of millions. Post-fix, Hubble became history's most productive scientific instrument, the disaster was always in the unchecked confidence, not the glass."},"dean-foods":{"origin":"Canadian miner Bre-X claimed the century's biggest gold find in Borneo, 70 million ounces. The stock rode from pennies to a $6B valuation; pension funds piled in. The 'gold': core samples salted with shaved jewelry gold and river gold, for YEARS, by the site geologist.","fall":"When partner Freeport's independent drilling found NOTHING, the geologist fell from a helicopter over the jungle days before questioning. The stock went to zero in weeks; Canadian markets rewrote their mining disclosure rules (NI 43-101) because of it."},"onecoin-ruja":{"origin":"In 2014, Bulgarian former-consultant Ruja Ignatova launched OneCoin as 'the Bitcoin killer', sold through multi-level recruiters at seminars across Europe, Africa and Asia. No public blockchain, no mining, no real exchange: just conference stages, diamond-draped keynote videos, and packages of 'tokens' priced ever upward.","fall":"Dr. Ruja Ignatova, Oxford-educated, McKinsey-polished, sold OneCoin in stadium events as the 'Bitcoin killer.' Investigators later found the truth: there was NO blockchain, just a SQL database where admins typed numbers. Sold via MLM 'education packages' across 175 countries, heaviest in developing nations, it took $4B+ from families who mortgaged homes. In 2017 Ruja boarded a flight to Athens and vanished, she remains on the FBI's Ten Most Wanted."},"nkotb-milli":{"origin":"In 1988, German producer Frank Farian, the man behind Boney M, assembled Milli Vanilli: model-dancers Rob Pilatus and Fab Morvan fronting vocals recorded by studio singers. By 1990 they had sold 30 million singles, won the Best New Artist Grammy, and were MTV royalty, their secret shared by exactly six people.","fall":"Milli Vanilli sold 30M+ singles and won the 1990 Best New Artist Grammy, and Rob and Fab had sung NONE of it (producer Frank Farian hired them as faces for session singers' vocals). The unraveling began onstage when their backing track skipped, repeating 'Girl you know it's-' like a broken machine. Under pressure, Farian confessed; the Grammy was revoked (unique in history); lawsuits offered refunds to millions of fans. Rob Pilatus spiraled and died at 32."},"leeson-society":{"origin":"In 1692, Salem village had survived frontier wars, smallpox and a charter crisis, and its minister's nine-year-old niece began convulsing. Under pressure, the Caribbean servant Tituba confessed to witchcraft, and a special court convened with a new standard of proof: 'spectral evidence', dreams and visions as testimony.","fall":"In Salem, afflicted girls' fits were accepted as proof of witchcraft, and courts admitted 'spectral evidence', testimony that the accused's SPIRIT had appeared in dreams. Denial was read as guilt; confession (with accusations of others) spared you, a mechanical engine for cascading accusations. Twenty were executed, one pressed to death for refusing to plead. It stopped only when accusations reached the governor's wife, suddenly, standards of evidence mattered. Courts later declared the trials unlawful; apologies took centuries."},"south-sea-mania":{"origin":"In 1711, Britain granted the South Sea Company a monopoly on trade with Spanish America, plus the job of refinishing £10 million of national debt. By 1720 the company was lending people money to buy its own shares, promising golden colonies, while the actual trade never materialised.","fall":"In the late 90s, Beanie Babies were 'investments': price guides projected specific toys' values decades out, families skipped vacations to buy inventory, a divorcing couple split their collection under court supervision (the photo is legendary), and one father famously spent ~$100k intending to fund college. Ty Inc. fueled scarcity with retirements; eBay made prices visible, until 1999, when the music stopped. The 'rare' bears turned out to be plentiful; collections became landfill."},"knight-capital":{"origin":"In 2012, Knight Capital was America's biggest handler of stock orders, a 17-year-old giant routing a tenth of the entire NYSE and NASDAQ volume, born from 1995's SunGard spin-off. On August 1 it began deploying a repurposed piece of old software nicknamed 'the Power Peg'.","fall":"Knight Capital deployed new trading software to its eight servers, but a technician missed ONE server, which still ran retired code awakened by a reused feature flag. At market open, that server fired millions of unintended orders: $7B of accidental positions in 45 minutes, moving 150 stocks. No kill switch existed; staff debugged live while the machine traded. Loss: $440M, four times annual profit. The firm was sold within months."},"mtgox-crypto":{"origin":"In 2010, Jed McCaleb bought the domain MtGox, 'Magic: The Gathering Online eXchange', and repurposed it for a strange new use: trading Bitcoin. Mark Karpelès bought it in 2011 and moved it to Tokyo; by 2013, 70% of all Bitcoin transactions on Earth passed through it.","fall":"Mt. Gox began as a Magic: The Gathering card exchange, pivoted to Bitcoin, and accidentally became custodian of 70% of world Bitcoin trading, run by one overwhelmed developer with no security team, no version control at times, and cold wallets tracked loosely. Hackers siphoned coins for YEARS undetected. In 2014 it collapsed owing 850,000 BTC. Creditors waited a DECADE for partial repayment while the coins' value grew 100x."},"german-wings-recall":{"origin":"Lufthansa's budget wing Germanwings flew 31 million passengers a year with a safety record built on Europe's strictest oversight. Its pilots were screened at hiring, but mental-health privacy rules, written in the era of stigma, meant no one ever looked once the uniform was on.","fall":"Takata's airbag inflators used cheap ammonium nitrate propellant that degraded with humidity, turning life-savers into shrapnel grenades. Internal tests showed ruptures as early as the 2000s; engineers reported data being deleted and reports edited. Instead of redesigning, Takata doubled down for a decade as injuries mounted. Result: the largest automotive recall in history (~100 million inflators), criminal charges, dozens of deaths, and 2017 bankruptcy."},"boeing-max":{"origin":"In 2011, with American Airlines flirting with Airbus's new fuel-sipping A320neo, Boeing faced a choice: design a new plane for the 2020s or stretch the 50-year-old 737. A clean-sheet design meant five years; re-engining the MAX promised airlines savings without retraining pilots, and kept Airbus out.","fall":"To match Airbus without a costly new design, Boeing bolted bigger engines onto the 60-year-old 737 frame and wrote software (MCAS) to mask the changed flight behavior, then, to preserve the 'no new pilot training required' sales promise, largely omitted MCAS from pilot manuals. MCAS took input from a SINGLE sensor. Two crashes, Lion Air 610 and Ethiopian 302, 346 dead, five months apart, as pilots fought software they hadn't been told existed. The fallout: a 20-month grounding, criminal settlements, and the engineering giant's culture on trial."},"excite-google":{"origin":"In 1999, Google, six postdocs and a server rack in a garage, was shopping itself for under $1 million to escape the dotcom chill. Excite, then one of the web's four great portals, said no. Its team even asked Larry and Sergey to turn off their demo on Excite's own site: it made search results too good.","fall":"In 1999, Larry Page and Sergey Brin, wanting to return to Stanford, offered to sell Google to search portal Excite for $1M, then dropped to $750k. Excite's CEO George Bell declined; one account says he objected that Google was TOO GOOD, better search would move users off the portal faster, hurting ad views. Excite died in the dot-com crash; Google became... Google. Bell's 'no' may be capitalism's most expensive sentence."},"zano-drone":{"origin":"In 2014, Torquing Group, a small Welsh engineering firm with defence contracts, crowdfunded Zano, a palm-sized autonomous camera drone, on Kickstarter. It raised £2.3 million from 12,000 backers to become the UK's biggest Kickstarter hardware project ever, with CES awards and a Trump-style demo video.","fall":"Zano promised a palm-sized selfie drone with obstacle avoidance and follow-me AI, Europe's biggest Kickstarter (£2.3M). The demo video was largely aspiration; the shipped units (a few hundred of 15,000 owed) could barely hover, drifting into walls with dead batteries in minutes. The company liquidated abruptly; Kickstarter itself commissioned an investigative post-mortem, which found no scam, 'just' catastrophic overpromising by founders who believed their own render."},"pets-com":{"origin":"In 1998, Pets.com launched to sell pet food online with sock-puppet mascot through Super Bowl ads and a Macy's Thanksgiving balloon. Amazon backed it; Julie Wainwright led it; 320,000 visitors a month bought dog bowls at below cost. It IPO'd in February 2000, nine months before the floor fell out.","fall":"The dot-com era's mascot: a sock-puppet dog with a Super Bowl ad, a Macy's parade balloon, and a business that lost money on every single sale. Shipping 40-pound bags of dog food cost more than the food; the company sold at a third BELOW cost to buy growth. IPO in February 2000, liquidation by November, 268 days, the fastest major flameout of the bubble."},"webvan-warehouses":{"origin":"In 1996, Louis Borders, who had built the bookstore chain Borders, founded Webvan to deliver groceries ordered online within a 30-minute window. The vision was total: $1 billion spent on robotics-powered warehouses across America, a fleet of silver delivery vans, and the promise that nobody would ever push a cart again.","fall":"Webvan decided online grocery needed to be BIG immediately: a $1B Bechtel contract for 26 automated warehouses (each the size of 18 supermarkets), 30-minute delivery windows, expansion to 10 cities before ONE was profitable. Orders never came close to capacity, the warehouses ran at ~30%. From $8B valuation to bankruptcy in 18 months; 2,000 people lost jobs in a single day. Twenty years later, Instacart won the same market with zero warehouses."},"theranos-board":{"origin":"In 2001, Elizabeth Holmes, a 19-year-old Stanford dropout, recruited former Secretary of State George Shultz onto her board. Within a decade Theranos had collected two secretaries of state, two senators, four generals and a former CDC director, none of them doctors, none of them laboratory scientists.","fall":"Katerra would 'do to construction what Amazon did to retail': factories building entire buildings like products, owning design, materials, and assembly end-to-end. SoftBank poured in ~$2B; Katerra bought 30+ companies, opened global offices, took hundreds of projects simultaneously, while never mastering ONE: projects late, factories misaligned with actual demand, acquisitions undigested. COVID delays finished it; bankruptcy in 2021."},"zume-pizza":{"origin":"In 2015, Julia Collins and Alex Garden founded Zume Pizza in Mountain View, pizzas assembled by robots and baked in ovens aboard delivery trucks, finishing the bake en route. It raised $365 million and imagined a kitchen with no cooks at all.","fall":"Zume's vision: robots make pizza in trucks that BAKE EN ROUTE, arriving fresh. SoftBank valued it at $2.25B. Reality: cheese slid off during cornering (physics doesn't take funding rounds), the ovens-in-trucks concept hit regulatory walls, and the robots were slower than humans. Pivoted to compostable packaging, burned through the rest, shut the pizza business entirely."},"fast-checkout":{"origin":"One-click checkout startup Fast raised $120M+ (Stripe led) on charisma-heavy founder Domm Holland. The company spent like a unicorn, big salaries, sponsorships, a huge engineering org, while generating roughly $600,000 in ANNUAL revenue.","fall":"When the next round didn't come, it collapsed in days. The post-mortem became Silicon Valley's favorite cautionary tweet: they'd built a rocket-ship org around a product the market barely used."},"clinkle-app":{"origin":"In 2012, Stanford senior Lucas Duplan raised $25 million, then the largest seed round in Silicon Valley history, for Clinkle, a phone-payment app demoed in his dorm room. Backers included Sir Richard Branson, Accel and Intel; the campus buzz made him the face of Gen-Z fintech.","fall":"22-year-old Lucas Duplan raised Silicon Valley's largest-ever seed round ($25M, Branson, Peter Thiel, a16z) for a payments app so secret even employees weren't sure what it did. Years of stealth, executive exodus (the entire senior team quit in waves), pivots from payments to a debit card to a lottery-ish rewards thing, and essentially no launch. The name became a verb for vaporware."},"coolest-cooler":{"origin":"The Coolest Cooler, blender, speakers, USB charger in one cooler, raised $13M from 62,000 backers, then Kickstarter's record.","fall":"But the $185 pledge price was BELOW the actual build cost, the blender motor supplier fell through, tariffs hit, and the company started selling coolers on Amazon at $400 to fund backer units, enraging the very people who'd funded it. It died in 2019 with 20,000 backers empty-handed."},"jawbone-slow":{"origin":"Jawbone made beautiful Bluetooth headsets, speakers, and the UP fitness band, peaking at a $3.2B valuation.","fall":"But the UP bands failed at scale (batteries, syncing), triggering massive returns and a legendary 'no-questions refund' that torched cash. Instead of fixing manufacturing, it litigated against Fitbit and chased new categories. Silicon Valley's second-biggest VC-backed death at the time."},"movie-pass":{"origin":"In 2011, Stacy Spikes and Hamet Watt founded MoviePass, a card letting people see a movie a day in theatres, with theatres refusing to share revenue. In 2017, data-analytics CEO Mitch Lowe bought the company, cut the price to $9.95 a month, and added three million subscribers in a year.","fall":"MoviePass dropped its price to $9.95/month for a-movie-a-DAY, while paying theaters FULL PRICE (~$9+) for every ticket. One heavy user could cost $200/month against $10 of revenue. Subscribers exploded to 3 million (of course); losses hit $20M+ per MONTH. The death spiral included blacking out popular films and changing terms mid-subscription, burning users' trust before the cash ran out. Parent company stock fell 99.9%."},"kozmo-delivery":{"origin":"In 1998, 22-year-old Joseph Park founded Kozmo.com in New York: order DVDs, ice cream or a phone charger online, and a bike messenger delivered within the hour, free. By 2000 it was in eleven cities, had Amazon as a partner, and its orange bags were on every doorstep in Manhattan.","fall":"Kozmo promised free one-hour delivery of ANYTHING in cities, a DVD, a Snickers, one magazine, with no minimum order and no delivery fee. Couriers biked $1.50 items across Manhattan at a $10 cost per trip. Amazon invested $60M. The company added a minimum-order rule only in its final months, far too late, and died in the dot-com winter, a cautionary tale that took 20 years to become... every quick-commerce startup's pitch deck."},"quirky-invention":{"origin":"Quirky crowdsourced inventions: the community voted, Quirky manufactured, a new product every week, from egg-separating gadgets to the Wink smart hub. GE partnered; $185M flowed in.","fall":"But shipping ~50 products a YEAR across unrelated categories meant mastering none: flopped products (a $80 Bluetooth speaker-cum-picnic-cooler), inventory pileups, and razor-thin retail margins. Bankruptcy in 2015."},"beepi-cars":{"origin":"Beepi promised to reinvent used-car buying: white-glove inspections, purple bows on delivery. Behind the bows: burning $17M a MONTH, ~300 staff paid above-market, luxurious offices, a cost structure from 2021 funding fantasies running on 2016 realities.","fall":"When markets tightened, rescue merger talks failed twice, and Beepi liquidated, its playbook later executed profitably by leaner rivals."},"homejoy-cleaning":{"origin":"In 2010, siblings Adora and Aaron Cheung built Homejoy in San Francisco, book a cleaner online like a taxi, at a third of agency prices. Y Combinator picked it up; by 2014 it operated in 35 cities across North America and Europe and was the face of the 'gig economy' gold rush.","fall":"Homejoy sold first-time home cleanings at $19 (cost: ~$85) via daily-deal sites, growth exploded, retention didn't: deal-hunters never paid full price, and worse, happy customers simply hired their cleaner DIRECTLY, cutting Homejoy out. Contractor-classification lawsuits added the final weight. Shut down in 2015 while its cleaners kept the clients."},"shyp-shipping":{"origin":"In 2013, Kevin Gibbon, selling shoes on eBay from his Army barracks room, founded Shyp to end packaging agony: photograph an item, and a courier arrives to box, label and ship it. By 2015 it had raised $50 million and expanded to four cities, with NBA star Kevin Durant among the investors.","fall":"Shyp picked up anything for $5, packed it professionally, and shipped it, magic UX, brutal math: the $5 pickup often cost $20+ to perform. Praised by press, beloved by users (of course, it was subsidized), it expanded to four cities before admitting the model needed B2B pivot... too late. The founder's post-mortem was admirably blunt: growth-first thinking killed unit-economics thinking."},"sprig-food":{"origin":"In 2013, Udemy co-founder Gagan Biyani launched Sprig in San Francisco: farm-to-table meals engineered by nutritionists, cooked in central kitchens and delivered in fifteen minutes, healthy fast food with no tipping, no cooking and no waiting.","fall":"Sprig cooked healthy meals in its OWN kitchens and delivered with its OWN drivers, full-stack food. Every layer added cost: chefs, kitchens, fleet, support. A $10 meal cost ~$32 to make and deliver. Even at scale in San Francisco, the best-case market, the math never crossed. Founder Gagan Biyani's post-mortem became required reading: 'the more we grew, the more money we lost.'"},"yik-yak":{"origin":"In 2013, two Atlanta roommates, Brooks Buffington and Tyler Droll, former? 'In 2013, two Furman University fraternity brothers, Brooks Buffington and Tyler Droll, launched Yik Yak: anonymous, hyper-local posts visible to everyone within a mile and a half. It exploded across American high schools and colleges, a million users in months, valued at $400 million within two years.'","fall":"Yik Yak, anonymous posts visible within 5 miles, conquered US campuses in months and hit a $400M valuation. Anonymity + school proximity = predictable outcome: bullying and threats, school district bans, campuses blocking it. The 'fix' (removing anonymity) deleted the app's entire reason to exist; users fled instantly. Sold for parts (~$1M) to Square."},"secret-app":{"origin":"In 2013, ex-Google engineers David Byttow and Chrys Bader launched Secret: anonymous confessions shared with friends of friends, the app Silicon Valley used to leak its own layoffs, dating lives and fundraising gossip. It raised $35 million in 18 months and topped charts worldwide within a year.","fall":"Anonymous-sharing app Secret hit Silicon Valley like gossip lightning, and founders David Byttow and Chrys Bader-Wechseler cashed out $6M PERSONALLY in a funding round months after launch. The app's toxicity problem (harassment behind anonymity) mirrored Yik Yak's; usage cratered; 16 months after launch it shut down. Byttow returned some money, the cash-out remained the era's cautionary symbol."},"better-place":{"origin":"In 2007, software entrepreneur Shai Agassi launched Better Place with $200 million and the boldest electric-car plan ever: skip the battery, swap it, robotic stations exchanging depleted packs in ninety seconds. Israel and Denmark signed on; Renault built the Fluence Z.E. just for it.","fall":"Better Place built battery-SWAP stations for electric cars: drive in, robot exchanges your depleted battery, drive on. It required carmakers to standardize batteries (only Renault half-agreed, with one model), countries to build station networks (Israel and Denmark tried), and drivers to not own their batteries. Fewer than 1,500 cars ever used it. Tesla chose charging; physics and simplicity won."},"solyndra-solar":{"origin":"Solyndra's cylindrical solar tubes were clever, no scarce polysilicon needed, no tilting mounts. The entire thesis rested on polysilicon staying expensive.","fall":"Then China scaled production and polysilicon prices fell ~90%; suddenly ordinary flat panels were cheaper than Solyndra's tubes could ever be. The company kept building a $733M factory anyway. Bankruptcy wiped $1.2B of private capital and a $535M federal loan."},"byjus-empire":{"origin":"In 2011, teacher Byju Raveendran, a village engineer's son who tutored friends for the CAT entrance exam, turned his classroom into Byju's: video lessons that made India's hardest exams feel like storytelling. A decade later it was the world's most valuable edtech, official sponsor of the FIFA World Cup, valued at $22 billion.","fall":"India's most valuable startup rode COVID-era edtech mania to $22B: acquisitions (Aakash, WhiteHat Jr, Great Learning, $2.5B+ spree), cricket sponsorships, Messi as ambassador. Underneath: aggressive sales tactics selling costly courses to families who couldn't afford them, audited financials perpetually delayed, an $85M+ FIFA sponsorship as revenue collapsed, and a $1.2B loan in default. Investors wrote holdings to zero; the founder's stake evaporated."},"wirecard-hole":{"origin":"In 1999, Wirecard began in Munich as a payment processor for gambling and porn sites banks wouldn't touch. Under Markus Braun it grew into a DAX-30 fintech colossus, €2 billion of claimed profits, a Mastercard partnership, 3,500 employees across 26 countries. Its Asian third-party-acquirer profits, the engine of it all, were unverifiable.","fall":"Germany's fintech champion, a DAX-30 member worth more than Deutsche Bank, claimed €1.9B in Philippine trustee accounts. Auditors finally checked: the money didn't exist; the banks had never heard of the accounts. Journalists (FT's Dan McCrum) had reported the fraud for YEARS, and German regulators responded by investigating the JOURNALISTS and banning short-selling. COO Jan Marsalek fled and remains one of Interpol's most wanted."},"nikola-truck":{"origin":"In 2015, Trevor Milton founded Nikola, named, ambitiously, after Tesla's own first name, promising electric and hydrogen semi-trucks. The 2016 Nikola One reveal stunned the trucking world: a sleek cab, a jet-black stage, and a rolling film that appeared to show the truck driving on an open highway.","fall":"Nikola's promo video showed its hydrogen truck cruising, actually it was TOWED to a hilltop and ROLLED down in neutral, filmed at an angle to look self-propelled. At peak, the pre-revenue company was briefly worth more than Ford. Short-seller Hindenburg's report unraveled it; GM's partnership collapsed; founder Trevor Milton was convicted of securities fraud in 2022."},"terra-luna":{"origin":"In 2018, Korean pianist-turned-programmer Do Kwon co-founded Terraform Labs. Terra was a payments blockchain; LUNA its governing coin; UST its masterpiece, a 'stablecoin' pegged not by reserves but by an algorithm: mint LUNA to burn UST, forever. By 2022 UST held $18 billion, and the Anchor protocol paid 20% interest on it.","fall":"TerraUSD was an 'algorithmic stablecoin', pegged to $1 not by reserves but by a mint-and-burn dance with sister token Luna, plus a 20% yield (Anchor) attracting billions in deposits. Critics called it a circular machine; founder Do Kwon called critics 'poor.' In May 2022 large withdrawals broke the peg; the algorithm hyperinflated Luna from $80 to $0.0001 in days. $60B evaporated; retail savers worldwide were wiped out; Kwon was later arrested."},"blackberry-touch":{"origin":"BlackBerry owned mobile email, 50% US smartphone share, presidents and CEOs addicted to the keyboard.","fall":"When the iPhone launched, co-CEO Mike Lazaridis reportedly took one apart in disbelief but the company's verdict was public confidence: businesspeople need physical keys; a virtual keyboard can't do real work; carriers would never allow such data hogs. Every quarter of denial armed Apple and Android further. By 2013, the OS was a rounding error."},"toysrus-amazon":{"origin":"In 2000, Toys R Us signed a 10-year deal making Amazon its EXCLUSIVE online store, paying Amazon $50M/year to not build its own e-commerce. Amazon took the toy data, learned the market, and let competitors on the platform anyway (courts later agreed, Toys R Us won $51M, and lost the internet decade).","fall":"Then a 2005 leveraged buyout loaded $5B of debt: interest payments consumed the cash that stores and websites needed. The 2017 bankruptcy killed an icon."},"gowork-gm":{"origin":"In 1990, Apple spun out its most magical engineers into General Magic, a secret company with one vision: a personal communicator in every pocket, touchscreen, email, shopping, emoticons. Sony, Motorola, AT&T and Philips paid millions for the right to partner on it, a decade before the iPhone.","fall":"Spun out of Apple in 1990 with its most brilliant engineers, General Magic built... the smartphone: touchscreen, apps, emoji, e-commerce, cloud sync, in 1994. Partners (Sony, Motorola, AT&T) shipped devices; consumers, who barely had email, shrugged. The web arrived and ate its proprietary network. It died quietly, while alumni Tony Fadell (iPod/iPhone), Andy Rubin (Android), and Megan Smith went on to build the exact future it prophesied."},"newton-southsea":{"origin":"In 1720, Isaac Newton, history's greatest scientist, Master of the Royal Mint, bought into the South Sea Company, sold early with a 100% profit, and pronounced the mania madness. Then, through the summer, he watched poorer friends grow richer, and bought back in near the absolute peak, three times larger.","fall":"The greatest scientific mind in history invested in the South Sea Company, wisely sold early with a 100% profit, then watched friends keep getting richer as the bubble inflated. Consumed by FOMO, he bought back in near the absolute peak with far more money. The bubble collapsed within weeks and Newton lost around £20,000, a fortune, roughly his entire life savings."},"twain-typesetter":{"origin":"In 1880, Mark Twain, America's most celebrated writer, rich from royalties and lecture tours, saw James Paige's automatic typesetting machine and pronounced it the future of print. He invested, then kept investing for fourteen years, bankrolling a contraption of 18,000 parts that was forever six months from working.","fall":"The world's most famous author poured today's-equivalent of $9 million into the Paige Compositor, a typesetting machine with 18,000 parts that was perpetually 'almost ready.' For 14 years he kept funding it, while turning DOWN the chance to invest in Alexander Graham Bell's telephone. The machine was obsolete on arrival; Twain went bankrupt and spent years on lecture tours repaying debts he wasn't legally required to repay."},"tyson-broke":{"origin":"In 1986, the youngest heavyweight champion in history unified the belts and became the most feared man on the planet, every fight a pay-per-view event worth $30 million a night. Don King promoted; the entourage multiplied: managers, minders, hangers-on, lawyers, tigers.","fall":"The youngest heavyweight champion ever earned over $300 million, and filed for bankruptcy at 37, owing $23 million. The inventory of the burn: two Bengal tigers ($140k each, plus $125k/year care), a $2M bathtub, dozens of cars, mansions he barely visited, and an entourage of hundreds on payroll. He later admitted he never once looked at his own bank statements."},"hammer-broke":{"origin":"In 1990, Oakland rapper MC Hammer turned 'U Can't Touch This' into the fastest-selling record in history, 18 million copies, Grammys, Saturday-morning cartoons and a toy line. He employed 300 people, built a $12 million mansion with a bowling alley, and kept a stable of racehorses.","fall":"'U Can't Touch This' made Hammer worth $33 million. He then built a $30M mansion (with gold-plated gates and a bowling alley), bought 19 racehorses, and, the legendary part, kept an entourage of roughly 200 friends and family on payroll costing $500,000 a month. Five years after his peak, he was $13 million in debt."},"cage-dinosaur":{"origin":"By 1995, Nicolas Cage was Hollywood's highest-paid actor, $20 million a film, and he collected like a pharaoh: yachts, jets, the Shah of Iran's Lamborghini, a 67-million-year-old dinosaur skull he outbid Leonardo DiCaprio for, a pet octopus, two albino king cobras, and castles in Germany and Rhode Island.","fall":"Cage earned over $150 million and spent it on: two European castles, an island in the Bahamas, 15 mansions, a fleet of yachts, shrunken pygmy heads, two king cobras, and a $276,000 Tarbosaurus skull, which turned out to be stolen from Mongolia and had to be returned. By 2009 he owed the IRS $6.3 million and spent the next decade doing back-to-back movies to dig out."},"becker-broke":{"origin":"In 1985, an unseeded 17-year-old named Boris Becker won Wimbledon, the youngest men's champion ever, the first German, diving across the grass like a man possessed. By 1989 he had three Wimbledon titles and roughly $50 million in winnings and endorsements. The tax advisers came next.","fall":"The youngest-ever Wimbledon champion earned over $25M in prize money and far more in endorsements. Bad investments, luxury properties, expensive divorces, and Nigerian oil ventures drained it all. Declared bankrupt in 2017, he then hid assets from his creditors, trophies included, and in 2022 a British court sentenced him to prison for it."},"schilling-38":{"origin":"In 2008, Boston ace Curt Schilling, the blood-soaked sock of the 2004 World Series, retired with $50 million earned and started 38 Studios, named after his jersey number, to build an epic fantasy game. Rhode Island handed him a $75 million state loan to move the company to Providence.","fall":"Baseball legend Curt Schilling put essentially his entire $50M career earnings into 38 Studios, his fantasy video-game company. No industry experience, one game shipped, massive state loans from Rhode Island, and when the money ran out mid-development of the second game, the company collapsed in weeks, taking his fortune, 400 jobs, and years of lawsuits with it."},"whittaker-lottery":{"origin":"In 2002, West Virginia construction contractor Jack Whittaker, already a millionaire, won America's biggest single-ticket lottery jackpot: $315 million. He drove to the counter in his Stetson, gave $14 million to charity, and carried a briefcase of cash; from there, it all went down.","fall":"Whittaker was already a millionaire contractor when he won America's then-biggest single jackpot: $315 million. Within five years: robbed of $545,000 sitting in his car outside a strip club, sued over 400 times, family relationships destroyed, and personal tragedies that followed the money. 'I wish I'd torn that ticket up,' he told reporters, and meant it."},"best-quote":{"origin":"In 1963, a 17-year-old from Belfast with slicked hair and impossible balance scored on his Manchester United debut. By 1968 George Best was European Footballer of the Year, 'the fifth Beatle', the most photographed man in England, earning more in a week than the prime minister made in a year.","fall":"One of football's greatest talents earned fortunes at Manchester United and delivered history's most honest financial statement: 'I spent a lot of money on booze, birds and fast cars. The rest I just squandered.' The joke was real: bankruptcy in 1982, and a career that peaked at 27, the talent undone not by rivals but by the lifestyle."},"wayne-apple":{"origin":"In April 1976, Ronald Wayne, the eldest of the three, the only one with savings and drafting skills, joined Jobs and Wozniak in founding Apple Computer, drew its first logo, and wrote the partnership agreement. Twelve days later, haunted by a previous venture's debts, he sold his 10% for $800.","fall":"Apple's forgotten third co-founder drew the first logo, wrote the partnership agreement, and 12 days later sold his 10% stake back for $800, fearing liability for the company's debts (he was the only founder with assets to lose). That stake would today be worth around $300 billion. He later sold his original Apple contract copy for $500; it re-sold at auction for $1.6 million."},"anil-ambani":{"origin":"In 2005, the Ambani empire split: Anil Ambani, the younger, flashier brother, walked away with telecom, power, infrastructure and finance, assets worth billions, while Mukesh kept oil and gas. Anil promised the sky: India's fastest-growing telecom, the country's biggest IPO for its power arm, Bollywood production, an F1? '...and a stake in Spielberg's studio.'","fall":"In 2008, Anil Ambani was the world's 6th richest man at $42 billion. A decade of debt-fueled bets, telecom price wars, power projects, defense ventures, collapsed one by one. Reliance Communications went bankrupt; in 2020 he told a London court his net worth was zero. His brother Mukesh, who took the boring cash-generating businesses in the family split, became Asia's richest man in the same decade."},"livermore-crash":{"origin":"In 1891, a 14-year-old farm boy named Jesse Livermore took a job posting stock quotes at a Boston brokerage and discovered he could read the tape like language. By 23 he was shorting bucket shops out of business; by 30 he had made, and lost, several fortunes.","fall":"Livermore shorted the 1929 crash and made $100 million in days, perhaps history's greatest single trade, while the world burned. By 1934 he was bankrupt: bigger bets, broken rules (his OWN published rules), and the emotional spiral of chasing past glory. The greatest trader alive was destroyed by the only opponent he couldn't read: himself."},"madoff-ponzi":{"origin":"In 1960, Bernard Madoff founded a Wall Street market-maker with $5,000 saved from lifeguarding and installing sprinklers. By the 1990s Madoff Investment Securities ran the third-largest market-making firm on Wall Street, he helped build NASDAQ itself, while his 'advisory' arm returned a serene 1% a month, rain or shine.","fall":"Madoff was NASDAQ's former chairman, running a legitimate business earning $25M+ a year, and simultaneously the largest Ponzi scheme in history: $65 billion in fake account statements, paying old investors with new investors' money for three decades. The 2008 crisis triggered $7B in withdrawals he couldn't fake. He confessed to his sons; they turned him in within 24 hours. He died in prison; his family was destroyed."},"enron-culture":{"origin":"In 1985, Houston Natural Gas merged with InterNorth to create Enron, a pipeline company. Under Jeffrey Skilling and Kenneth Lay it reinvented itself as 'the world's leading energy company', trading gas futures, then electricity, then weather, then bandwidth, and named 'America's Most Innovative Company' six years running.","fall":"America's 'most innovative company' six years running was a hallucination of accounting: hidden debts in shell partnerships, fake revenue, and a 'rank and yank' culture that fired the bottom 15% yearly, ensuring nobody dared report the truth. Employees were encouraged to load retirement accounts with Enron stock; when it collapsed from $90 to under $1, thousands lost jobs and life savings simultaneously."},"theranos-holmes":{"origin":"In 2003, a 19-year-old Stanford chemical-engineering dropout named Elizabeth Holmes filed a patent for a wearable patch that could diagnose disease and adjust dosage. She dropped out with her tuition money and founded Real-Time Cures, soon Theranos, promising one drop of blood, hundreds of tests.","fall":"Holmes promised 200+ blood tests from a single finger-prick. The technology never worked, Theranos secretly ran samples on Siemens machines while raising $945M from investors (Murdoch, Walton family, Kissinger on the board) who never saw audited financials. Real patients got wrong results about real diseases. Whistleblowers, two 20-something employees, brought down the $9B company. Holmes got 11 years."},"ftx-sbf":{"origin":"In 2017, MIT-grad trader Sam Bankman-Fried, vegan, sleeping on beanbags under his desk, wild hair, founded Alameda Research to arbitrage Bitcoin's 40% price gap between American and Korean exchanges. In 2019 he built FTX, the exchange 'by traders, for traders', and became crypto's favourite wonderkid.","fall":"FTX was crypto's most trusted exchange, Super Bowl ads, stadium names, congressional testimony. Behind it: customer deposits secretly funneled to sister fund Alameda, accounting done partly on QuickBooks, and a company that didn't know how many employees it had. One leaked balance sheet triggered a bank run; $32 billion evaporated in ten days. SBF got 25 years."},"harshad-mehta":{"origin":"Harshad Mehta arrived in Mumbai in the late 1970s with little more than a B.Com and hustled as a jobber's clerk. By 1990 'Big Bull' was the market's folk hero, the man who took ACC cement from ₹200 to ₹9,000 on 'replacement cost' logic, living in a sea-facing penthouse with a Lexus no Indian could buy.","fall":"The 'Amitabh Bachchan of the stock market' discovered banks would issue fake bank receipts, and used them to pump ₹4,000+ crore of banking money into stocks, driving the Sensex up 274% in a year. Journalist Sucheta Dalal's exposé collapsed the market 40%, wiped out lakhs of small investors who'd followed him like a god, and rewrote India's market regulations (SEBI got real teeth because of him)."},"satyam-raju":{"origin":"In 1987, B. Ramalinga Raju founded Satyam ('truth' in Sanskrit) from Hyderabad with 20 employees, growing it into India's fourth-largest IT giant, a $2 billion NYSE-listed firm with 53,000 employees, serving a third of the Fortune 500. Raju was the industry's statesman: philanthropist, sports patron, CEO of the year.","fall":"Satyam was India's 4th-largest IT company, and ₹7,000 crore of its cash simply didn't exist. For years, founder Raju inflated profits slightly each quarter to please markets; each lie required a bigger one. His resignation letter became legend: 'It was like riding a tiger, not knowing how to get off without being eaten.' 50,000 employees' futures hung in the balance overnight."},"ponzi-original":{"origin":"In 1919, Italian immigrant Charles Ponzi, years in and out of Canadian prisons behind him, received a letter from Spain carrying an International Reply Coupon. Buy coupons cheap in Italy, redeem them at American post-office rates: a 400% profit on paper. He opened the Securities Exchange Company in Boston, promising 50% in 45 days.","fall":"Ponzi promised 50% returns in 45 days via international postal coupons, a real arbitrage worth pennies that could never scale. New deposits paid old investors; at peak he collected $1M per WEEK from working-class Boston. It lasted eight months. Investors got back ~30 cents on the dollar; six banks collapsed; his name became the crime."},"gupta-insider":{"origin":"Gupta ran McKinsey, the most trusted position in global business, sat on Goldman Sachs' board, co-founded the Indian School of Business, and was worth $100 million.","fall":"Then he phoned hedge-fund friend Raj Rajaratnam 16 seconds after a confidential Goldman board call, passing inside information. He earned nothing directly from the tips. He wanted entry into the billionaire circle. He got prison, and his name became a business-school warning."},"armstrong-doping":{"origin":"In 1996, 25-year-old Lance Armstrong was the world's top-ranked cyclist, then metastatic testicular cancer spread through his lungs and brain, 50-50 odds to live. Three years later, having co-founded the Lance Armstrong Foundation with the yellow LIVE STRONG band, he won the Tour de France. And then won six more.","fall":"Cancer survivor, seven-time Tour de France champion, global inspiration, and the operator of 'the most sophisticated doping program in sports history.' Worse than the doping was the machine protecting it: Armstrong sued journalists who told the truth, destroyed teammates who confessed, and lied under oath for a decade. The 2013 Oprah confession came only after the evidence was overwhelming. Every title: stripped."},"cronje-fixing":{"origin":"In the 1990s, Hansie Cronje was the most beloved cricketer in South Africa, captain of the national side from 26, the man who led the Proteas back into world cricket after apartheid, reading scripture to teammates and shaking hands in the rain the day he dropped his bat.","fall":"South Africa's beloved cricket captain, the born-again Christian, the leader of leaders, was caught on Delhi police wiretaps fixing matches for bookmakers. His initial confession: he'd taken money but 'never fixed a game'; the King Commission revealed years of payments, and teammates he'd tried to recruit into throwing matches. Banned for life at 30. 'An unfortunate love of money,' he called it himself."},"fyre-festival":{"origin":"In 2016, rapper Ja Rule and tech entrepreneur Billy McFarland, whose 'Fyre' app promised to let anyone book music celebrities, launched a festival to promote it: private jets to Norman's Cay in the Exumas, supermodels painting the map orange, luxury villas, gourmet food, two weekends of it.","fall":"Fyre Festival sold $12,000 luxury packages via supermodel Instagram posts, private island, villas, gourmet chefs. Reality on arrival: disaster-relief tents, cheese sandwiches in foam boxes, no water, no acts, no way home. McFarland had known for months it couldn't be delivered and kept selling, then launched a NEW ticket scam while awaiting sentencing for the first one."},"napoleon-russia":{"origin":"In June 1812, Napoleon crossed the Neman with the largest army Europe had ever assembled, 600,000+ men from twenty nations, the Grande Armée of the world's greatest general, undefeated in a decade. Russia's Tsar Alexander had broken the Continental Blockade; the campaign was expected to be a parade.","fall":"Master of Europe, undefeated in his own mind, Napoleon marched the Grande Armée, over 600,000 men, into Russia against every advisor's warning. The Russians retreated, burned their own cities (including Moscow), and let winter fight for them. Roughly 100,000 combat deaths; the rest starved and froze on the retreat. Fewer than 100,000 returned. Europe smelled weakness; two years later: Elba."},"titanic-hubris":{"origin":"In 1909, Belfast's Harland & Wolff laid down RMS Titanic, the largest moving object humans had ever built: 882 feet, swimming pool, Turkish baths, a grand staircase of oak and crystal. White Star's marketing promised not speed but luxury and safety; the Shipbuilder magazine declared her 'practically unsinkable'.","fall":"The 'unsinkable' ship carried lifeboats for barely half its passengers, why waste deck space on the impossible? It received six ice warnings on April 14th and maintained near-full speed through a known ice field, chasing a crossing record. The nearest ship's radio operator had been told to shut up by Titanic's overworked operators earlier that night, and had gone to bed."},"fouquet-party":{"origin":"In 1661, Nicolas Fouquet, Louis XIV's finance minister and the most elegant man in France, threw a housewarming at his château Vaux-le-Vicomte: fountains, a Molière premiere, fireworks over gold plate for 6,000 guests. The 22-year-old king had never seen anything so beautiful, or so dangerous.","fall":"France's finance minister threw history's most magnificent housewarming at his château Vaux-le-Vicomte, fountains, gold plates, Molière premiering a play, to impress young King Louis XIV. Louis saw a subject living better than his king, concluded (correctly or not) it was funded by embezzlement, and arrested him within weeks. Fouquet died after 19 years in prison. Louis then hired Fouquet's architect, painter, and gardener to build... Versailles."},"hughes-isolation":{"origin":"Howard Hughes set airspeed records, flew around the world in 91 hours, made Hollywood's most expensive film, and bought TWA, RKO and a chunk of Las Vegas, the richest, most photographed man in America. By his forties the germs, the painkillers and the OCD had begun to close the door.","fall":"The world's richest man, aviator, filmmaker, industrialist, spent his final decade in blacked-out hotel penthouses: unwashed, storing his own waste in jars, watching the same films on loop, surrounded only by paid aides instructed never to speak first. His empire was looted by handlers while he issued memos about tissue-box protocols. He died on a plane weighing 90 pounds; the FBI needed fingerprints to identify him."},"wework-neumann":{"origin":"In 2010, Adam Neumann and Miguel McKelvey opened the first WeWork in SoHo, shared desks, exposed brick, free cold-brew and community. A decade later it leased 80 million square feet across 120 cities, SoftBank had valued it at $47 billion, and Neumann was selling 'elevating the world's consciousness'.","fall":"Neumann sold investors a real-estate subletting company as a tech platform to 'elevate the world's consciousness.' He trademarked 'We' personally and charged the company $5.9M for it, took loans against his own inflated stock, and flew a private jet with a cereal box of marijuana. The IPO filing exposed everything, losses of $1.6B, self-dealing throughout, and the valuation collapsed from $47B to $8B in weeks. He left with a nine-figure exit package while employees' options went to zero."},"quibi-flop":{"origin":"In 2020, DreamWorks founder Jeffrey Katzenberg and former HP and eBay CEO Meg Whitman raised $1.75 billion for Quibi: Hollywood-quality shows cut into ten-minute 'quick bites' for phones, premiering with Steven Spielberg, Chrissy Teigen and Liam Hemsworth on the marquee.","fall":"Hollywood legend Jeffrey Katzenberg and tech royalty Meg Whitman raised $1.75 billion for 'quick bites', premium 10-minute shows for phones, before a single user validated the idea. Launched April 2020 (lockdowns: nobody commuting), no screenshots allowed (killing all social sharing), couldn't cast to TVs, competing against free TikTok. Six months later: shut down. Possibly the fastest ever destruction of that much startup capital."},"ratner-crap":{"origin":"In 1984, Gerald Ratner inherited his family's jewellery shop and built it into Britain's biggest jewellery group, 2,500 stores on every high street, £1.1 billion in value, and a chairman famous for jokes. In April 1991, 5,000 businesspeople paid to hear him speak at the Grosvenor House hotel.","fall":"Ratner built Britain's biggest jewelry chain, then, in one speech to 5,000 businesspeople, joked that his products were 'total crap' and that his earrings were 'cheaper than a prawn sandwich but probably wouldn't last as long.' The press feasted. Customers, humiliated for owning his products, vanished. £500 million of value evaporated; he was forced out of his own company; 'doing a Ratner' entered the dictionary."},"khwarezm-envoys":{"origin":"By 1218, Genghis Khan had conquered the Jin empire and reopened the Silk Road under Mongol protection. He sent 450 merchants with a gold-laden caravan to open trade with the Khwarezmian Empire, the richest Muslim power in Central Asia, with a message: I want commerce, not conquest.","fall":"Genghis Khan sent the wealthy Khwarezmian Empire a trade caravan and offer of peace, he wanted commerce, not war, having just conquered China. A governor seized the caravan and killed the merchants. Genghis sent three ambassadors requesting justice; the Shah beheaded one and burned the others' beards. The Mongol response erased the empire: cities that resisted were annihilated, rivers rerouted over capitals. Millions died. The Shah fled and died on a Caspian island."},"caesar-ides":{"origin":"By 44 BC, Julius Caesar was dictator perpetuo, conqueror of Gaul, victor of the civil war, his face on coins while the Republic's institutions bowed. The Senate kept voting him honours: statue among the kings, month renamed July, gold chair in the House. Behind the flattery, a conspiracy of sixty gathered, senators he had pardoned.","fall":"A soothsayer warned him about the Ides of March. His wife Calpurnia dreamed of his murder and begged him to stay home. A note detailing the entire plot was pressed into his hand on the walk to the Senate, he didn't read it. He'd dismissed his bodyguard, declaring it better to die once than live in fear. Twenty-three stab wounds later, the men he'd pardoned and promoted had killed him, led by Brutus, whom he'd treated as a son."},"challenger-launch":{"origin":"In 1986, the space shuttle Challenger carried the first teacher in space, Christa McAuliffe, for a lesson beamed to millions of schoolchildren. The launch had slipped three times over weather; that January morning Florida saw unprecedented cold, 26°F, ice on the pad, and the O-ring seals on the right solid rocket booster had never been tested below 53°F.","fall":"The night before launch, Morton Thiokol engineers begged for a delay: the O-ring seals had never been tested at such cold temperatures and were already showing damage on prior flights. Managers, under schedule pressure with a teacher aboard and the world watching, asked them to 'take off your engineering hat and put on your management hat.' The seals failed 73 seconds after liftoff. The Rogers Commission found the disaster was organizational long before it was mechanical."},"chernobyl-test":{"origin":"In April 1986, Reactor No. 4 of the Chernobyl Nuclear Power Plant, an RBMK design the Soviet Union knew could be unstable at low power, was to run a safety test it had failed three times: could coasting turbines bridge the 70 seconds before backup generators woke? The Kiev grid asked for a nine-hour delay.","fall":"Operators ran a safety test on Reactor 4 with most safety systems manually disabled, pushing the reactor into a state its own designers had warned was unstable, because the test had been ordered, delayed, and had to be completed on this shift. A known design flaw in the control rods (classified secret, hidden even from the operators) turned the emergency shutdown itself into the detonator. The state then spent days denying what dosimeters were screaming."},"kodak-digital":{"origin":"In 1975, Kodak engineer Steve Sasson built the first digital camera from an experimental CCD, scrap parts and a tape reader, eight pounds, 0.01 megapixels, 23 seconds to record. He showed it to executives by taking their portrait. The company that owned 90% of American film profits asked a question he never forgot: 'Why would anyone ever want to look at pictures on a TV?'","fall":"Kodak engineer Steven Sasson built the world's FIRST digital camera in 1975. Management's response: 'That's cute, but don't tell anyone about it.' Film margins were 70%; digital threatened them. Kodak owned the patents, the brand, the labs, and sat on all of it for two decades while others built the future it had invented. Bankruptcy: 2012. The patents were sold to pay creditors."},"blockbuster-netflix":{"origin":"In 2000, Blockbuster was king of American living rooms: $6 billion in revenue, 9,000 stores, and a business model of late fees quietly worth $800 million a year. Netflix CEO Reed Hastings flew to Dallas and offered to run the online business for Blockbuster, reportedly for $50 million. The executives laughed them out of the room.","fall":"In 2000, Netflix's founders flew to Dallas and offered Blockbuster their company for $50 million. CEO John Antioco reportedly struggled not to laugh them out of the room, Netflix was a niche DVD-mail service; Blockbuster had 9,000 stores and $800M/year in LATE FEES alone. That late-fee addiction was exactly the customer pain Netflix existed to solve. Blockbuster: bankrupt 2010. Netflix: worth hundreds of billions."},"nokia-smartphone":{"origin":"In 1998, Nokia, a 130-year-old Finnish paper and rubber conglomerate turned phone maker, passed Motorola to become the world's biggest mobile-phone company. By 2007 it sold four in every ten smartphones on Earth, running Symbian, with its own OS team, its own browser, and total confidence nothing could unseat it.","fall":"Nokia owned half the world's phone market and had a working touchscreen smartphone prototype YEARS before the iPhone, plus an internal report predicting exactly Apple's move. Middle managers, afraid of top management's tempers, softened bad news at every layer; engineers knew Symbian was doomed but saying so was career damage. At the sale to Microsoft, the CEO ended his speech: 'We didn't do anything wrong, but somehow, we lost', and the room wept."},"yahoo-google":{"origin":"In 1997, Yahoo, the web's front door, built by two Stanford grad students, passed on buying a Stanford research project called BackRub. A year later, founders Larry Page and Sergey Brin, exhausted, tried to sell Google itself to Excite for under a million. Yahoo kept licensing their search; by 2002 it finally tried to buy them, for $3 billion. Google asked five.","fall":"The full menu of missed forever-fortunes: 1998, Page and Brin offer Google's search algorithm for $1M; Yahoo passes. 2002, Yahoo could buy Google for ~$3B; balks at $5B. 2006, agrees to buy Facebook for $1B, then lowers the offer to $850M; Zuckerberg walks. 2008, Microsoft offers $44.6B for Yahoo itself; Yahoo refuses. 2017, sold to Verizon for $4.8B."},"xerox-parc":{"origin":"In 1970, Xerox, flush with a decade of copy-machine monopoly profits, opened a research lab 3,000 miles from headquarters in Palo Alto and gave geniuses their heads. By 1973 PARC had built the Alto: windows, icons, a mouse, ethernet, the entire modern computer, working, five years before the Apple II.","fall":"Xerox's research lab invented the graphical interface, the mouse, ethernet, laser printing, and WYSIWYG editing, the complete modern computer, working, in 1973. Headquarters, 3,000 miles away and obsessed with copier margins, couldn't see it. In 1979 they gave Steve Jobs demo access in exchange for pre-IPO stock. Jobs: 'They had no idea what they had.' The Macintosh was born from that tour; Xerox stayed a copier company."},"decca-beatles":{"origin":"On January 1, 1962, five lads from Liverpool drove ten hours through snow to London for an audition with Decca Records. They played fifteen songs in an hour. A month later came the verdict, delivered by A&R man Dick Rowe: 'Guitar groups are on the way out, Mr Epstein.'","fall":"On New Year's Day 1962, four boys from Liverpool auditioned for Decca Records, 15 songs in an hour. Decca's verdict, delivered to their manager: 'Guitar groups are on the way out, Mr. Epstein... The Beatles have no future in show business.' They signed a local band called The Tremeloes instead (closer to London, cheaper travel). The Beatles became the best-selling act in the history of recorded music."},"westernunion-phone":{"origin":"In 1876, Western Union was America's communications empire, a telegraph network spanning the continent, the tech monopoly of its century, with revenues that dwarfed the federal budget. That year, an internal memo assessed a rival invention: 'The telephone has too many shortcomings to be seriously considered as a means of communication.'","fall":"Alexander Graham Bell offered his telephone patent to Western Union, the telegraph monopoly, the most powerful communications company on Earth, for $100,000. An internal committee's legendary assessment: 'This telephone has too many shortcomings to be seriously considered as a means of communication. The device is inherently of no value to us.' Within two years, Western Union was desperately (and unsuccessfully) fighting Bell in court. Bell Telephone became AT&T, for a century, the most valuable company alive."},"aol-timewarner":{"origin":"In January 2000, Steve Case and Gerald Levin announced the merger of the century: AOL, the dial-up internet giant, barely 15 years old and worth $165 billion, would swallow Time Warner, the 80-year-old colossus of magazines, HBO, Warner Bros and CNN. 'The most significant media merger ever'; the combined company would own the internet's future.","fall":"At the dot-com peak, AOL used its inflated stock to buy Time Warner, $350 billion combined, the largest merger ever. The cultures despised each other from day one; the dot-com crash vaporized AOL's value; broadband made AOL's dial-up business a museum piece mid-merger. In 2002 the company posted a $99 billion loss, still among the largest annual corporate losses ever recorded. Both sides spent a decade undoing it."},"sears-amazon":{"origin":"In 1886, railway station agent Richard Sears bought a shipment of watches by accident and sold them for profit. Sears, Roebuck grew into the Wish Book, a catalogue thicker than a phone directory, reaching farmers before roads did, then the first retail store outside city centres, and by 1989, America's biggest retailer.","fall":"Sears WAS Amazon a century early: the catalog that delivered everything to every doorstep in America, even entire houses. It had the brands, the logistics, the customer trust, and (in its Discover card and Prodigy network!) the raw pieces of e-commerce before e-commerce. Instead, hedge-fund management pitted internal divisions against each other in Ayn Rand-style internal markets, starved stores of investment, and financial-engineered the giant to death. Bankruptcy: 2018."},"concorde-fallacy":{"origin":"In 1962, Britain and France signed a treaty to build the world's first supersonic airliner, Mach 2, New York in three hours, the future itself. The budget was £70 million. Development costs ran to six times that, and by 1965 everyone in the programme knew it would never sell enough to repay a penny.","fall":"Britain and France knew by the late 1960s that supersonic passenger flight couldn't be profitable, fuel costs, tiny cabins, sonic-boom bans over land. They finished it anyway: too much invested, too much national pride. It flew 27 years, a magnificent machine losing money on champagne-service for 100 passengers, and gave economics its most famous term: the 'Concorde fallacy', the textbook name for throwing good money after bad."},"mars-orbiter":{"origin":"In 1999, NASA's $327 million Mars Climate Orbiter was ten months into a 416-million-mile journey to study Martian weather, the first weather satellite of another planet, designed to relay data from the polar lander behind it. The navigation team at JPL worked in metric; Lockheed Martin's software in Denver worked in pound-force seconds.","fall":"After a 9-month journey to Mars, the Climate Orbiter burned up in the Martian atmosphere because Lockheed Martin's software sent thrust data in POUND-FORCE while NASA's navigation expected NEWTONS. Multiple navigators had noticed course anomalies for months; the concerns never made it through the formal reporting process. One unit conversion, the kind checked on high-school homework, ended a $327 million mission."},"osborne-effect":{"origin":"Osborne made the first successful portable computer, $70M in year-two sales.","fall":"Then founder Adam Osborne proudly pre-announced the next models: dramatically better, coming soon. Customers did the obvious: stopped buying the current model to wait. Inventory mountains, cash-flow collapse, bankruptcy within months. 'The Osborne Effect' is now the textbook term for announcing your own product's obsolescence."},"schlitz-beer":{"origin":"In the 1970s, Schlitz was America's second-biggest beer, the 'beer that made Milwaukee famous', spending millions on the slogan 'You only go around once in life, so grab all the gusto you can'. But rising ingredient costs were squeezing margins, and the board wanted to close the gap with Budweiser.","fall":"Schlitz, 'the beer that made Milwaukee famous', chased margins by swapping barley for cheap corn syrup, shortening brewing time, and adding a stabilizer that, combined with another ingredient, made the beer form visible FLAKES and go hazy on shelves. Ten million cans secretly recalled. Drinkers noticed the taste before the accountants noticed the savings. The brand never recovered; the breweries were sold off."},"juicero-squeeze":{"origin":"Silicon Valley's finest invested $120M in a $699 (later $399) Wi-Fi-connected machine that squeezed proprietary juice packets.","fall":"Then Bloomberg reporters discovered you could squeeze the packets BY HAND, faster than the machine. The demonstration video ended the company within months. The juice packets, it turned out, were the product; the machine was $400 of ceremony."},"myspace-fall":{"origin":"In 2003, MySpace launched in LA as the place to be anyone: custom profiles, top-eight friends, auto-playing music. By 2006 it was the most-visited website in the United States, bigger than Google. In 2005 News Corp bought it for $580 million, a bargain, everyone agreed.","fall":"In 2006 MySpace passed Google as America's most-visited website. After News Corp's $580M acquisition, the mandate became monetize NOW: ads carpeting every page, features chasing revenue targets, glittering profile chaos, while a clean blue-and-white site for college kids quietly ate its lunch. MySpace optimized quarterly numbers; Facebook optimized the product. By 2009 the race was over; MySpace later sold for $35M."},"mcdonald-kroc":{"origin":"In 1954, Ray Kroc, a 52-year-old milkshake-machine salesman with decades of grind behind him, drove to San Bernardino because one restaurant kept ordering eight of his multi-mixers. The McDonald brothers' Speedee system served a burger in thirty seconds; Kroc saw the golden arches multiplied a thousand times.","fall":"Dick and Mac McDonald invented the fast-food system; Ray Kroc franchised it, then bought them out for $2.7M. In the negotiation, the brothers asked for 0.5% of gross revenues in perpetuity, Kroc agreed, but asked to keep it out of the written contract (a handshake deal, for 'tax reasons'). The royalty was never paid. Today it would be worth well over $100 million a YEAR. Kroc even kept their name, and opened a McDonald's across from their original restaurant to bury it."},"saverin-dilution":{"origin":"In 2004, Harvard junior Eduardo Saverin, the only founder with savings, put $15,000 into TheFacebook for 34% and ran the business side while interning in New York. The site exploded to a million users in ten months; Zuckerberg moved to Palo Alto, fell under Sean Parker's spell, and the investors followed.","fall":"Facebook's co-founder and first funder stayed in New York doing ad-sales internships while Zuckerberg moved to Palo Alto, met Sean Parker, and rebuilt the company around new investors. A restructuring issued new shares that diluted Saverin's 34% stake to near-nothing while others were protected. He'd signed the papers without fully understanding them. The lawsuit settled (he remained a billionaire), but the seat at the table, and the friendship, were gone forever."},"beatles-klein":{"origin":"In 1967, Brian Epstein, who had discovered the Beatles in a Liverpool cellar and choreographed every detail of Beatlemania, died at 32. The band, unsupervised at the peak of their powers, poured money into Apple Corps: boutiques, electronics, a bakery, a school for transcendental meditation.","fall":"After manager Brian Epstein's death, the Beatles' finances were chaos (Apple Corps was bleeding money on boutiques and whims). John, George, and Ringo hired tough-guy manager Allen Klein; Paul wanted his father-in-law's firm. The 3-vs-1 split poisoned everything, Klein's aggressive deals and opaque accounting deepened the mistrust, and the money fights finished what creative tensions had started. Paul sued his own bandmates to escape Klein; the Beatles ended in courtrooms. Klein was later jailed for tax fraud."},"elvis-parker":{"origin":"In 1955, 'Colonel' Tom Parker, a carnival promoter with an invented title and a past he kept locked, became manager of a 20-year-old Memphis truck driver who moved like nobody on earth. Within two years Elvis was the biggest act in the history of entertainment; Parker never lost a negotiation.","fall":"Colonel Tom Parker took 25%, then 50% of Elvis's earnings, the highest management cut in entertainment history. Elvis never toured internationally (fortunes waiting in Europe and Japan) because, it later emerged, Parker was an undocumented immigrant with no passport, and never told him. In 1973 Parker sold the rights to Elvis's entire back catalog, everything before that year, for a fraction of its worth, taking half the proceeds. Elvis never renegotiated, never audited, never left."},"trojan-horse":{"origin":"For ten years the Greeks besieged Troy, the walled city of King Priam, and got nowhere: Hector dead, the war dying of boredom. Then Odysseus, cleverest of the Greeks, had the army pretend to sail home, leaving on the beach a colossal wooden horse, and in its belly the best of the Achaeans.","fall":"Ten years of siege had failed. Then one morning the Greeks were gone, leaving only a giant wooden horse, an 'offering to Athena.' Priest Laocoön warned: 'I fear Greeks, even bearing gifts' (and was promptly eaten by sea serpents, which the Trojans read as divine disapproval of his doubt). Cassandra warned too, cursed to be never believed. Troy dragged the horse inside, partied, slept. The soldiers inside opened the gates. Troy burned."},"kerkoporta-gate":{"origin":"By 1453, Constantinople, the thousand-year capital of the Romans, was reduced to 50,000 people behind the greatest walls on Earth, besieged by Mehmed II's 80,000 with the largest cannons ever cast. The walls held for 53 days. Just inside, near the palace, stood a small postern gate called Kerkoporta, left unbarred during a sortie.","fall":"Constantinople's triple walls had repelled every siege for a thousand years. During the final Ottoman assault, defenders repulsed wave after wave, until someone noticed Ottoman flags INSIDE the walls. The Kerkoporta, a small sally-gate, had been left unlocked after a night sortie. A handful of soldiers slipped through, panic spread faster than the enemy, and the Roman Empire's last chapter ended, through a door somebody forgot to bolt."},"jet-goyal":{"origin":"In 1993, Naresh Goyal, a travel-agent's son who had grown up sleeping in railway stations after his father's death? keep '...who built his trade as a ticketing agent for foreign airlines, launched Jet Airways with four leased Boeing 737s when India first let private airlines fly. Within a decade Jet was India's biggest airline: full meals, a 'Jet Touch' of polish, and 20 million passengers a year.","fall":"Naresh Goyal built India's best full-service airline, then ran it as a personal fiefdom for 25 years: micromanaged decisions, revolving-door CEOs with no real authority, opaque related-party dealings, and refusal to dilute control even as debt mounted past ₹8,000 crore. Etihad wanted to invest more, with governance conditions. Goyal wouldn't cede control. The airline stopped flying in April 2019 with employees unpaid for months."},"sahara-roy":{"origin":"Subrata Roy called himself 'the Managing Worker' of Sahara India Pariwar, an empire of 30 million depositors run like a family, festivals, uniforms, loyalty oaths. He owned an IPL team, a Formula-1 team, Force India, and threw weddings for thousands of employees' children.","fall":"Sahara raised ₹24,000+ crore from millions of small investors through bond schemes SEBI ruled illegal. Instead of settling, Roy waged total war on the regulator for years, defying orders, sending 127 TRUCKS of documents to SEBI's office (allegedly 31,000 cartons) in a compliance stunt, and skipping a Supreme Court appearance. The court's patience ended: two years in Tihar jail, assets seized, the empire dismantled piece by piece."},"maginot-line":{"origin":"After 1918, France, having buried 1.4 million men, built an insurance policy: the Maginot Line, a 450-kilometre subterranean fortress of reinforced concrete, retractable guns, underground railways and hospitals, a decade in the making and impregnable by every lesson of the Great War.","fall":"France spent a decade and 3 billion francs building the world's most sophisticated fortification line against Germany, underground railways, air-conditioned bunkers, retractable turrets. It was genuinely impregnable. So Germany didn't attack it: panzers went AROUND, through the Ardennes forest (declared 'impassable' by French planners) and neutral Belgium. France fell in six weeks with its magnificent forts intact, facing the wrong direction."},"tulip-mania":{"origin":"In the 1630s, Holland's new merchant rich discovered tulips, rare, flamboyant, virus-striped, and a futures market formed in the taverns of Haarlem and Amsterdam. Bulbs were sold by weight while still in the ground, flipped in winter before anyone had seen the flower; a Semper Augustus bulb cost the price of a canal house.","fall":"At the peak of Dutch tulip mania, a single Semper Augustus bulb traded for the price of an Amsterdam canal house. Farmers mortgaged land for bulbs; contracts changed hands ten times a day in tavern 'colleges'; everyone knew someone getting rich. In February 1637, at a routine auction, the bids simply... didn't come. Prices collapsed 99% in weeks. The bulbs still grew perfectly good tulips, it was only the story that died."},"john-law":{"origin":"In 1716, John Law, Scottish gambler, duellist, and the most brilliant monetary theorist of his age, persuaded the Regent of France to let him found a bank printing paper money. Then the Mississippi Company: France's claim to Louisiana, Canada and the trade of half a continent, sold by subscription in Rue Quincampoix.","fall":"A Scottish gambler-economist convinced France to let him print paper money and merge it with his Mississippi Company, shares in the 'boundless riches' of Louisiana swamps. Shares rose 20x; the word 'millionaire' was coined for the winners. Law printed money to prop up shares, shares to prop up money, a doom loop wearing genius's clothes. The 1720 collapse ruined a generation, made 'banque' a dirty word in France for a century, and helped set the fuse for revolution."},"qin-mercury":{"origin":"In 246 BC, a 13-year-old became king of Qin; by 221 BC he had conquered all rival states and named himself Qin Shi Huang, First Emperor. Terrified of death, he banned discussion of mortality and dispatched expeditions to the sea for the elixir of immortality, which alchemists often made with mercury.","fall":"China's first emperor, unifier of the warring states, builder of the Great Wall, became obsessed with living forever. His physicians and alchemists prescribed the era's premium immortality treatment: pills of MERCURY. He sent fleets hunting mythical islands of immortals and consumed quicksilver elixirs daily. The mercury likely destroyed his brain and body; he died at 49 on a tour seeking more elixirs, his death hidden by advisors using carts of rotting fish to mask the smell."},"light-brigade":{"origin":"At Balaklava, October 25, 1854, the British light cavalry, 670 men on horses bred for show jumps, not fortresses, waited above a valley held by Russian guns on three sides. General Raglan sent an order to 'advance rapidly' and 'recover the guns'; the messenger galloped; Lord Lucan squinted at the wrong guns in the valley's end.","fall":"At Balaclava, Lord Raglan ordered cavalry to stop Russians removing captured guns from a hillside, but his vague written order ('advance rapidly to the front... try to prevent the enemy carrying away the guns') never specified WHICH guns. The messenger, when asked, gestured angrily at the wrong valley, one lined on three sides with Russian artillery. Six hundred horsemen charged into the guns; a third fell in twenty minutes. A French marshal watching: 'It is magnificent, but it is not war. It is madness.'"},"easter-island":{"origin":"Rapa Nui's civilization built the moai, 900 giant stone heads, in escalating competition between clans, each statue demanding timber for transport. The palm forests that provided canoes (deep-sea fishing), soil protection, and everything else were cut faster than they grew, for monuments.","fall":"When the last trees fell, no canoes could be built, soil washed away, and the society that carved wonders collapsed into famine. Someone, as the saying goes, cut down the last tree, knowing it was the last."},"jobs-surgery":{"origin":"Jobs's pancreatic tumor was the RARE treatable kind, doctors called it lucky and urged immediate surgery. The man who bent reality in business tried to bend biology: nine months of fruit diets, acupuncture, juice fasts, and a psychic, against the pleading of family and physicians.","fall":"When he finally operated, the cancer had spread. Biographer Isaacson: Jobs later understood the delay may have cost him his life, the reality distortion field's one fatal casualty was its owner."},"mallya-kingfisher":{"origin":"Vijay Mallya inherited United Breweries at 28 and built an empire of lifestyle: Kingfisher beer on every shelf, a Formula-1 team, a golf stable, a seat in Parliament, yachts, and the title 'King of Good Times'. When he launched an airline in 2005, he insisted it would carry the same flag: glamour first.","fall":"The liquor baron launched Kingfisher Airlines as a flying five-star hotel, and ran it like a party: bought loss-making Air Deccan to go international faster, kept lavish service amid fuel spikes, funded losses with ₹9,000 crore of bank loans (much of it personally guaranteed), all while sponsoring F1 teams, IPL cricket, and yacht celebrations as employees went unpaid. The airline died in 2012; Mallya left for London in 2016 and has fought extradition since, 'King of Good Times' turned fugitive."},"siddhartha-ccd":{"origin":"In 1996, V.G. Siddhartha, scion of a Karnataka coffee-growing family, married into political power, opened India's first café chain, Café Coffee Day, on Brigade Road, Bengaluru: 'a lot can happen over coffee'. He owned the plantations, the roasting and the supply chain; by the 2010s there were 1,700 cafés, India's answer to Starbucks before Starbucks arrived.","fall":"The founder of Café Coffee Day built 1,700 cafés and gave a generation of Indians their third place, 'a lot can happen over coffee.' Behind the empire: mounting debt (₹7,000+ crore across entities), pledged shares, tax pressures, and a founder who confided in almost no one. His final letter apologized to his team and spoke of pressure he could no longer bear. India lost him in July 2019; the business, restructured, survived, the debts were solvable. He never let anyone help him solve them."},"depp-wine":{"origin":"Depp earned $650 million and reached the edge of ruin: 14 residences, a 156-foot yacht, 45 luxury cars, $200,000/month in private jets, a $3M cannon to fire Hunter S. Thompson's ashes, and, per his own managers' court filings, $30,000 a month on wine.","fall":"When he sued his management for mishandling money, their defense was an itemized list of his spending. Both sides were, in a sense, right."},"yesbank-kapoor":{"origin":"In 2004, banker Rana Kapoor and his brother-in-law Ashok Kapur won one of India's last new private-bank licences and built Yes Bank into the country's fifth-largest private lender, fastest-growing, tech-forward, courting startups, airlines and stressed companies others wouldn't touch, with Kapoor's name on every award wall.","fall":"Yes Bank's swaggering co-founder famously tweeted his shares were his 'diamonds', he'd never sell. The bank's growth secret: aggressive loans to exactly the borrowers other banks refused (IL&FS, DHFL, Anil Ambani companies, Café Coffee Day), booking fees upfront while the risk compounded. RBI forced him out; bad loans surfaced in tens of thousands of crores; depositors faced frozen withdrawals in 2020's rescue. Kapoor was arrested, the diamonds, sold under distress, had financed the very hole."},"ltcm-geniuses":{"origin":"In 1994, legendary bond trader John Meriwether left Salomon Brothers and assembled Long-Term Capital Management: two Nobel laureates (Merton and Scholes), the Fed's former vice-chair, MIT and LSE PhDs, 'the best finance faculty in the world', trading convergence models with 25-to-1 leverage.","fall":"LTCM's partners included two Nobel laureates whose formulas priced the modern options market. The fund leveraged $4.7B of capital into $1+ TRILLION of exposure on 'impossible-to-lose' convergence bets, their models said a losing year this bad was a once-in-billions-of-years event. Russia defaulted in 1998; correlations went to one; the impossible took four months. The Fed had to orchestrate a bailout to protect the global financial system from math this confident."},"newcoke-formula":{"origin":"In the early 1980s, Coca-Cola was losing the cola war it had invented: Pepsi was winning blind taste tests and the youth market, and Coke's share had slid for fifteen years. So the company, 99 years after the sacred formula was written, ran 200,000 blind tastings of a sweeter, smoother replacement.","fall":"Losing taste tests to Pepsi, Coca-Cola changed its 99-year-old formula, backed by 200,000 taste tests showing people PREFERRED New Coke. The tests measured sips; they didn't measure identity. America revolted: 8,000 angry calls a day, hoarding, protest groups, lawsuits. Seventy-nine days later, 'Coca-Cola Classic' returned to national celebration, a senator announced it on the floor of Congress. Sales then surged past Pepsi anyway."},"edsel-ford":{"origin":"In 1955, Ford Motor Company, riding America's postwar boom, began planning a brand-new middle marque, named after Henry Ford's only son, with two years of research, 18? '...its own division, its own dealer network, and the largest advertising campaign in automotive history. The name was picked from 6,000 candidates; the launch date was set: E-Day, September 4, 1957.'","fall":"Ford spent a decade and $250M researching, designing, and hyping the Edsel, 'the car of the future,' teased for months in ads that showed everything but the car. Launch day revealed: an odd vertical grille (compared unkindly to a toilet seat), a price straddling markets confusingly, quality problems from rushed production, and a 1958 recession killing mid-market demand. Ford projected 200,000 sales in year one; the entire three-year run sold barely 118,000. 'Edsel' became American for flop."},"radioshack-dilution":{"origin":"RadioShack was the neighborhood electronics authority for a century, the store where you bought your first soldering iron, your batteries, your CB radio.","fall":"Then it tried to be everything: phones, kiosks inside other stores, own-brand gadgets, services, random accessories, a brand extension free-for-all that erased what it WAS in the customer's mind. By the time it realized, Amazon owned 'electronics' and Best Buy owned 'stores,' and RadioShack owned nothing. Bankruptcy in 2015; a zombie second bankruptcy by 2017; nearly all stores shuttered."},"google-plus":{"origin":"In 2011, Google, which had already stumbled at social with Buzz and a fading Orkut, launched Google+ with circles, hangouts and +1s, built by a team of hundreds under Vic Gundotra. Facebook put its whole company on lockdown. Google+ had 10 million users in two weeks and the internet's biggest company behind it.","fall":"In 2011 Google decided it had to beat Facebook, and built Google+, forcing its way into every Google product with the 'Google+ integration' hammer. Users were dragged in by the hundreds of millions, but they weren't choosing it; they were being marched through it. Engagement was a desert: the average session was seconds. Google poured billions into a product that had no reason to exist, because 'social' as a position in people's minds was already owned by Facebook. Google+ limped on for eight years before Google quietly pulled the plug in 2019."},"zune-player":{"origin":"By 2006, Microsoft, the company that owned the PC, was watching Apple own music: the iPod had 75% of players, iTunes 80% of downloads. So it launched Zune: a 30GB player with a bigger screen, FM radio, wireless song-sharing ('squirting'), and its own music subscription, red, green, brown and pink hardware.","fall":"In 2006 Microsoft looked at the iPod, which had effectively owned 'music player' in the world's mind for five years, and decided to compete head-on with a me-too device, the Zune. It wasn't bad; it was late and indistinguishable. Microsoft outspent everyone but offered nothing the mind could file under 'different': same mp3, same white earbuds era, slightly clunkier software. By 2011 Microsoft killed the Zune brand entirely, the 'iPod killer' became a footnote, and Microsoft never seriously returned to consumer music."},"therac-25":{"origin":"The Therac-25 was a radiation therapy machine that seemed safer than its predecessors because its safety was implemented in software instead of hardware interlocks.","fall":"But when the software hit a rare race condition, the machine delivered massive overdoses of radiation, silently, and repeatedly. Over two years, six patients were over-irradiated; four died. The horrifying part: the machine's logs showed 'no malfunction' even as patients screamed, because the software recorded normal operation while the hardware delivered death. The investigation found a system designed by brilliance and tested by assumption, with no independent verification and no checklists."},"ge-immelt":{"origin":"In 2000, GE was the most valuable company on earth, $600 billion, the gold standard of management.","fall":"Then two decades of 'financial engineering over industrial discipline': GE Capital grew into a shadow bank nobody understood, acquisitions stacked on acquisitions, and leadership chased earnings per share with buybacks instead of building the businesses. When the 2008 crisis hit the shadow bank and the accounting games unwound, the empire decayed: write-downs, dividend cuts, a fraud settlement, and in 2018 the stock lost half its value in a year. In 2024, GE, the company that defined American business, split into three."},"mc-hammer-broke":{"origin":"MC Hammer was the biggest pop star on earth in 1990, 'U Can't Touch This' sold 10 million albums, and the money poured in: $33 million at his peak, houses, jets, a 200-person entourage (with salaries), 17 cars, staff for everything. The spending was the strategy.","fall":"When the hits stopped, as they always do, the empire had no buffer: he filed for bankruptcy in 1996 with $13 million in debt. The man who earned like a corporation spent like a government and saved like neither."},"bunker-hunt":{"origin":"Nelson Bunker Hunt and his brother William inherited one of America's great oil fortunes, and decided they could control the silver market. Throughout the 1970s they quietly bought silver by the boatload, amassing over 100 million ounces, roughly half the world's tradable supply. The price exploded from $6 to $50 an ounce.","fall":"Then the bubble logic turned: the exchange changed its rules, margin calls came, and the corner collapsed, silver crashed from $50 to $11 in a matter of days. The Hunts, leveraged to their eyeballs, lost over a billion dollars of their own money and filed for bankruptcy."},"salad-oil-scandal":{"origin":"Tino De Angelis built Allied Crude Vegetable Oil Refining, and claimed to hold hundreds of millions of dollars of soybean oil in tanks in New Jersey. The collateral was verified... sort of. What the auditors missed: the tanks contained mostly water, with a thin layer of oil on top; some 'full' tanks were secretly connected by pipes so the same oil could be measured twice.","fall":"When the fraud finally collapsed in 1963, the losses cascaded: a brokerage went under, banks took billions (today's dollars) in hits, and the 'verification' system that trusted a few men's word about some tanks was exposed as theater."},"99-cents-only":{"origin":"For 42 years, 99 Cents Only Stores built a beloved, simple promise: everything for 99 cents.","fall":"Then inflation arrived, and broke the promise. When costs rose, the company couldn't raise prices without betraying its own name, and it couldn't keep selling everything at 99 cents without bleeding. Management tried every lever: shrinkflation, private labels, a new format, but the anchor held the brand hostage. In April 2024, the chain announced it was closing all 377 stores and liquidating everything. The price was the brand; the brand was the trap."},"casper-mattress":{"origin":"In 2014, five startup founders, none of whom had sold a mattress, launched Casper: one perfect mattress in a wine-purple box, shipped free to your door with a 100-night trial. Raised $13 million in the year's fastest VC round; sold $1 million of mattresses in 28 days; the 'bed-in-a-box' land rush began.","fall":"Casper was the poster child of the direct-to-consumer revolution: a mattress in a box, brilliant branding, 'the Warby Parker of sleep.' Venture money poured in; the valuation hit $1.1 billion. The problem was the business underneath the brand: mattresses are a rare purchase (once a decade), returns are brutal, and every dollar of marketing bought a customer who might never return. The IPO priced at $12 against a target of $17; within a year, private equity bought the whole company for $320 million, a third of the 'unicorn' valuation, after years of losses that the brand could never outrun."},"trench-warfare":{"origin":"Every general in 1914 planned a war of movement, swift, Napoleonic, glorious. Within months, the machine guns and artillery turned it into a war of static trenches, where both sides were perfectly matched in a game neither could win.","fall":"Yet the generals kept playing the same move: mass frontal assault. The Somme's first day: 57,000 British casualties for a few hundred meters. The lesson should have been 'change the game', instead they repeated it for four years, because every general was playing a strategy that worked in the last war."},"sega-console":{"origin":"In the early 90s, Sega was the coolest company in gaming, Genesis vs. Super Nintendo, and Sega was winning: 60% of the US 16-bit market.","fall":"Then the strategic fog rolled in: multiple hardware platforms (32X, Saturn, Game Gear) that confused retailers and developers; a Saturn launch that enraged partners; and an obsession with beating Nintendo at its own game instead of defining a new one. Sony's PlayStation, with a clear strategy, developer-friendly tools and focus, walked past both. Sega's Dreamcast (1999) was genuinely brilliant, but the money and trust were gone; Sega exited hardware in 2001, a decade after owning the market."},"evergrande":{"origin":"Evergrande was China's largest property developer, a symbol of the boom that built cities overnight. Its model: borrow endlessly, build endlessly, sell presale apartments, borrow more.","fall":"When Beijing cracked down on developer debt and home sales froze, the music stopped: Evergrande owed over $300 billion, missed its bond payments, and began the largest default in financial history. Construction sites across China went silent with half-sold towers; the company that once topped the world's developers became a monument to leverage."},"first-republic":{"origin":"First Republic served wealthy clients with jumbo mortgages and low-rate loans, funded by uninsured deposits, a model that looked brilliant while rates stayed low.","fall":"When the Fed raised rates and Silicon Valley Bank collapsed, wealthy depositors, the bank's own customers, panicked and pulled $100 billion in weeks. Unlike SVB, First Republic got rescue deposits, but the run kept coming. In May 2023 regulators seized it and sold it to JPMorgan, the second-largest bank failure in US history, felled not by bad loans but by fragile funding."},"dhfl-scam":{"origin":"DHFL (Dewan Housing Finance) was India's second-largest housing finance company, a trusted lender to millions of middle-class homebuyers. Behind the trust, promoters allegedly siphoned tens of thousands of crores through shell companies, routing deposits into their own firms.","fall":"When the IL&FS crisis froze India's shadow-banking market, DHFL's rot surfaced: rating downgrades, a default, and finally a CBI investigation that revealed the largest housing-finance fraud in Indian history. Lakhs of investors and homebuyers were left holding promises."},"pmc-bank":{"origin":"In 1984, Punjab & Maharashtra Cooperative Bank was founded in a Bombay suburb to serve small savers, a cooperative with 137 branches across six states and, crucially, the absolute trust of Mumbai's working middle class: teachers, policemen, railway families who kept every rupee of a lifetime in it.","fall":"Punjab & Maharashtra Cooperative Bank looked like the safest thing in India, a small cooperative bank, government-insured, trusted by working-class depositors. In 2019, regulators discovered the horror: over 73% of the bank's loans, ₹4,355 crore, had gone to one real-estate company, hidden through thousands of fake accounts over years. The bank had 1.2 million depositors, many with life savings over the insured limit. Withdrawals were capped at ₹1,000; people queued for months to get their own money. The 'safest' bank in the neighborhood was the biggest fraud."},"ilfs-crisis":{"origin":"In 1987, India's central bank and a consortium of giants, HDFC among them, founded IL&FS to finance the nation's infrastructure: roads through Gujarat, water plants, power lines, city metros. Over three decades it became the 'shadow RBI' of project finance, a $17 billion balance sheet rated AAA, safe as India itself.","fall":"IL&FS was India's infrastructure financing giant, the lender behind roads, ports and power projects, rated AAA by every agency. In 2018 it defaulted on a small commercial paper, and the truth cascaded: ₹91,000 crore of debt, a labyrinth of 348 subsidiaries, and governance that let management hide losses for years. The default froze India's entire shadow-banking system, mutual funds holding IL&FS paper froze redemptions, other NBFCs got starved of credit, and the economy felt the chill for years. One AAA-rated company's rot froze the country's lending."},"videocon-empire":{"origin":"Venugopal Dhoot built Videocon from a small TV maker into India's most iconic consumer-electronics brand, the name in every middle-class living room.","fall":"Then ambition outran the business: he borrowed globally (including a controversial $800M loan from a subsidiary of another Dhoot firm) to expand into oil fields, telecom, and everything. When commodity prices fell and repayments came due, the empire, levered to the eyeballs, collapsed into India's insolvency courts in 2018. The brand that defined Indian electronics died under the debt that was supposed to make it bigger."},"future-group":{"origin":"Kishore Biyani was India's retail pioneer, he built Big Bazaar, Pantaloons and Central, the stores that changed how Indians shopped. At its peak the Future Group was India's largest retailer with 1,700 stores.","fall":"But expansion was financed with debt, and the pandemic was the final blow: stores closed, sales vanished, and creditors came calling. The ₹29,000-crore debt forced a fire-sale of the empire to Reliance, then a bitter legal battle over the deal. The man who taught India retail lost his company to the debt that built it."},"gofirst-airline":{"origin":"GoFirst (formerly GoAir) was the Wadia family's airline, a low-cost carrier that flew millions of Indians. Its collapse was fast and dramatic: in May 2023, it stopped flying overnight, blaming Pratt & Whitney engine failures that grounded half its fleet.","fall":"But the real story was financial: ₹11,000 crore owed to lessors, banks and vendors, mounting losses, and a failed attempt to blame the crisis on engines. Lessors repossessed planes within days; thousands of employees and passengers were stranded. The engine problem was real, the survival plan was not."},"zee-debt":{"origin":"Subhash Chandra built Zee from a tiny packaging business into India's first private TV network and a media empire, Zee TV, Zee News, an entire broadcasting dynasty.","fall":"But his holding company Essel Group borrowed massively against Zee shares to fund non-media bets. When markets fell, lenders invoked pledges and sold the shares. To save the empire from insolvency, Chandra was forced to sell his stake, the founder who created Indian private television ended his reign watching his own company slip away, all because personal debt was bigger than the crown jewel it was pledged against."},"google-stadia":{"origin":"In 2019, Google, which owned YouTube, Android and the world's data centres, announced Stadia at the Game Developers Conference: AAA games streamed from the cloud to any screen, no console, no download. Phil Harrison (ex-PlayStation) led it; Jade Raymond (Assassin's Creed) built first-party studios in Montreal and Los Angeles.","fall":"Stadia was Google's moonshot to stream AAA games with zero console, 'the Netflix of gaming', launched in 2019 with massive marketing. The tech worked impressively. The market didn't: pricing was confusing, the game library was thin, and Google's famous fear of commitment made studios hesitate to build for a platform that might vanish. In 2023 Google shut it down, refunding hardware, and left the industry with a lesson: even Google can't buy a market it doesn't understand. The controller-shaped tombstone of cloud gaming."},"windows-phone":{"origin":"In 2007, Microsoft owned the mobile OS market, Windows Mobile had over 50% share before the iPhone.","fall":"Then a decade of missteps: a botched transition, an app ecosystem developers ignored, and a strategy that kept changing direction. Windows Phone launched to critical praise (the tile UI was genuinely good) but never cracked 3%, and by 2017 Microsoft had killed it, exiting mobile entirely. The company that invented the smartphone platform category watched Apple and Google take everything."},"fire-phone":{"origin":"In June 2013, Amazon, the everything store, flush from two decades of retail conquest, unveiled its first smartphone at a Seattle launch with fireworks and OK Go. The Fire Phone's trick was 'Dynamic Perspective': four front cameras tracking your face for 3-D effects, plus Mayday live support and a year of Prime included.","fall":"Amazon decided to conquer the smartphone, and the Fire Phone was Bezos's personal project: a device with four cameras and 'Dynamic Perspective' 3D effects, priced higher than the iPhone. The problem: nobody asked for 3D gimmicks, the app store was thin, and the price screamed hubris. It flopped so hard that Amazon wrote off $170 million in unsold inventory and killed the project within a year. The richest company's customer obsession, its core principle, was abandoned for a technology demo."},"note7-fires":{"origin":"The Galaxy Note 7 was Samsung's flagship, a masterpiece that reviewers loved.","fall":"Then phones started catching fire. Samsung first recalled 2.5 million units, replaced them with 'safe' phones, which also caught fire. The replacement phones had the same flaw: rushed battery design in a race to beat Apple. Samsung recalled everything, destroyed 2.5 million devices, and lost $17 billion. The company survived, and its subsequent apology-and-fix culture became a case study, but the Note 7 remains the most expensive recall in tech history."},"groupon-collapse":{"origin":"Groupon invented daily deals, and grew at a pace no company had ever matched: $500M revenue in two years, a $6B Google acquisition offer turned down, and a 2011 IPO that valued it at $16B.","fall":"Then the model's cracks showed: merchants hated the deal economics (50% cuts, one-time customers), competition cloned the idea, and growth stalled. The stock collapsed over years to under $100M, a 99% loss. The fastest-growing company in history became the fastest lesson in fake economics."},"wish-collapse":{"origin":"Wish was the viral shopping app of the 2010s, $1 sunglasses, instant gratification, the app everyone joked about but millions used. At its 2020 IPO it hit a $140 billion valuation.","fall":"Then reality: cheap products meant quality disasters, repeat customers evaporated, shipping times ballooned, and TikTok Shop plus Temu cloned the playbook with more money. Wish's stock collapsed from $32 to $0.13, a 99.99% loss, and in 2023 it was sold for scraps. The 'cheapest' brand had no defense when cheaper arrived."},"tumblr-fire-sale":{"origin":"Tumblr was the cultural internet of the 2010s, 500 million blogs, the home of fandoms, art and internet culture. Yahoo bought it in 2013 for $1.1 billion.","fall":"Then the mismanagement classic: Yahoo forced ad-riddled redesigns that alienated users, and in 2018 the parent company (Verizon by then) banned adult content, the platform's most distinctive community, causing traffic to crash 30% in weeks. In 2019, Verizon sold Tumblr for $3 million, 0.3% of what Yahoo paid. The internet's cultural home became corporate real estate that got flipped into a loss."},"bitconnect":{"origin":"In 2016, BitConnect launched a proposition the crypto-curious couldn't ignore: lend your Bitcoin to its 'volatility trading bot' and earn 1% a day, roughly 3,700% a year, plus bonuses for recruiting. Ponzi was a word nobody at the packing-house conferences in Pattaya and Pune was allowed to say.","fall":"BitConnect promised investors up to 40% monthly returns through a 'trading bot', a return that would turn ₹100 into a crore in two years. The pitch was so absurd that the crypto community mocked it endlessly ('BitConnect!') while millions still poured money in, especially in India where it ran massive seminars. The inevitable happened: in 2018 the price crashed 92% in a day, the exchange shut down, and the founders vanished. The US SEC later charged it as a Ponzi scheme. The joke was on everyone who ignored the joke."},"quadriga-lost-keys":{"origin":"In 2013, 23-year-old Gerald Cotten and a partner founded QuadrigaCX in Vancouver, Canada's largest Bitcoin exchange, trading 95% of the country's crypto. Cold storage kept the reserves offline in encrypted wallets. Cotten controlled the private keys; he shared them with no one, not even his wife.","fall":"QuadrigaCX was Canada's largest crypto exchange, and its 30-year-old CEO Gerald Cotten was the only custodian of the private keys to customer funds, $190 million worth. In December 2018, Cotten died suddenly in India of complications from Crohn's disease. The exchange collapsed: no one could access the cold wallets, and audits later suggested the exchange may have been trading customer funds without reserves all along. 115,000 customers lost their money, literally locked behind keys that died with one man. The crypto exchange that trusted everything to a single point of failure."},"byjus-edtech":{"origin":"Byju's was the poster child of Indian startups, a teacher from Kerala building a $22 billion edtech empire, backed by every marquee investor, with celebrity ads on every cricket match. The model: sell expensive online courses with aggressive sales calls and huge marketing. It worked while money was cheap.","fall":"When funding dried up in 2022, the cracks appeared: financial statements delayed, auditors quitting, a $1.2B loan default, and an acquisition spree (WhiteHat Jr, Aakash, Epic) that added billions of debt. By 2023-24 the company was effectively worthless, employees unpaid, investors writing off 100%, founders gone. The fastest rise and fall in Indian startup history."},"snapdeal-war":{"origin":"In 2015-16, Snapdeal was India's #3 e-commerce giant, Kunal Bahl and Rohit Bansal had raised over $1.6B and were locked in a brutal war with Flipkart and Amazon.","fall":"Then the money ran out and the board pushed a merger with Flipkart. Bahl refused, famously telling the board to 'back off'. The company's response was to shrink: massive layoffs (80% of staff), abandoning categories, retreating to a niche of unbranded value products. But the war had already been lost, the market had moved on. By 2017-19 the once-$6.5B company was effectively sold for scraps. Refusing the merger saved control and destroyed the company."},"dunzo-collapse":{"origin":"Dunzo started as a WhatsApp-based errand service in Bengaluru and became the city's favourite way to get anything delivered, from chai to groceries to forgotten chargers. It raised hundreds of millions from Google and others, valued near $775M.","fall":"But the economics were brutal: every delivery was subsidized, and quick-commerce giants (Zepto, Blinkit, Swiggy Instamart) entered the same space with deeper pockets. When funding froze in 2022-23, Dunzo couldn't pay salaries, vendors or even its own delivery partners. The app that delivered everything finally delivered layoffs to 80% of its staff, a beloved brand killed by unit economics."},"gomechanic-fraud":{"origin":"GoMechanic was a Gurugram car-service startup, connect customers with garages, ride the car-repair wave. It claimed to service 1 million+ cars with 400+ partner garages, raising over $100M from Sequoia, Tiger and others at a valuation near $300M.","fall":"Then, in early 2023, during due diligence for a new funding round, the truth emerged: revenue had been fabricated. The founders publicly admitted it, the numbers were 'misreported'. The funding round collapsed, layoffs followed, and one of India's most-backed startups became a case study in fake metrics. Sequoia later wrote the investment down to zero."},"wework-india":{"origin":"WeWork's India arm rode the co-working wave hard, massive offices in Mumbai, Bengaluru, Gurugram, signing long-term leases with landlords while renting desks month-to-month to startups. The model looked genius in a boom: occupancy was high, the brand was cool, and corporates loved it.","fall":"Then the pandemic emptied the offices, and the mismatch revealed itself: WeWork owed landlords for 10-15 year leases while its tenants could cancel monthly. The global parent filed for bankruptcy in 2023 ($47B valuation to $44M), and the India entity, though operationally better, spent years restructuring leases and repricing. India's hottest office market couldn't save a model built on borrowed time."},"reliance-communications":{"origin":"In 2005, the Ambani brothers split Reliance, Anil got telecom (Reliance Communications), and it was a crown jewel: India's #2 telco with 100+ million subscribers.","fall":"But while his brother Mukesh built Jio from scratch, Anil's RCom borrowed aggressively to buy spectrum and expand, loading up debt while the industry was about to be disrupted. Then came 2016: Jio launched with free calls and data, and the entire industry's economics collapsed. RCom, drowning in debt, unable to compete, saw its subscriber base evaporate, its stock crash from ₹1,000+ to under ₹1, and finally filed for bankruptcy in 2019. An empire that was once worth billions was auctioned for scraps, and Anil Ambani went from the world's 6th richest man to a man selling his private jet to pay debts."},"zomato-ipo":{"origin":"In July 2021, Zomato raised over ₹12,000 crore in one of India's most-hyped IPOs ever, oversubscribed 38 times as millions of retail investors rushed to own a piece of India's food-tech story. The company had never made a profit; it was losing money on every order, subsidizing food delivery to grow. The IPO priced the story, 'India's food revolution', at a valuation that assumed decades of growth with no clear path to profitability.","fall":"Then the music changed: funding winter, rising delivery costs, and competition from Swiggy. The stock fell roughly 60% within a year, and many small investors who bought the dream at the top watched their savings shrink. The food was hot; the numbers were not."},"shopclues":{"origin":"ShopClues launched in 2011 as India's first unicorn e-commerce company, a marketplace for small-town India selling everything from phone covers to sarees. It had 100,000 sellers and 60 million SKUs, and was valued at $1.1 billion in 2016.","fall":"But the model had no moat: Flipkart and Amazon fought for the same customers with deeper pockets and faster logistics, while ShopClues bled cash on discount wars it couldn't sustain. After failed merger talks with Snapdeal and a fire-sale attempt, it was acquired by Singapore's Qoo10 in 2020 for a token sum, a company once worth a billion dollars changing hands for what amounted to loose change, with most of its 1,000+ employees gone years earlier."},"vedantu":{"origin":"Vedantu was founded in 2014 as a live online tutoring platform, rode the COVID lockdown boom to unicorn status in 2021 with a $1 billion valuation, and raised over $250 million from marquee investors.","fall":"When schools reopened, demand collapsed, the 'new normal' turned out to be temporary. The company pivoted hard, burning millions on celebrity-led shows and offline centres it couldn't fill. By 2023 it had shut its live classes business, laid off the vast majority of its workforce and was surviving on a fraction of its former revenue, its valuation effectively written down to near zero in investor books."},"oyo-hotels":{"origin":"OYO was India's fastest-growing startup story, Ritesh Agarwal's Airbnb-style hotel aggregator that went from a 19-year-old's dorm-room idea to a $10 billion global giant backed by SoftBank, with 100,000+ hotels across 80 countries. The pitch was irresistible: unbranded hotels get bookings, app, and quality standards. The reality: OYO paid property owners guaranteed rents even when rooms went empty, and booked revenue on rooms it never collected.","fall":"When COVID hit, the guaranteed-rent model collapsed, OYO shut thousands of hotels, and repeated audits found much of its 'inventory' was ghost hotels or double-counted. The valuation slid to ~$2.4B, losses crossed ₹21,000 crore, and its IPO filing revealed how thin the real economics were under the growth story."},"paisabazaar":{"origin":"PaisaBazaar was India's largest online lending marketplace, 'the Loan Marketplace' that promised to compare credit cards, personal loans and insurance across every bank. Parent company PolicyBazaar rode it to a $3 billion valuation at its 2021 peak.","fall":"But PaisaBazaar's economics never worked: it earned thin commissions while spending heavily on ads and discounts to acquire customers, and a flood of fintech rivals (cred, Groww, Jupiter, banks' own apps) ate the same customers with cheaper distribution. After years of mounting losses and failed attempts to turn profitable, parent PB Fintech wrote down PaisaBazaar's value by over 95% in 2024, effectively shutting its lending ambitions and gutting its team."},"housing-com":{"origin":"In 2012, twelve IIT Bombay graduates led by Rahul Yadav built Housing.com, India's most beautiful property portal: verified listings, data-viz maps, 3-D walkthroughs. SoftBank led the rounds, the founders became cover celebrities, and ₹700 crore went up in an advertising blitz remembered to this day.","fall":"Housing.com raised eyebrows and money in equal measure, a real-estate portal founded by IIT Bombay graduates that raised $90 million at a $900 million valuation in 2015, backed by SoftBank, with a legendary ₹700 crore advertising blitz on its way. The 26-year-old CEO was fired in a public boardroom drama within months. The startup spent recklessly, a ₹100 crore Super Bowl-adjacent marketing campaign was rumoured, lavish offices, and a 'free listings forever' model that meant it never charged the brokers who actually create real-estate listings. By 2020, after years of losses and a failed merger with PropTiger, it was sold for a token sum, investors who put in hundreds of crores recovered a fraction of a percent."},"pepperfry":{"origin":"Pepperfry was India's furniture e-commerce pioneer, founded in 2011, it built its own supply chain, warehouses, and a network of experience studios, and claimed a $2 billion valuation trajectory as one of India's first profitable-path e-commerce stories.","fall":"But furniture is a brutal category: high delivery costs, high returns, low repeat purchase, and heavy competition from Amazon, Flipkart, IKEA and local furniture hubs. Despite over $200 million raised, Pepperfry never achieved the scale to win, accumulated ₹400+ crore in losses, and by 2024 was acquired by a rival (Fynd-owned parent) at a fraction of its claimed valuation, the once-celebrated 'Pepperfry story' reduced to an integration footnote."},"voonik":{"origin":"Voonik was one of the most-hyped fashion e-commerce startups of the mid-2010s, 'the fashion app for India', raising over $20 million from top VCs and expanding into five vertical apps (Voonik, Voonik Men, Picksilk, Vilara, Zivame took a stake). It aimed to personalize fashion shopping for millions of Indians.","fall":"But the market was already owned: Myntra, Flipkart and Amazon had the inventory, logistics and discounts. Voonik's personalization was a nice feature, not a reason to switch. After burning through millions on cash-burn discounts, a failed pivot to B2B, and salaries going unpaid, the company shut down in 2020, leaving a trail of unpaid sellers and employees, a cautionary footnote in Indian e-commerce history."},"juul":{"origin":"Juul was supposed to be the salvation of smokers: a sleek e-cigarette that delivered nicotine without the tar, valued at $38 billion in 2018, the fastest company ever to reach that figure, with 75% of the US e-cigarette market.","fall":"But Juul's marketing and flavors (mango, mint, creme) made it irresistible to teenagers, by 2019, one in four US high school students used e-cigarettes, and Juul was the cause célèbre. Regulators banned flavored pods, states sued for billions, the FDA moved to take products off the market, and retailers from Walmart to CVS dropped it. The $38 billion valuation collapsed to nearly zero; the company cut 95% of staff, exited most markets, and accepted a $1.7 billion settlement. The fastest unicorn in history became the fastest meltdown in history."},"silk-road":{"origin":"In February 2011, 26-year-old idealist Ross Ulbricht launched Silk Road, a darknet market on Tor that accepted only Bitcoin, his stated rules banned stolen goods and anything harming children. Under the alias 'Dread Pirate Roberts', he blogged libertarian philosophy by day and arbitrated drug deals by night.","fall":"Silk Road was the first great dark-web marketplace, an anonymous bazaar on Tor where buyers and sellers traded drugs, forged documents and hacking tools using Bitcoin, under the alias 'Dread Pirate Roberts'. At its peak it processed $1.2 billion in sales, with a commission structure that made its founder, Ross Ulbricht, a multimillionaire in Bitcoin. The FBI eventually caught Ulbricht through a trail of operational slips: forum posts from his real identity, an early post asking a programming question, and a laptop seized while he was logged into the site as an administrator. In 2015 he was sentenced to double life plus 40 years without parole. The site itself was seized and later resurrected by copycats that were themselves seized."},"cryptopia":{"origin":"In 2014, two Christchurch brothers launched Cryptopia in New Zealand, a friendly little exchange listing 900+ tokens the big exchanges wouldn't touch, from Dogecoin to obscure community coins. By 2018 it had 2.5 million users worldwide and held the savings of ordinary crypto holders across 170 countries.","fall":"Cryptopia was a New Zealand-based crypto exchange founded in 2014, once ranked among the top 10 exchanges globally by volume. In January 2019, hackers drained a massive portion of its Ethereum holdings, later estimated at over $400 million at peak prices, through what investigators described as a months-long, sophisticated attack. The exchange suspended trading, tried to recover, but never regained user trust; thousands of users' funds were stuck in limbo for years as liquidators fought over what remained. By 2020 Cryptopia was in liquidation, its founder's crypto vanished into mixers, and the case became a textbook example of how a single security failure can end an entire business, and how little recourse users have when their 'bank' is a startup with a server."},"blockfi":{"origin":"BlockFi was one of the biggest names in crypto lending, users deposited Bitcoin and earned high interest rates, and BlockFi lent the crypto out to institutions and hedge funds. It was valued at nearly $5 billion in 2021, with $18 billion in assets under management, backed by firms like Bain and Tiger Global. The model was a giant, unregulated margin loan: BlockFi lent aggressively to risky counterparties, including Alameda Research, FTX's sister firm, and pledged collateral that was itself crypto.","fall":"When FTX collapsed in November 2022, the contagion hit BlockFi instantly: its exposure to Alameda and the falling prices of its collateral wiped it out within weeks. BlockFi filed for bankruptcy days later, users' withdrawal requests frozen, and billions in customer funds were locked in a years-long legal battle."},"avon":{"origin":"Avon was the pioneer of door-to-door selling, 6 million independent representatives worldwide, famous for the 'Ding-Dong, Avon Calling' jingle, and by the 1970s the world's largest cosmetics company with over $12 billion in peak revenue.","fall":"But the direct-selling model, door-to-door reps, printed brochures, home parties, was the 20th century's answer to distribution. The 21st century brought Instagram, e-commerce and beauty influencers who could reach more customers in a day than a rep could in a year. Avon's US operations suffered scandal over bribing foreign officials (a $135 million SEC settlement) and a failed merger with rival Nu Skin. Sold to Natura, then split off, its US arm filed for bankruptcy in 2024 with over $1.2 billion in debt, the iconic brand reduced to a cautionary tale about distribution models that outlive their era."},"spirit-airlines":{"origin":"Spirit Airlines built the most extreme ultra-low-cost model in American aviation: bare-bones fares, fees for everything from carry-on bags to water, and a fleet of uniform Airbus jets kept cheap through volume. For years it worked, Spirit was profitable and the model was so feared that United and Delta created their own 'basic economy' to compete.","fall":"But the pandemic and its aftermath broke the model: pilot costs soared, Spirit's Pratt & Whitney engines had to be grounded, and its planned merger with JetBlue was blocked by the US government in 2024. With $3.8 billion in debt and losses mounting, Spirit filed for Chapter 11 bankruptcy in November 2024, the first major US airline bankruptcy in over a decade, its stock going from $80 to under $1."},"lumosity-fine":{"origin":"In 2007, San Francisco startup Lumos Labs turned neuroscience headlines into games: brain training that promised to sharpen memory, attention and even delay decline, 'building your cognitive reserve', as its ads said, with a daily five-minute workout and a BPI score to show your progress.","fall":"Lumosity was the hottest brain-training startup of the 2010s, 70 million users, ads everywhere claiming its games would make you smarter, sharper and younger-brained. The company raised over $100 million and became the poster child for 'neuroplasticity at home'. The problem: the science never supported the marketing. Independent researchers repeatedly failed to find any transfer from Lumosity games to real cognitive ability, you got better at Lumosity, not at life. In 2016 the FTC fined the company $2 million and forced $50 million in customer refunds, ruling that its claims of preventing cognitive decline, dementia and Alzheimer's were unsubstantiated. The brand collapsed into obscurity almost overnight, and 'brain training' became a punchline for the entire wellness-tech industry."},"delvey-con":{"origin":"Anna Sorokin left a magazine internship in Moscow for London, then Paris, then New York, reinventing herself as 'Anna Delvey', a German heiress with a €60 million trust. She studied the Rich Kids of Instagram, tipped in hundred-dollar bills, and booked tables that didn't exist yet.","fall":"Anna Sorokin, posing as 'Anna Delvey', a German heiress with a $67 million trust fund, talked her way into New York's most exclusive circles between 2013 and 2017. She charmed bankers, private-jet brokers, hotel managers and art-world insiders with a flawless pitch: a private arts foundation, huge wire transfers 'on the way', and effortless confidence. She flew private, stayed in the most expensive hotels, and never paid, because the money never existed. Her presentation skills were so good that she nearly secured a $22 million loan from a major bank. The con collapsed only when she tried to cash a $100,000 cheque with insufficient funds. Convicted in 2019, she served years in prison, and her story became a Netflix show, proving she could still sell the story after the fraud was exposed."},"bay-of-pigs":{"origin":"In 1960, the CIA, fresh from overthrowing governments in Iran and Guatemala, trained 1,400 Cuban exiles in Guatemala for an invasion to topple Fidel Castro. Kennedy had just taken office; the plan presumed a spark of uprising, air cover kept deniable, and a quiet escape into the Escambray mountains if it failed.","fall":"The Bay of Pigs was a plan so obviously broken that almost everyone involved privately doubted it, and nobody said so. The CIA's plan to invade Cuba with 1,400 exiles assumed: local uprisings that never came, US air cover that was cancelled at the last minute, and a Cuban population ready to defect. Every assumption was wrong. President Kennedy's advisors, the brightest minds of their generation, the 'best and brightest', sat in rooms and nodded, each assuming someone else had the reservations they all privately held. Arthur Schlesinger, who opposed it, was told by Kennedy to keep his doubts to himself. The invasion was crushed in three days. The lesson became the textbook case of groupthink: when everyone suspects the plan is bad but nobody says it, the plan proceeds, brilliantly organised, unanimously endorsed, and catastrophically wrong."},"ravana-arrogance":{"origin":"Ravana was no ordinary villain: a scholar of the Vedas, a master of the veena, a devotee of Shiva so devoted that legend says he composed the Shiva Tandava Stotram. He had won the world through learning, austerity and power.","fall":"Then arrogance curdled his gifts: he took Sita believing he was entitled to her, dismissed every warning, from his own brother Vibhishana to the omens of his city, and refused to return her even when the cost was total war. Every advisor, every sage, every sign told him the same thing. His certainty was so complete that he burned his own kingdom rather than reconsider."},"duryodhana-envy":{"origin":"The Kauravas' eldest prince, Duryodhana, was born to a hundred brothers and one problem: the Pandavas, his cousins, outshone him at everything, Bhima's strength, Arjuna's archery, Yudhishthira's righteousness and claim to the throne. The people loved them; his father was blind; the palace whispers never stopped.","fall":"Duryodhana had everything: a throne waiting, a kingdom, a father who indulged him, and warriors like Bhishma and Karna at his side. What he could not bear was that his cousins had anything at all. Every Pandava success, the palace, the fame, the love of the people, felt like a theft from him. Envy turned neutral events into attacks and small grievances into existential wounds. His envy engineered the dice game that disgraced Draupadi, refused every compromise (Krishna offered him five villages, he demanded five fingers), and chose war over peace with such certainty that he told Krishna, 'I will not give them even a needle-point of land.' He won the war's first day and lost everything on the last."},"karna-loyalty":{"origin":"Karna was arguably the greatest warrior of the Mahabharata, born to Kunti, raised by a charioteer, cursed twice (by Parashurama for a lie, by a Brahmin for his pride), and gifted his divine armour away because a god asked. His flaw was not skill or courage: it was indiscriminate loyalty. He owed Duryodhana one kindness, being made a king when the world mocked his birth, and repaid it with everything, including his honour. He knew the war was wrong. He knew Krishna's counsel. He knew his brothers were on the other side.","fall":"But he had made his word his identity, and he chose the side that had chosen him, and died on it, his loyalty praised by everyone, and useful to no one."},"dasharath-vow":{"origin":"Dasharatha was a great king of Ayodhya who loved his son Rama more than his own life.","fall":"But he had made a promise decades earlier: to his queen Kaikeyi, for saving his life in battle, he had granted her two boons to be claimed whenever she chose. She chose the worst moment, on Rama's coronation eve, she demanded Rama's exile and her son Bharata's coronation. Dasharatha had the power, the army, the love of his people, and every moral right to refuse. He had one thing he valued more: his word. He kept the vow, exiled his beloved son, and died of grief within days, a kingdom intact, a father shattered, an heir exiled by the purity of a promise."},"daksha-yagna":{"origin":"Daksha, chief of the Prajapatis, father of Sati, held the grandest yagna of the age and invited every god, every sage, every king. He excluded one name: Shiva, his own son-in-law, the ascetic he considered beneathcivilisation. Sati went anyway, uninvited, to her father's fire.","fall":"Daksha, father of Sati and a patriarch of immense status, held the greatest yagna of his era, every god and sage invited. One guest was deliberately excluded: his son-in-law Shiva, whom Daksha despised as an unkempt ascetic unworthy of his daughter. The insult was public and deliberate. Sati, humiliated by her father's contempt for her husband, immolated herself at the ceremony. The grief and fury that followed destroyed the yagna, the guests fled, and Daksha lost his daughter and, briefly, his head, a sacrifice meant to bring blessings ended as a catastrophe of pride, all because the host refused a single invitation."},"sati-curse":{"origin":"Sati was the daughter of Daksha and the wife of Shiva, a woman who chose her love against her father's wishes, marrying the ascetic her family despised.","fall":"When Daksha held his great yagna and publicly insulted Shiva, Sati faced an impossible choice: her birth family's contempt versus her husband's honour. In the epic telling, she immolated herself in the sacrificial fire, an act of protest and grief that became the origin of the 'sati' tradition misremembered for centuries. Her death unleashed Shiva's grief-fueled destruction: the yagna shattered, Daksha humiliated, the world singed by a sorrow that had nowhere to go. A daughter's protest became a cosmic wound."},"samudra-manthan":{"origin":"When the gods lost their strength to a sage's curse, Vishnu prescribed the ultimate collaboration: churn the ocean of milk with the serpent Vasuki as rope and Mount Mandara as churning-staff, gods pulling one side, demons the other, until the nectar of immortality rose from the deep.","fall":"The devas (gods) and asuras (demons), sworn enemies, agreed to cooperate on the greatest project ever attempted: churning the cosmic ocean to extract the nectar of immortality. They used the mountain as a churn and the serpent as a rope, pulling together with everything they had. The churning worked, and the first thing it produced was halahala, a poison so potent it threatened to destroy creation itself. The alliance nearly killed everyone before the nectar ever emerged. Only Shiva's intervention, drinking the poison, saved the enterprise. The lesson was recorded in the mythology: cooperation produces results, but the results arrive in the order the ocean chooses, and the poison always comes first."},"bhasmasur":{"origin":"Bhasmasura performed ages of austerity in the fire until Lord Shiva appeared, pleased, and offered any boon. The asura asked for one: that whatever he placed his palm upon would burn to ash. Shiva granted it, and Bhasmasura, testing his gift, reached for Shiva's own head.","fall":"Bhasmasur was an ascetic who performed terrible penance to win a boon from Shiva. His request: the power to turn anyone to ash simply by placing his hand on their head. Shiva granted it. Bhasmasur's first instinct was to test his new power on the very god who had given it, he lunged at Shiva. The god fled, and only Vishnu's intervention (in the form of Mohini) saved him, tricking Bhasmasur into placing his own hand on his own head. The demon who had demanded absolute power was reduced to ashes by his own boon. The mythology is explicit: the man who asks for unlimited power without asking what it will do to him is writing his own ending."},"harappa-collapse":{"origin":"The Indus Valley (Harappan) civilization was the most advanced of its age: planned cities with drainage that rivals modern engineering, standardized weights and measures, writing, and trade spanning three thousand kilometres. For 700 years it flourished.","fall":"Then, around 1900 BCE, it collapsed, and the mystery is that there was no obvious enemy, no invasion, no fire. The leading evidence: a slow environmental decline, the monsoons weakened, the river systems shifted, the floodplains that fed millions became marginal. The cities kept their sophisticated infrastructure right up to the end, but the environment that the infrastructure depended on had quietly changed. A civilization that had perfected its systems never adapted its systems to the new reality, and the great cities were abandoned, their writing lost for four thousand years."},"plassey-betrayal":{"origin":"In June 1757, the Nawab of Bengal, Siraj-ud-Daulah, faced the East India Company at Plassey with an army of 50,000 against a few thousand soldiers. On paper, the battle was unwinnable for the Company.","fall":"But the Nawab's own general, Mir Jafar, had been secretly promised the throne in exchange for betraying him. When the battle began, Mir Jafar's army stood idle, the Nawab's forces collapsed in confusion, and Siraj fled and was later executed. Mir Jafar got his throne, and the Company got Bengal, the richest province in India, its treasury looted, its trade monopolised. Within a century, a trading firm ruled the subcontinent. The battle was less a war than a transaction: one man's ambition sold a nation's future, and the buyer paid with the country itself."},"nanda-tyrant":{"origin":"By 326 BC, Alexander the Great had crossed the Indus and defeated every king in his path, Porus at the Hydaspes, wounded but honoured. Ahead lay the plains of the Ganges and the Nanda Empire: 200,000 foot soldiers, 80,000 cavalry and 6,000 war elephants. His army, eight years from home, heard the numbers.","fall":"The Nanda dynasty ruled Magadha, the richest empire of ancient India, with an army numbering in the hundreds of thousands and treasuries so vast that even the later Mauryan conquerors marvelled at what they inherited. The Nandas were brilliant administrators but hated rulers: their taxes were crushing and their king, Dhana Nanda, was infamous for arrogance and parsimony. The legend that built the dynasty's grave: the young Chanakya, a Brahmin scholar, was humiliated at the Nanda court, thrown off the king's seat, insulted in public. He vowed to destroy the dynasty, and he did: he found a boy named Chandragupta, trained him, raised an army, and toppled the Nandas. The richest empire on earth fell not to a stronger army but to one humiliated scholar's 20-year promise."},"kurukshetra-war":{"origin":"The Pandavas asked only for five villages to end the succession war. Duryodhana refused them even land the size of a needle's tip, 'without war'. Krishna himself rode as peace envoy; courts gathered, gods watched, and even the blind king Dhritarashtra could have stopped it with one word.","fall":"The Mahabharata's war was not a surprise, it was a plan. Both sides spent years preparing, every warrior knew it was coming, and every elder, Bhishma, Drona, Vidura, Krishna, knew the price. The war lasted eighteen days and consumed the entire generation: the Pandavas won, and the victory was a funeral. Every woman in Hastinapur and Indraprastha became a widow; every warrior's family lost a son; the heirs, the five sons of the Pandavas and all hundred Kauravas, died. The victors inherited a kingdom of ashes and a guilt that never lifted. The epic's own arithmetic is the lesson: war is the only business where even the winners describe the outcome as defeat."},"sita-exile":{"origin":"Sita, after surviving abduction, war and the fire itself, returned to Ayodhya as its queen, beloved by her husband Rama, her people, and her own conscience.","fall":"Then a washerman's public remark about his own wife made Rama fear that his people doubted Sita's purity. He made the choice that haunts the epic: to protect his reputation as a king, he exiled his pregnant wife to the forest. Sita, who had chosen him over her kingdom, who had walked through fire for him, was abandoned, not for a crime, but for the fear of what people might say. She raised their sons alone in the hermitage and, in the later telling, refused to return, calling the earth itself to take her back. The king who could defeat an empire could not face a rumour."},"dice-game":{"origin":"The Pandavas, five rightful heirs, fresh from conquering half of India, were invited by their cousins to a festival game of dice in the brand-new golden hall built to shame them. Shakuni, the Kaurava uncle, carried dice carved from his father's bone that never rolled against him.","fall":"In the Mahabharata, the Kaurava court invited Yudhishthira, the eldest Pandava, famous for never lying, to a game of dice. Shakuni, the Kaurava uncle, threw the dice with loaded skill, and Yudhishthira, knowing the game was rigged, kept playing: first his wealth, then his kingdom, then his brothers, then himself. Finally he staked Draupadi, the wife he shared with his brothers, and lost her too. She was dragged into the court by her hair, and when she asked the assembled elders a single question, 'was I not free to speak?', the greatest warriors and teachers of the age sat silent, hiding behind rules. The game's outcome was reversed the same evening, but nothing was reversed: the insult was never undone, the silence of the elders was never forgotten, and the war that followed was rooted in that single night of a king who could not stop gambling."},"emergency-1975":{"origin":"On June 12, 1975, the Allahabad High Court found Prime Minister Indira Gandhi guilty of electoral malpractice and voided her 1971 election. Strikes and mass rallies led by Jayaprakash Narayan paralysed Gujarat and Bihar; the capital marched. On the night of June 25, the government cut power to Delhi's press.","fall":"On the night of 25 June 1975, Prime Minister Indira Gandhi declared a state of emergency, the Indian constitution, the world's longest, suspended; opposition leaders arrested in a single sweep; the press censored; forced sterilizations carried out in the name of population control. The Emergency lasted 21 months, and the justification, 'the nation is in danger', was the classic line of every autocrat in history. What happened next is the part worth studying: when elections were finally called in 1977, the Indian people, including the poor, the illiterate, the supposedly uninformed, voted the regime out in one of democracy's great vindications. The institutions bent but did not break; the press recovered its nerve; and the Emergency became a permanent scar that has disciplined every subsequent government. The system survived because the people remembered what it was for."},"rajneeshpuram":{"origin":"Rajneeshpuram was built in the Oregon desert in the 1980s: a utopian city for thousands of followers of the Indian guru Bhagwan Shree Rajneesh (Osho), designed to prove that a spiritual community could be self-sufficient, free and enlightened. What it became was the opposite: a fortress run by the guru's personal secretary, Ma Anand Sheela, with surveillance, weapons, and a secret plan to take over the local county by busing in homeless voters.","fall":"When the plan failed, the commune poisoned salad bars across Oregon, 751 people fell ill, two communities were devastated, to suppress voter turnout. The FBI descended, the commune collapsed in scandal, the guru was deported, and the 'utopia' became a synonym for spiritual authority without accountability. The movement that preached inner freedom had built a police state."},"syd-barrett":{"origin":"Syd Barrett was the original creative engine of Pink Floyd, the songwriter, the voice, the wild imagination that gave the band its name and its first hits. He wrote 'See Emily Play' and the psychedelic masterpiece 'Piper at the Gates of Dawn' before turning 21.","fall":"Then the intensity that powered his creativity turned inward: heavy LSD use, erratic behaviour, on-stage meltdowns where he played one chord for an hour or stood frozen. The band, his friends, had to choose between the genius and the machine; they chose the machine, and Barrett drifted into obscurity, living out his decades in his mother's house in Cambridge, painting and playing alone. Pink Floyd went on to sell 250 million records without him. The man who invented the band's sound died in 2006, remembered as much for the burn as for the brilliance."},"flappy-bird-removed":{"origin":"In 2013, Dong Nguyen, a 24-year-old Vietnamese developer in Hanoi, uploaded Flappy Bird, a free game he'd built in a weekend: tap, a yellow bird, green pipes. Four months later it topped charts in 100 countries, made $50,000 a day in ads, and was being blamed in US schools for ruining children's attention.","fall":"In 2014, a Vietnamese developer named Dong Nguyen released Flappy Bird, a simple, brutally difficult game where a pixel bird flaps through pipes. It became the most downloaded app on earth: 50 million downloads in a month, 90 million in total, and an estimated $50,000 a day in ad revenue. It was the purest product of the attention economy, designed to be impossible to put down, engineered to trigger the same dopamine loops as a slot machine. And then Nguyen did something unprecedented: he deleted it. 'Flappy Bird was designed to be a pleasant game for a few minutes,' he said, 'but it became an addictive product. I think it has become a problem. To solve that problem, it is best to take down Flappy Bird.' The game vanished overnight, and the creator, the richest man in his field for a week, chose his conscience over his cash."},"stanford-ponzi":{"origin":"R. Allen Stanford, Texan banker, knighted by Antigua, sponsor of cricket's richest match ($20 million a game), built Stanford International Bank on the Caribbean island of Antigua: CDs paying steady returns 2-3 points above American banks, 'safer than the stock market', audited by a 12-person Antiguan firm.","fall":"Sir Allen Stanford, knighted, celebrated, owner of cricket leagues and Caribbean banks, was the 'king of cricket' and one of the world's richest men. His Stanford International Bank promised investors spectacular, consistent returns on certificates of deposit, backed by a 'highly liquid, globally diversified' portfolio. For 20 years the money flowed in from 30,000 investors, many of them retirees in Latin America and the Caribbean who trusted the knighted billionaire with their life savings. The returns were a fiction: the 'diversified portfolio' was mostly real estate and an office tower, the returns were paid from new deposits, and the whole structure was a Ponzi scheme dressed in a cricket blazer. In 2009 the SEC caught up; Stanford was convicted of 13 counts of fraud and sentenced to 110 years. Thousands of elderly investors lost everything, and a system that was supposed to protect them had been charmed for two decades."},"subhiksha":{"origin":"Subhiksha was the miracle of Indian retail, founded by a dentist-turned-entrepreneur, R. Subramanian, it grew from one store in Chennai in 1997 to over 1,600 stores across India in a decade, promising 'lowest prices, always'. It was India's fastest-growing retail chain, and the media loved the story: a first-generation entrepreneur beating the big conglomerates with pure pricing aggression. The growth was funded by short-term debt and supplier credit, Subhiksha paid suppliers late and expanded into new stores with borrowed money.","fall":"When the 2008 credit crunch hit and the banks stopped rolling over loans, the whole structure froze within months: stores ran out of stock, suppliers stopped delivering, salaries stopped, and by 2009 the company was effectively dead, its assets sold for scrap."},"air-deccan":{"origin":"Captain G.R. Gopinath's Air Deccan was a genuine revolution: in 2003, he launched India's first low-cost airline, selling tickets at prices that let the 'common man' fly for the first time, famously selling some seats for ₹1. The airline grew explosively, forcing every legacy carrier to cut prices.","fall":"But the model had a fatal gap: Air Deccan was a low-cost airline without the low-cost discipline, a mixed fleet, an early-morning flight schedule that hurt yields, and thin operating margins that left no room for error. When losses mounted and capital was needed, Gopinath sold a stake to Kingfisher's Vijay Mallya, who merged the airlines and eventually grounded the brand. The revolution survived; the company didn't."},"aircel":{"origin":"In 1994, C. Sivasankaran, the 'Disruptor of Chennai' who'd started selling computers at 19? keep '...launched Aircel from Tamil Nadu and became the first operator to give the south cheap, cheerful coverage. Malaysia's Maxis bought a majority in 2006, and Aircel grew to 20 circles and 100 million subscribers, until the spectrum scandal and Reliance Jio's free data arrived.","fall":"Aircel was once one of India's most promising telecoms, launched in 1999, it grew to 100 million subscribers and was a serious player in the pre-Jio era. Its fatal move came in 2010: borrowing heavily to buy 3G spectrum, betting that revenues would grow fast enough to service the debt. The bet didn't survive contact with reality, tariffs crashed, competition intensified, and then Reliance Jio's 2016 entry collapsed data prices to near zero. Aircel's debt had ballooned to over ₹40,000 crore while its revenues shrank. In 2018, after failed merger attempts, the company filed for bankruptcy, 100 million subscribers were left scrambling, and one of India's oldest private telcos simply ceased to exist."},"hmt-watches":{"origin":"HMT (Hindustan Machine Tools) was once the watchmaker of India: from 1961, its watches were a national institution, the wedding gift, the retirement gift, the symbol of Indian self-reliance. For decades it had a near-monopoly, with waiting lists for its most popular models.","fall":"Then the world changed: quartz watches arrived from Japan, electronic watches from everywhere, and eventually mobile phones that made watches optional. HMT's response was decades of denial, it kept making mechanical watches, kept its bloated workforce, and kept refusing to accept that its monopoly was gone. Losses mounted year after year; governments propped it up through political cycles. Finally in 2016, after losing ₹20 crore a year, the government shut the watch division down. The brand that defined Indian time became a museum piece."},"srei-infra":{"origin":"SREI was a respected Indian infrastructure financier, a lender to construction companies, equipment buyers and road builders, listed on the stock exchanges for decades. Its collapse in 2021 was sudden and total: an RBI inspection found that a huge share of its loans had gone to a web of related parties, companies linked to its own promoters, with collateral that was largely overvalued or illusory.","fall":"When the RBI took over the board, the true picture emerged: the books showed a healthy lender; the reality was a system designed to move depositors' money to promoter-linked entities. Lenders who had trusted a 30-year-old institution were left with recoveries of a few paise per rupee, and thousands of small investors lost their savings."},"woolworths-uk":{"origin":"For 99 years, Woolworth's was a British high-street institution, 'nothing over sixpence' to a national chain of 807 stores selling everything from pick-and-mix sweets to children's clothes and music. Generations shopped there; it was a fixture of every British town.","fall":"But the fixture had stopped being a business: its product range was undifferentiated (anyone could sell the same items), its stores were ageing, and its online presence was nearly non-existent while competitors like Amazon and Argos digitised. The board, management and successive owners kept 'restructuring', new logos, new ranges, new owners, but never addressed the core: what was Woolworth's FOR in a world that no longer needed a general store? In November 2008, administration was announced; within days, all 807 stores closed and 27,000 people lost their jobs, the largest retail collapse in British history at the time."},"body-shop":{"origin":"Anita Roddick founded The Body Shop in 1976 and built it into a global pioneer of ethical beauty, cruelty-free products, fair trade, environmental activism, a brand with a soul that attracted millions of loyal customers. In 2006, Roddick sold it to L'Oréal for £652 million; she died a year later. What followed was two decades of ownership churn: L'Oréal, then Natura (Brazil), each promising to restore the magic.","fall":"But the magic had been Roddick herself, her activism, her story, her authenticity, and without her, the brand became just another cosmetics label competing in a market now flooded with 'clean' and 'ethical' brands that had copied its playbook. In 2023, the chain was sold for £1; in early 2024, it collapsed into administration, closing hundreds of stores and cutting thousands of jobs. The pioneer of ethical beauty was made obsolete by the very movement it created."},"bear-stearns":{"origin":"Founded in 1923, Bear Stearns was Wall Street's tough little outsider, the firm of traders and street fighters that survived the Depression without a loss? (myth-tinged but widely repeated), and by the 2000s, under Jimmy Cayne (a bridge champion who kept a cigar in the trading day), it was the Street's fifth-largest bank and the boldest player in mortgage bonds.","fall":"Bear Stearns was one of Wall Street's most storied investment banks, 85 years old, a survivor of the Depression, the wars and every crisis since, famous for its aggressive trading culture and its headquarters a block from the New York Stock Exchange. In March 2008, the unthinkable happened in a weekend: as mortgage-backed securities collapsed, clients fled, lenders pulled their lines, and the bank's cash evaporated within days. The Federal Reserve engineered a rescue, JPMorgan bought Bear for $2 a share, a price that valued the bank that had traded at $170 a year earlier at almost nothing. Employees who had been paid partly in stock watched their life savings vanish overnight. The 85-year-old bank that had survived everything died in 72 hours, not from a single mistake, but from the leverage that turned a liquidity problem into an extinction event."},"facebook-beacon":{"origin":"In November 2007, Facebook, 58 million users and two years into opening beyond colleges, launched Beacon, the dream of social advertising: buy a concert ticket, rent a movie, and your friends would see it automatically in their feeds, across 44 partner sites. No opt-in. No warning. Just share everything.","fall":"In November 2007 Facebook launched Beacon: when members made purchases at partner sites like Blockbuster and Overstock, the purchase was automatically 'beamed' into their friends' news feeds, no consent asked, no opt-in offered. Users discovered their private purchases (engagement rings, gifts, medical items) were being broadcast. MoveOn.org ran an ad campaign, privacy groups mobilised, a senator wrote to Facebook, and within weeks the company was apologising for 'a lot of mistakes.' A class action (Lane v. Facebook) settled for $9.5M in 2009, and Beacon was switched off. The design was brilliant for advertisers and catastrophic for trust, the prototype for every 'we'll ask forgiveness' default that followed."},"bikram-yoga":{"origin":"Bikram Choudhury built the most famous yoga franchise on earth: the same 26-posture sequence, the same 40°C room, and a legally enforced monopoly over its own method. At its peak, 650+ studios and millions of students swore by the practice.","fall":"But the franchise ran on the guru's ego: he sued 21 studios in 2011-12 for using 'his' sequence, and from 2013 former students began coming forward with harassment claims. In 2016 he was hit with a default judgment of more than $6.5M; in 2017 another warrant was issued in Los Angeles while he remained abroad. The same year, his yoga college filed for bankruptcy. The empire that sold 'yoga, copyrighted' collapsed when the practice's own ethics caught up with its business, the first limb of yoga is how you treat people, not the temperature of the room."},"toyota-recalls":{"origin":"Toyota was the world's most admired manufacturer, the company that invented kaizen, the Andon cord and just-in-time.","fall":"But between 2009 and 2011, over 10 million vehicles were recalled worldwide for sudden unintended acceleration; Toyota suspended US sales and production in January 2010, and Congress hauled executives to Washington. The U.S. government fined Toyota $16.375M and then a further $16.4M, the largest civil penalties of their kind at the time, and capped the episode with a $1.2B deferred-prosecution settlement in 2014. Independent studies (including NASA's) later found no electronic defect: the causes were floor-mat interference and pedal entrapment, but the deeper finding was that Toyota's growth had outrun its own culture. The company that invented 'stop the line' had kept the line running."},"ratings-fail":{"origin":"In the years before 2008, S&P, Moody's and Fitch stamped more than $3 trillion of subprime-mortgage securities with their highest rating: triple-A, the same grade as U.S. Treasury debt. The ratings were, in practice, paid for by the issuers whose products they rated; the analysts were judged on market share; and the models were fed with two years of housing data that could never see a nationwide decline.","fall":"When the music stopped, more than half of AAA-rated subprime securities defaulted or were downgraded to junk. The U.S. Department of Justice extracted $1.375B from S&P (2015) and $864M from Moody's (2017). The failure was not corruption alone, it was noise: the same judgment made by thousands of analysts, all wrong the same way, presented as an objective stamp."},"valeant-pharma":{"origin":"In 2010, activist investor Bill Ackman and CEO J. Michael Pearson, a former McKinsey partner who believed pharma's R&D was wasted money, took over Biovail and renamed it Valeant. The model: buy old drugs, fire the research, and raise prices as high as the market would bear. Wall Street called it brilliant for five straight years.","fall":"Valeant looked like the perfect 'quality' business on paper: spectacular margins, soaring returns on capital, and a stock that rose from $30 to $263. The engine: acquire old drugs, then raise prices relentlessly, 2,000% on some, 500% on others, plus a pharmacy (Philidor) used to push its own products. Bill Ackman of Pershing Square bought in at ~$200, calling it 'a wonderful business'; short-sellers called it fraud. In 2015-16 the truth arrived: the accounting was restated, the price hikes were politically radioactive, the debt was $30B, and the stock collapsed to under $10. Ackman eventually sold at ~$11, roughly a $4B loss with an 'I was wrong' that cost a fortune to say."},"purdue-opioids":{"origin":"Purdue Pharma launched OxyContin in 1996 with a claim its own executives knew was false: that the 12-hour pill was less addictive than other opioids. Sales reps were told to say the addiction rate was under 1%, and marketing targeted doctors with dining, trips and scripts. Sales hit $2.8B by 2001; addiction and overdose deaths climbed with them. In 2007, Purdue and three executives pleaded guilty to misbranding and paid $634M.","fall":"When states sued en masse, the company filed for bankruptcy in 2019; in 2020 it pleaded guilty again, this time $8.3B, and the Sackler family agreed to pay billions more. By then the opioid crisis had killed more than 200,000 Americans and was still counting."},"tobacco-denial":{"origin":"In 1950, the first major studies appeared in JAMA and BMJ linking smoking to lung cancer; by 1953, scientists had painted tar on mice and grown tumours. Cigarette sales were 58 billion a year in America, and the industry's executives, from RJR, Philip Morris, Brown & Williamson, Lorillard and the rest, met at New York's Plaza Hotel that December.","fall":"By 1953, the industry's own funded research had linked smoking to lung cancer, so the industry funded its own answer: the 'Frank Statement to the American Public' promised impartial science, then never delivered it. For decades, companies paid scientists, buried results and repeated one phrase like a prayer: 'more research is needed.' In 1964 the Surgeon General's report settled the science; the industry settled the law. In 1994, an archive of internal documents surfaced and proved executives knew about addiction and cancer all along. In 1998, 46 states signed the Master Settlement Agreement, $206 billion over 25 years. In 2006, a federal judge ruled in 1,600 pages that the industry had conspired to defraud the American public."},"sugar-industry":{"origin":"In the 1950s, American sugar consumption was under attack: heart-disease researchers, notably John Yudkin in Britain, were beginning to finger sugar, not fat, as the dietary villain. The Sugar Research Foundation, the industry's trade group, had money, connections to Harvard, and a problem to solve.","fall":"In 1967, the New England Journal of Medicine published a review by Harvard's Dr. Fred Hegsted, arguing that saturated fat, not sugar, was the main driver of coronary heart disease. In 2016, researchers at UCSF (Kearns et al.) revealed in JAMA Internal Medicine what was hidden in the archives: the review had been commissioned and paid for by the Sugar Research Foundation, which had also approved the manuscript's framing, the fat got the blame, the sugar got the cover. Similar sugar-funded 'calories are calories' papers kept circulating for decades. The result: a generation of dietary guidelines built on sponsored conclusions, and a metabolic crisis that is still compounding."}};
